1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === George Spivey
2 CONT * Son of George Spivey and Margaret (Matthews) Spivey
2 CONT * Brother of Ann Spivey, Matthew Spivey
2 CONT * Husband of Virginia (Hall) Spivey
2 CONT * Father of John Francis Spivey, Matthew William Spivey and James Angus Spivey
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spivey of Nansemond County, Virginia, and Chowan and Gates Counties, North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.geneajourney.com/spivey.html
2 CONT
2 CONT George Spivey b abt 1620, prob England, d Nansemond, VA. He md Margaret (poss Matthews) abt 1644. She was b abt 1622.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known children of George Spivey and Margaret were:
2 CONT
2 CONT # George Spivey b abt 1646, prob Nansemond, VA.
2 CONT # Matthew Spivey b abt 1650.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === Research Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT Death seen as 02/09/1742
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Spivey-361
2 CONT
2 CONT The Spivey family orginated in Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yorkshire is located very close to the southern border of Scotland, and the Spiveys may have emigrated from Scotland to England.
2 CONT
2 CONT This Spivey branch descended from the Spivies of Yorkshire, England at Barnsley ...
2 CONT
2 CONT .1 William Spivye, Sr., b: c1530 per Yorkshire achives
2 CONT ..2 William Spivie, Jr., b: c1525; m: Elizabeth 1583
2 CONT ...3 William Spivie, III, b: 1585
2 CONT ...3 Jane Spivie, b: 1588
2 CONT ...3 Esther Spivie, b: 1590
2 CONT ...3 Thomas Spivie, Sr., b: 1583 in Barnsley; m: 1606 Dorythie Escom
2 CONT ....4 Isabell Spivie, b: 1607
2 CONT ....4 John Spivie, b: 1610
2 CONT ....4 Elizabeth Spivie, b: 1613
2 CONT ....4 Ester Spivie, b: 1614
2 CONT ....4 Thomas Spivie, Jr., b: 1617
2 CONT ....4 George Adolphus Spivey, Sr., b: 1612; christened: 02 Dec 1612; m: Margaret Matthews
2 CONT .....5 Matthew Spivey
2 CONT .....5 George Spivey, Jr.
2 CONT By January 8, 1643 Thomas Dew, Gentleman had been granted 750 acres Upper Norfolk County, upon the eastward side of the southwestward branch of the Nansemond River, beginning at the mouth of Craney Creek opposite two small islands
2 CONC  called Craney and New Havenriver, and adjacent to Mr. Randell Crew. Of the 750 acres, 300 acres were Mr. Dew's by a former land patent and 450 acres were due to him for the transportation of nine persons, his own personal adventu
2 CONC re. Those nine people included ...
2 CONT
2 CONT William Broughton
2 CONT Robert Fleming
2 CONT Samuel Jeffery
2 CONT Henry Brown
2 CONT John Corne
2 CONT Elizabeth Tockwood
2 CONT George Adolphus Speevy (per transport list).
2 CONT In 1643 the above 9 were transported as indentured servants to Lower Norfolk County, Virginia by the Gentleman Thomas Dew, who was later, during 1646, sent by Governor Berkeley of Virginia on an expedition against the Indians alon
2 CONC g the Chowan River in North Carolina for the purpose of preparing the area for colonization.
2 CONT
2 CONT The record shows George Adolphus Spivey had at least two sons: George and Matthew. Neither of his sons nor his wife were listed on the transportation list. However, it is known that George Spivey, Jr. remained in England until 166
2 CONC 8, when he too finally immigrated to Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT If George Adolphus Spivey paid for Margret's and Matthew's passage, they would not necessarily have been listed, as the list only contained the nine people transportated by Thomas Dew.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is believed that George Adolphus Spivey's wife may have been Margaret Matthews, in view of the son who was named Matthew.
2 CONT
2 CONT During his servitude, George Adolphus Spivey was possibly the manager for a firm such as the "London Tea and Tobacco Shipping Company", or he may have been a farm manager for another such company with royal land grants along the J
2 CONC ames River in the Virginia Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT At age 40, George Adolphus Spivey was transferred into the Virginia Colony land grants to organize and supervise farming, along with other related operations for the new company. (These operations may have included clay pipe makin
2 CONC g, as a colonial-time ship was recently discovered off the Virginia coast. Explored and raised, the ship contained a load of pipes and other produce from America.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently, George Adolphus Spivey was located in Nansemond County - mostly in the Suffolk area, along the south bank of the James River and in southeast Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sometime between 1666 and 1679, George Spivey was also granted 150 acres in Nansemond County, due for the transportation of three persons ...
2 CONT
2 CONT Jno. Clay
2 CONT Margaret Jresh
2 CONT Will Patrick.
2 CONT George Adolphus Spivey was also granted land in Nansemond County, Virginia in 1681 (per Nugent).
2 CONT
2 CONT George Adolphus Spivey is the ancestor of most of the Spiveys in North Carolina, USA.
