1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Carol, you probably already know about all the following but just in case you have not found everything this is what I have in my notes about Edward Nelson, Sr., grandfather to Noah Nelson that married Ruth.  He was also the grand
2 CONC father to my Nancy Nelson that married William Henry Stricklin (my g-g-grandfather) and Elizabeth Nelson that married Thomas Stricklin (brother to William Henry).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Concerning Thomas and Elizabeth--please see the bottom of this email concerning notes that I have about them.  Things are still a little cloudy concerning Thomas and Elizabeth--there was another Stricklin family that lived in the 
2 CONC same vicinity that they did but I don't think that family is related and they ultimately left Alabama.  
2 CONT
2 CONT There was also another William Strickland/Stricklin family that came to Blount County about the same time my William Riley Stricklin did but they came out of Georgia.  I have not been able to find a kinship with this family but th
2 CONC e children of both William's have such similar names that make me think somewhere along the line there is a connection.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have never requested copies of Edward's Revolutionary Pension Application but it is possible there could be documentation of his Native American Heritage in that.
2 CONT
2 CONT When things calm down for you I would like for us to get together and discuss what could be worked out about the DNA testing.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Broderbund WFT Vol. 1, Ed. 1, Tree #2502, Date of Import: 18 Jul 1997. Blount County Heritage Book has a section on Marshall Morton, an early settler of Blount. He was apparently an Indian trader and lived close to the Nelson
2 CONC s. the family was also from the Carolinas and lived next to one of the Nelson families. Morton was the maiden name of William Nelson's wife, and the blurb in the Heritage book says that his daughter, "Sarah Morton, born 1804, Gree
2 CONC nville Dist, SC, died 13 Sept 1870 either in Alabama or Indian Territory. (Husband was William Nelson, son of Edward Nelson of the Cherokee Nation.) Article also says, "A mass exodus of pioneers from Orange North Carolina was divi
2 CONC ded in two directions - one to Tennessee and one to South Carolina. Marshall Morton, and at least two brothers, followed the latter route. Marshall established his home on the wilds of Twelve Mile River in what is known today as P
2 CONC endleton, SC. (Edward Nelson is also listed on 1790 Pendleon Co Census). Surrounding neighbors were a mixture of ethnic backgrounds and half breed indians." From Charles Shelton McCleskey, "Descendants of James and Joseph McCleske
2 CONC y, Soldiers of the American Revolution, The Texas Group", (Published Privately, 1977-78), p. 389.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Alabama Records, Vol. 204, p. 57 Blount County Compiled by Pauline Jones Gandrud: "Edward Nelson: Blount County, Alabama. August, 1833. Edward Nerlson makes declaration, is age 70; that he entered service in 1780 and served i
2 CONC n Regiment of Continental Line under Gen. Ugee and Major Hamilton.  At time of enlistment, he resided in Chatham County, North Carolina, marched to Congaree River in North Carolina and was there about 1 year.  Had later various to
2 CONC urs to near Charleston to mouth of Ashley River and camped there until 1783, and when the war was over he was sent home, he had enlisted for duration of the war.  He was a private, was in no battle, but was in skirmishes. Pension 
2 CONC was rejected."
2 CONT
2 CONT From DAR Book of Patriots for Blount County, Alabama, p. 448: Nelson, Edward (b.         /d. after 1840) Buried: Mt. Moriah Cem. in Blount Co., Govt. marker placed by Warrior Rivers chap, DAR, Blount Co. Mem. Commission. Service: 
2 CONC N.C.  Entered service in 1780 and served in Regt. of Cont. Line under Gen. Ugee and Maj. Hamilton.  Served under Gen. Daniel Morgan in the Carolina Campaign. Pension:  No. R7587.  Made declaration that he entered service in 1780, 
2 CONC resided in Chatham Co. N.C. at the time.  Marched to Congaree River in N.C. and was there about one year.  Served other tours.  When the war was over, he was sent home.  Pension rejected.  Reason unknown.   Family: Name of wife un
2 CONC known. Children: 1.  Edward Nelson Jr. 2.  William Nelson 3.  Matthew Nelson Ref:  Gandrud AL Rec. Vol. 204, p. 57; information submitted by Warrior Rivers Chap, DAR.
2 CONT
2 CONT I have "hear say" reports (from Prodigy Genealogy Bulletin Board) that Edward was half Cherokee Indian which is why he was denied a pension.  Supposedly this information can be found in the Alabama Archieves.  I have also seen an 
2 CONC account from someone that reported that he was denied a pension because of desertion (it was probably time to "put in a crop").    I have not  verified any of this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward Nelson, Blount County AL  Posted by: LisaMortonMills (ID *****1123) Date: November 07, 2006 at 15:20:03 of 10503   In recent months I've noticed that some Nelson researchers are claiming the Blount County, Alabama Edward Ne
2 CONC lson as the son of William Nelson of Craven NC who married Winifred Johnson. I recently found the following and believe this will clear up the discrepancy. The Edward Nelson of Blount County, AL was refused Reveloutionary War pens
2 CONC ion because he was half Cherokee, whereas the Edward Nelson son of William of Craven NC did obtain a pension. Anyway, the below indicates the proper name for Edward of Blount County and I thought researchers might be interested: P
2 CONC robate Record Book 1830-1834, Blount County, AL "Edward and Polly Nelson sold to John Morton June 16, 1832 seventy nine acres and 74 hundredths of one acre. Eastern half of SE quarter of section 30, Township 10, range 1 East, in d
2 CONC istrict of Laws."
2 CONT
2 CONT Re: Edward Nelson, Blount County AL  Posted by: LisaMortonMills (ID *****1123) Date: July 12, 2007 at 05:42:26  In Reply to: Re: Edward Nelson, Blount County AL by Dawn Peterson  of 10503   I can only recite to you the facts as I 
2 CONC knew them from my Mother. She began doing genealogy back in the 1940's, after WWII really and was active in the genealogical community of Alabama up until my father became sick in the late 1980's. None of her work was ever publish
2 CONC ed and most of it was based on her early interviews of those who were alive back in the 1940's to the 1960's. Those old ones still remembered some of the early pioneers and provided priceless information. My Mother was told by one
2 CONC  of the descendants that Edward Nelson was half Cherokee and that his father was a white man from Virginia. Edward "took" the name Edward Nelson as his white name and was known by this name when he functioned in the military. My M
2 CONC other did locate something which referred to this in the archives in Montgomery, but it was in the 1980's when I finally located that Edward's oldest son William migrated to the Going Snake District of the Cherokee Nation, whereas
2 CONC  the other sons did not go. I have often wondered if this meant that the mother of William was Cherokee, whereas the mother of his other children was white. Lisa Mills
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Thomas and Elizabeth:
2 CONT
2 CONT Alabama Department of Archives and History Civil War Database Last Name: Stricklin   Branch: Infantry   Regimental Unit: 28th Alabama   Company Unit: C   Authority: Pay Roll near Knoxville 1862/10/31   First Name: Thomas    MI: L 
2 CONC   Enlistment Date: 1862/02/04   Enlistment Information: Murphree's Cross Roads, Private   Remarks: Commutation money due him $25.00   ADAH Civil War Database: http://www.archives.alabama.gov/civilwar/soldier.cfm 
2 CONT
2 CONT Those Gallant Men of the Twenty-eighth Alabama Confederate Infantry Regiment by James H. Walker and Robert Curren; Heritage Books, Inc., Bowie Maryland, 1997: Company C, Blount County 28th Alabama Infantry Stricklin (Strickland?),
2 CONC  Thomas L., born about 1825 in Alabama son of William R. and Mary Stricklin; enlisted at age 26 at Murphree's Crossroads 6 Feb 1862; on company muster-in roll at Shelby Springs 6 Feb 1862; on muster roll from 4 Feb to 30 Apr 1862;
2 CONC  appeared on a consolidated list of deserters in a report dated near Tupelo, Mississippi 28 Jun 1862, residence: Blount County; muster roll for Jan and Feb 1864 reported he reenlisted for the duration of the war at Dalton, Georgia
2 CONC  29 Jan 1864; received clothing 18 and 23 Apr and 30 Jun 1864; captured at Chattahoochie, Georgia 10 Jul 1864; sent to Nashville; forwarded to Military Prison at Louisville, Kentucky Jul 1864; transferred to Camp Douglas, Illinois
2 CONC  26 Jul 1864; reportedly died of smallpox at Camp Douglas 18 Dec 1864; however, a subsequent letter dated 7 Feb 1865 claimed the reported death was in error and he was returned to prison on 6 Feb; he applied to enlist in the 5th a
2 CONC nd 6th U.S. Volunteer Infantry, but was rejected 22 Apr 1865; took the oath of allegiance; discharged from Camp Douglas 11 May 1865; dark complexion, brown hair, hazel eyes, 5' 9 1/2", residence: Larkinsville, Alabama. Thomas L. S
2 CONC tricklin married Elizabeth Nelson 16 Mar 1854, Blount County.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1860 Blount Co., Alabama Census, pg 55, 381/378: Stricklin, Thomas.....25M.....AL Elizabeth...24F......AL William.......6M......AL George........4M......AL James.........1M......AL
2 CONT
2 CONT 1870 Blount Co, Alabama Census , The Eastern Half (of Blount Co.) pg 315, (472/478), ln 31, August 6 (next to William R. and Mary Stricklin): Strickland, Thomas.....34M.....AL E............... 34F......AL W.P............15M.....AL
2 CONC  G.E............13M.....AL N.B..............9F......AL James........11M....AL M.L...............3F.....AL H.J................1M....AL
2 CONT
2 CONT 1880 Blount Co, Alabama Census, Beat #8, Township 11Range 2E, pg 30 (397B), 243/243, ln 33 Strickland, Thomas 47 * AL SC NC   Farmer Elizabeth 47 wife AL SC SC  Keeping House Nancy 19 d. AL AL AL Mary 14 d.   AL AL AL Henry 12 s. 
2 CONC AL AL AL Sarah 7 d.   AL AL AL Martha 6 d. AL AL AL
2 CONT
2 CONT 1900 Cullman County, Alabama Census Pct. 6, Livingston, ED56, sheet 13A 208/208 Strickland, Thomas--head--  W  M  Aug 1835  64  m46 AL  SC  NC , Elizabeth--wife---W  F   Sept 1834 65 m46  AL  SC  AL , Nancey P.-dau--W  F   Jan  18
2 CONC 61  39 s       AL  AL  AL , Boyes--G-son---W  M   July 1884  15 s       AL  AL  AL
2 CONT
2 CONT Personal Communication from Sandra Evans, February 25, 2003: There was an article in The Moulton Advertiser on Thursday, August 3, 1995 on the Stricklin Clan.  There is a picture of Tom Stricklin and Betty Sue Nelson (listed as hi
2 CONC s wife).  The article is wonderful.   Tom's Great-grandchild is pictured with the muzzle loading rifle that Tom supposedly used in the Civil War.  The article states that Betty Sue (Could this be Elizabeth Nelson)?  was a full-blo
2 CONC oded Cherokee Indian who walked the Trail of Tears.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rootsweb Boards > Surnames > Stricklin  URL: http://boards.ancestry.com/mbexec?htx=message&r=rw&p=surnames.stricklin&m=110 Subject: Thomas E Stricklin  Author: Richard C Stricklin  Date: 27 Jan 2001 8:56 AM GMT   Email:  
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT I am looking for the descendants of Thomas E Stricklin b 1831 Lawrence, AL & Mary Elizabeth Unknown. Their children were Hinrietta M Stricklin b abt 1852, John B Stricklin b abt 1854, Laura Ann Stricklin b abt 1858, Samuel F Stric
2 CONC klin b abt 1864, James H Stricklin b abt 1867, Riley H Stricklin b abt 1872, Dora Stricklin b abt 1837. Contact me at richardstricklin@hotmail.com. 
2 CONT
