1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Billy J. Rainer (1934 - 2011)
2 CONT RAINER, Billy Jerald, 76, passed peacefully from his earthly home to his new home in Heaven on the morning of February 10, 2011. Visitation with family, 5:30-7:30 p.m. Wednesday, February 16 at Broadway Mortuary. (www.CozineMemori
2 CONC al.com) Funeral services, 2:00 p.m. Thursday, February 17, Broadway Mortuary, Pastor Terry Fox officiating. Burial at Resthaven Cemetery, Garden of Devotion. Billy was born in Newton, MS on October 25, 1934 to James Arnold & Bessi
2 CONC e Lee (Williams) Rainer. He graduated from Newton High School and completed one year of college at East Central Junior College (ECJC) in Newton before moving to Wichita, KS in 1952. On July 1, 1955, he married Wanda K. Egnew at Im
2 CONC manuel Baptist Church in Wichita. He had a career spanning 40 years as an Industrial Engineering Manager at Boeing Wichita, KS, and briefly, in New Orleans, LA, working on projects such as Air Force One and on the NASA Apollo proj
2 CONC ect in 1965 in New Orleans. He hired on at Boeing in 1952, at the age of 17. One of the many highlights of his career was having his name on a list of people who significantly contributed to the effort of putting the first man on 
2 CONC the moon. That list was encased in a capsule and buried beneath the surface of the moon. He was the grandson and great-grandson of Southern Baptist preachers and was a member of Summit Church. He was a member of the Boeing Managem
2 CONC ent Club. He was an avid golfer, a fan of most sports and a worthy competitor in all he engaged. He got his first hole-in-one several years ago on the Auburn Hills golf course in Wichita, but his family was his greatest joy in lif
2 CONC e. He was a precious son, an ornery brother, a faithful husband, a terrific father and a fabulous grandfather. He was looking forward to becoming a great-grandfather. He was also a dearly loved uncle, nephew, cousin, friend, neigh
2 CONC bor, etc. His passing has been a devastating blow to most all who knew him in any capacity, illustrating his strength of character and personality and the joy he brought with his warm sense of humor. At the request of Billy's fami
2 CONC ly, Governor Sam Brownback issued a proclamation declaring February 28, 2011 as Rare Disease Day in Kansas. Copies of that Proclamation were sent in the mail and he was able to see and read them on the day before his death. He exp
2 CONC ressed his hope that nationwide efforts like that one might one day be helpful to others who also suffer from any rare disease. Billy was preceded in death by his parents & by Wanda Kay Egnew Rainer, his wife of 55 years. He is su
2 CONC rvived by his two daughters Cynthia Kay (J. Michael) Duckett & Janet Lynn (Michael D.) Brewer; grandchildren, Jacob Matthew & Amanda Kay Duckett & Jonathan David (Kristi Dawn) Brewer; brother, James Lee (Donna) Rainer of Longmont,
2 CONC  CO, nieces & nephews, Karen Lee. Clark, Anita Kay (Dave) Idleman, John Robert (Sharon) Scheurer, Jay Curtis (Susan) Scheurer, Steven James (Daelene) Rainer and Patrisa Ann (Steve) McHone; the special ladies in his grandson's life
2 CONC , Lisa Nicole Urman, Abrianna Mia Urman, age 2, and one great-grandchild due in mid-September. Memorials have been established with the Myeloproliferative Disorder Foundation, 180 N. Michigan Ave., Suite 1870, Chicago, IL 60601 or
2 CONC  online at http://www.mpdfoundation.org/donations.asp., and Summit Church, 10225 E. Kellogg, Wichita, KS 67207; http://www.summitchurchkansas.com/home.html.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/65564144/billy_jerald_ra
2 CONC iner Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 22 2024, 0:30:11 UTC''
