1 NAME Robert Carter /Burwell/
2 GIVN Robert Carter
2 SURN Burwell
2 _MARNM Burwell
2 NICK Robin
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 3 JUN 1720
2 PLAC Carters Creek, Gloucester, Virginia, United States
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 30 JAN 1777
2 PLAC Retreat, Hanover, Virginia, United States
2 ADDR
1 TITL Colonel
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Burwell_Robert_1720-1777
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Burwell was a member of the House of Burgesses (1752–1758), representing Isle of Wight County, and the governor's Council (1762–1776). Active in land development, he was a trustee of the town of Smithfield and an investor i
2 CONC n the Dismal Swamp Company. His appointment to the governor's Council in 1762 aroused anger among his contemporaries because of Burwell's bad temper and rumored mental deficiencies. His service on the Council allayed these fears, 
2 CONC and he served throughout the remainder of the colonial period. Like many of his contemporaries, he experienced financial difficulties in the 1770s. For unknown reasons he took no part in the government formed by the new commonweal
2 CONC th of Virginia in 1776.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burwell was the son of Elizabeth Carter Burwell and her first husband, Nathaniel Burwell (1680–1721), and was born on June 3, 1720, probably at Fairfield, his father's plantation on Carter's Creek in Abingdon Parish, Gloucester Co
2 CONC unty. His grandfathers were Lewis Burwell (d. 1710), a wealthy Gloucester County planter, and Robert "King" Carter (d. 1732), one of the wealthiest men in North America, who served as president of the governor's Council. His elder
2 CONC  brothers included Lewis Burwell (d. 1756), who also served as president of the Council, and Carter Burwell, who built Carter's Grove in James City County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Very little is known about Burwell's early life. His father died shortly after Burwell's birth, and in 1724 his mother married a second time, to George Nicholas, a Williamsburg physician. Burwell inherited large estates in Frederi
2 CONC ck, Isle of Wight, and Prince William counties and perhaps elsewhere, as well as at least one tract in North Carolina. For most of his adult life he lived in Isle of Wight County, which he represented in the House of Burgesses fro
2 CONC m 1752 to 1758. Burwell was a vestryman of Newport Parish, one of the first trustees of the town of Smithfield in 1752, and in 1763 one of the incorporators of the Dismal Swamp Company. About 1742 he married Sarah Nelson, of Yorkt
2 CONC own, half sister of William Nelson (1711–1772) and Thomas Nelson (1716–1782), both of whom became presidents of the Council. William Nelson, in turn, married one of Burwell's sisters, which made Robert Burwell an uncle of Thomas N
2 CONC elson (1738–1789), a signer of the Declaration of Independence and future governor of Virginia. Burwell and his wife had one son and one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1762, following the death of Philip Grymes, a member of the Council and a relative by marriage of Carter Burwell, family or business associates in London quietly and quickly arranged for Robert Burwell's appointment to th
2 CONC e Council. When the news reached Williamsburg at the end of July it provoked shock and disbelief because, as Lieutenant Governor Francis Fauquier put it, of some rumored defect in Burwell's "mental Qualifications, and an unwarrant
2 CONC able Impetuosity of Temper." Burwell duly took the oath and was admitted to the Council on July 30, 1762, but at that same meeting his kinsman Thomas Nelson suggested that the Council adopt an address to the king requesting "that 
2 CONC he would be graciously pleas'd to appoint some other more able and discreet Person in the Room of Mr. Burwell." The other councillors postponed the motion, and after Burwell had served on the Council for a few months Fauquier admi
2 CONC tted that the doubts about his fitness to serve had subsided. Burwell sat on the Council throughout the remainder of the colonial period.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burwell had financial problems. Early in the 1770s he attempted to sell some of his land. He eventually had to bequeath a plantation to his son-in-law because he could not raise the £1,000 cash dowry he had promised his daughter a
2 CONC t the time of her marriage to John Page (1743–1808), of Rosewell, who was a member of the Council at the end of the colonial period, its first president under the Constitution of 1776, and still later governor of Virginia. Early i
2 CONC n March 1770 Burwell's wife died, plunging him into depression. Both of his children were by then grown and living away from home, and he had nothing to occupy his time except financial worries. Burwell's loneliness came to an end
2 CONC  on the last day of December 1774, when he married Mary Blair Braxton, widow of George Braxton (d. 1761) and a sister-in-law of Carter Braxton, who was another signer of the Declaration of Independence and who later served on the 
2 CONC Council of State.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing is definitely known about Burwell's attitude toward the imperial crises that resulted in independence in 1776. Whether because of some lingering uncertainties about his intelligence or temperament, some unrecorded Loyalist
2 CONC  leanings, or premonitions of failing health, nobody seriously considered him for any responsible position when the new government of the commonwealth of Virginia was established in July 1776. Burwell lived quietly with his second
2 CONC  wife at her Newington estate in King and Queen County until his death there on January 30, 1777. The place of his burial is not recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT Time Line
2 CONT
2 CONT June 3, 1720 - Robert Burwell is born, probably at Fairfield in Abingdon Parish, Gloucester County, to Nathaniel Burwell and Elizabeth Carter Burwell.
2 CONT
2 CONT ca. 1742 - Robert Burwell marries Sarah Nelson. They will have one son and one daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1752 - Robert Burwell is one of the first trustees of the town of Smithfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1752–1758 - Robert Burwell represents the Isle of Wight County in the House of Burgesses.
2 CONT
2 CONT April 1762 - Robert Burwell is appointed to the governor's Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT July 30, 1762 - Robert Burwell takes the oath and is admitted to the Council.
2 CONT
2 CONT November 3, 1763 - Robert Burwell signs the Dismal Swamp Land Company Articles of Agreement.
2 CONT
2 CONT March 1770 - Robert Burwell's wife, Sarah Nelson Burwell, dies.
2 CONT
2 CONT December 31, 1774 - Robert Burwell marries Mary Blair Braxton.
2 CONT
2 CONT January 30, 1777 - Robert Burwell dies at his wife's Newington estate in King and Queen County.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
