1 NAME John Thomas /Rosamond/
2 GIVN John Thomas
2 SURN Rosamond
2 _MARNM Roseman
2 NICK "The Highwayman"
2 NICK "The Highway Man"
2 NICK John "the highwayman" Rosamond
1 NAME Pvt. John "The Highway Man" Rosamond
1 SEX M
1 OCCU unknown
2 PLAC Militia serving under Capt Beall
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1710
2 PLAC Poss County Leitrim, Ireland
2 ADDR
1 BAPM
2 DATE 11 SEP 1715
2 PLAC London St Botolph without Aldgate, London, England
2 ADDR
3 ADR1  Aldgate High Street
3 CITY London
3 STAE England
3 POST EC3N 1AB
3 CTRY United Kingdom
1 OCCU unknown
2 DATE 1750
2 PLAC Augusta County, Virginia; John Rosmond listed as Constable filing list of tax delinquents for the year 1750.
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1789
2 ADDR
3 CITY Abbeville
3 STAE South Carolina
3 POST 29620
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Master shoemaker, soldier
1 TITL Pvt.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (1) Source: Jimmy Rosamond <jdrosamond@comcast.net>.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Jon Presco <http://rougeknights.blogspot.com/2008/10/highwayman-is-free-images-hi gwayman.html>:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1724, . . . John ROSAMOND and his friend William Ray were arrested in Abingdon, Berkshire, England for stealing a hat, periwig, 30 pounds British sterling, five pairs of shoes, and a brown gelding. They were held in the gaol in
2 CONC  Reading, Berkshire, after their trial where they were sentenced to be exiled to the colonies for 14 years hard labor. By March 1725, they were transported to Newgate Prison and held there until they boarded the convict ship "Forw
2 CONC ard" owned by Jonathan Forward, and captained by Daniel Russell. The ship set sail on 28 September 1725 from London via the Thames River. The ship arrived [and] disembarked at Annapolis, Maryland on 8 December 1725. We don't know 
2 CONC who bought his indenture, but he is recorded as being in CPT Beall's militia of Prince George Co, Maryland between 1734-1737. By 1747-1765 we find John ROSAMOND living in Augusta Co, Virginia and listed as a master shoemaker, owne
2 CONC d land, paid tithes, served in the militia, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT (3) Coldham, Peter Wilson, Bonded Passengers to America, 9 Volumes in 3, Baltimore, MD: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1983, Vol. 6, Oxford Circuit, 1663-1775: Berkshire, Gloucestershire, Herefordshire, Monmouthshire, Oxfordshire, S
2 CONC hropshire, Staffordshire, and Worcestershire, p. 9:
2 CONT
2 CONT Berkshire
2 CONT
2 CONT Ray, William. S Lent R 14 yrs Summer 1725 for highway robbery LC from Forward at Annapolis, Md, December 1725. . . .
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosamond, John. S Lent R 14 yrs Summer 1725 for highway robbery LC from Forward at Annapolis, Md, December 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT ABBREVIATIONS USED
2 CONT
2 CONT LC - Landing Certificate issued at the colonial port of entry, followed by the date of issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT R - Reprieved on condition of transportation to the American colonies. In the case of those reprieved for 14 years or of life transportation, this is noted where it appears in the records; otherwise the term imposed was normally s
2 CONC even years.
2 CONT
2 CONT S - Sentenced to be transported to the American colonies for a period of seven years unless otherwise stated, followed by the date of the Sessions at which the trial took place.
2 CONT
2 CONT (4) Christianson, Scott, With Liberty for Some: 500 Years of Imprisonment in America, Boston, MA: Northeastern University Press, 1998, pp. 23-24:
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1717 Parliament passed an act empowering courts to sentence offenders directly to transportation. Persons convicted of clergyable felonies or petty larceny could now be sent to American plantations for seven years instead of be
2 CONC ing whipped or burnt on the hand. This meant that a large portion of England's offenders were eligible to be shipped abroad and sold as servants for seven-year terms. Felons convicted of capital crimes could, with royal consent, b
2 CONC e commuted to a term of fourteen years' transportation or, in some cases, life. Anyone who returned before her or his term expired or who helped a convict to escape was liable to be hanged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan Forward, a young London merchant with extensive contacts in Maryland, obtained a lucrative subsidy of three pounds for every Newgate felon and five pounds for every convict taken from the provinces. In exchange, he agreed
2 CONC  to ship any and all criminals sentenced to transportation, and to pay all costs, including gaol fees, for their conveyance. Forward was experienced in the African slave trade and had recently shipped two vessels with 171 convicts
2 CONC  to Maryland. Operating out of his Cheapside house on Fenchurch Street, London, he collaborated with Jonathan Wild, who helped to provide "felons" for shipment abroad.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 26, 1718, 29 malefactors at the Old Bailey were ordered to be transported. Four months later the Historical Register reported that 106 convicts "that were ordered for transportation, were taken out of Newgate and put on b
2 CONC oard a lighter at Blackwall Stairs, from whence they were carried through the Bridge to Long Reach, and there shipped on board the Eagle galley, Captain Staples commander, bound for Virginia and Maryland. (The Eagle was a well-kno
2 CONC wn slave ship that had sailed for the Royal African Company for more than a decade, so the transport of prisoners to America was nothing new for her.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Forward retained his monopoly for over twenty years until April 1739, when Andrew Reid was added to the payroll. Although Forward continued to transport felons from provincial gaols until the late 1740s, Reid assumed main control 
2 CONC of the convict trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) Bailyn, Bernard, Voyagers to the West: A Passage in the Peopling of America on the Eve of the Revolution, New York, NY: Vintage Books, 1988, pp. 262-263:
2 CONT
2 CONT For thoughtful Americans concerned with the character of American society, the banishment of convicts to America was an abomination, and for those with an eye for macabre humor it was ludicrous. The most famous comment on the prob
2 CONC lem was [Benjamin] Franklin's proposal-published after a crime wave, perpetrated in Virginia, Maryland and Pennsylvania chiefly by convict servants, was luridly reported in the press-that the colonies should be authorized to "tran
2 CONC sport" their rattlesnakes to Britain in exchange for "the human serpents sent us by the mother country."
2 CONT
2 CONT (6) Jimmy Rosamond <jdrosamond@comcast.net>:
2 CONT
2 CONT Researchers [have] assumed that John relocated to Virginia where he married Sarah Wilson. . . . John and Sarah relocated their family to the Abbeville District of South Carolina no later than 1765.
2 CONT
2 CONT The first instance of John being in Virginia is from Chalkley's Chronicles showing him in Augusta County in 1747. Then in 1765, there is a record in Chalkley's that says the property he and Sarah owned was sold to them by a man wh
2 CONC o didn't own the property himself. This apparently caused a problem, because the final record in Chalkley's shows them selling the land to someone else with the court's permission. The next record of him is a land grant dated 1767
2 CONC  in Abbeville District, SC. That dates their move to SC between 1765 and 1767.
2 CONT
2 CONT John was a master shoemaker in Augusta County, VA in the 1750s. This is documented in Chalkleys.
2 CONT
2 CONT (7) Caution: Based on a will which Jimmy Rosamond <jdrosamond@comcast.net> has found for a John ROSEMAN in Prince George's County, MD, proved in 1789, John "The Highwayman" ROSAMOND may have lived out his life and died in Prince G
2 CONC eorge's County, MD. If this proves to be true, the John ROSAMOND who lived in Augusta County, VA is probably not the same person as John "The Highwayman" ROSAMOND.
2 CONT
2 CONT (8) John ROSAMON, John ROSAMOND, John ROSEMAN, John ROSEMAND, John ROSEMOND, John ROSMAN and John ROSMOND are mentioned in Chalkley, Lyman, The Chronicles of the Scotch-Irish Settlement in Virginia: 1745 to 1800 [Reprint, Original
2 CONC ly Published, 1912], Baltimore, MD: Genealogical Publishing Co., 1980 <http://www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~chalkley/>. The compiler assumes that all of these Johns were the same person, namely, John ROSAMOND. Set forth below are all
2 CONC  of these references from Chalkley, with the capitalization of John's surname added by the compiler:
2 CONT
2 CONT Volume I, p. 39:
2 CONT
2 CONT AUGUST 24, 1749.
2 CONT
2 CONT John ROSEMOND added to tithables.
2 CONT
2 CONT Volume I, p. 120:
2 CONT
2 CONT APRIL 15, 1765.
2 CONT
