1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From http://rosamondpress.wordpress.com/2011/10/16/the-rosamond-hodges-all-american-redneck-family/
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Rosamond, R255, M. Born ca 1751 in Augusta County, Virginia. Samuel died in Anderson District, SC on 8 Nov 1814, he was 63. Buried in Williamston, SC. Occupation: Farmer, Soldier.
2 CONT
2 CONT Again there is some controversy regarding Captain Samuel Rosamond. William Sam Rosamond shows him as being born in County Leitrim, Ireland arouund 1738. This is based on the supposistion that his father John came to the US in 1740
2 CONC . If we accept that John arrived in 1725, and Samuel’s mother, Sarah Wilson, didn’t come to Virginia until 1740, Samuel would have to have been born after 1740. Barbara Morgan estimates that he was born sometime between 1745 and 1
2 CONC 755. If we assume that John and Sarah were married ca. 1749, 1750 would be an approximate date for his birth.
2 CONT
2 CONT After that things are a little clearer. We know that Samuel grew up in the Abbeville District, SC. He may have been married more than once as his will mentions a daughter Polly (Mary) that was not the daughter of his wife Sarah Ho
2 CONC dges.His wife Sarah was from a neighboring plantation and she was 10-15 years younger than Samuel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel enlisted in the militia around 1776-77 and served as a Lieutenant under Captain Adam Crain Jones and Colonel Robert Anderson (for whom Anderson County, SC was named.) In 1782 he was appointed Captain and served at the Siege
2 CONC  of Ninety-Six and the Battle of Kettle Creek in Wilkes County, GA on Feb. 14, 1779 during the Revolutionary War. This battle enabled the revolutionists to halt the British advance in Georgia after the capture of Savannah. Accordi
2 CONC ng to Samuel’s great-grandson James Oliver Rosamond, Samuel served as a scout and spy under the direction of Colonel Francis Mariion, the “Swamp Fox.”
2 CONT
2 CONT A copy of Samuel’s will is contained in another web page on this site at http://rosamond.ourfamily.com/samswill.htm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several women have joined the DAR based on the Revolutionary War record of Capt. Samuel Rosamond: Mrs. Josie Dean Rosamond HILBUN (275253); Miss Nannie SULLIVANT; Mrs. Molly Rosamond SULLIVANT; Mrs. Mary Rosamond RHYNE (251829); M
2 CONC rs. Allison Sullivant GUYTON (254893) – - – From 1965 DAR book.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Feb 1785 when Samuel was 34, he married Sarah (Salley) Hodges, H322, F, daughter of Richard Hodges, M & Elizabeth (Betty) Jones, F, in South Carolina. Born ca 1765 in South Carolina. Sarah (Salley) died in SC or MS on 24 Apr 18
2 CONC 44, she was 79.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruth Menhekl noted that Sarah’s father, whose name is unknown, also served in the Revolutionary War. It is uncertain whether Sarah died in SC, or if she traveled to Missisiippi with the family when they migrated in the early 1800s
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT They had the following children:
2 CONT        12       i.       Margaret “Peggy”, F (1786-1872)
2 CONT        13       ii.       Elizabeth, F (ca1786-)
2 CONT        14       iii.       John Hodges, M (1789-)
2 CONT        15       iv.       Sarah, F (>1790-)
2 CONT        16       v.       Richard, M (1795-1856)
2 CONT        17       vi.       Addison, M (1803-1873)
2 CONT        18       vii.       James, M (ca1807-)
2 CONT        19       viii.       Mary, F
2 CONT        20       ix.       Lucinda Jane, F
2 CONT        21       x.       Jane, F
