1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Record of service in the War of 1812 on file in the Old Records Section, War Department, U.S. Department of Archives, Washington, D.C. show that Henry Youngblood enrolled as a private in the 13th Regiment Mississip
2 CONC pi Militia on January 6 1815 for a 3 month tour of duty. This was Nixon's regiment, and Henry was in Caption Moses Collins' company.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Was this Moses Collins, his Uncle, or his Grandfather?)
2 CONT
2 CONT Declaration for pension on May 16, 1878 by hiswidow, Sarah Youngblood, at the age of 76, was made from Hemphill, Sabine County, Texas. She  stated that she was Sarah Harvey before marring and that shemarried on
2 CONT
2 CONT Feb. 27, 1917, in Marion County Mississippi. She further states that Henry Youngblood died in Caddo Parish, Louisanna on June 2 1840. "Widow's Brief" claim number 21,786, Certificate No. 8229, shows 14 days of service in Captain N
2 CONC ichols' company, enrolled in the winter of 1814,
2 CONT
2 CONT and discharged in 1815. He served in Captain Collins' company from Jan 6 to February 5, 1815. Pension was granted from March 9, 1878 at $8.00 per month. the last payment was made on February 4, 1895. Affidavit from
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah Youngblood included among papers, states that she signed the pension application by mark becauseof her age and disability. A letter concerning the pension, written July 15, 1876, says the marriage certificate will have to be
2 CONC  obtained from Marion County, Mississippi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah recived 160 acres of bounty land in full satisfaction for her husband's service in Camption Collins' Company, Mississippi Militia,War of 1812, under warrant 87,470-160-55, on June 20 1859. At which time her
2 CONT
2 CONT address is shown as Shreveport, Louisiana. In her declaration of January 5, 1859 concerning her land claim, she stated that she was married to Henry Youngblood on February 27, 1817 by James Phillips, Justice of the
2 CONT
2 CONT Peace for Marion County, Mississippi.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Ms. Carolina "Carrie" N. Walker, youngest daughter of Dr. Alexander Spann Walker and Sarah Anna Nicholson, (reported to ): "...At one time our great grandmother (Sarah Harvey) lived in Summer Grove, and then she also 
2 CONC lived near Mansfield. Mama used to often speak of how as children, they would visit their grandmother and what a good time they would have, staying all night. Mama said she was such a sweet,
2 CONT
2 CONT tiny littleperson, and that she humored them in every way.... of course mama's grandfather (Henry Youngblood) died years before she was born, and
2 CONT
2 CONT I doubt if she knew where he was buried. Summer grove is the little village located about eight or nine miles from Shreveport, on the Mansfield road, and is, I expect, one of the oldest settlements near here. There are churches an
2 CONC d school houses and a few stores there. This
2 CONT
2 CONT is the church taht my grandparents attendand where mama was baptized. There is an old cemetery located near the church. It is possibel the our great grandfather was buried there, but I doubt it.
2 CONT
2 CONT My grandfatehr and his brothr homesteaded land near Summer Grove, and there is whre my mother (Sarah Anna Nicholson Walker) was born, raised and married. He had his family burying ground located in sight of
2 CONT
2 CONT the residence, but Iknow Henry Youngblood isn't buried there..."
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
