1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Thomas Foster==
2 CONT * Birth:	1600 - Ipswich, Suffolk, England
2 CONT * Death:	Apr 20 1682 - Billerica, Middlesex, Massachusetts
2 CONT * Parents:	Thomas Foster, Abigail Wimes
2 CONT * Wife:	Elizabeth Whitmire
2 CONT
2 CONT Voyage of the Hercules in 1633/34 - The Departure From London and Southhampton
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hercules departed London on March 24, 1633/4, and Southampton on April 18, 1634, for New England. A Warrant was issued on February 22, 1633/4, to hold the departures of the Hercules, among other ships, which were in the Thames
2 CONC  with the intended destination of New England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ship and Passenger Information
2 CONT
2 CONT John Kiddey, Master. Passengers (12):
2 CONT
2 CONT Certificate of March 24, 1633/4, London
2 CONT
2 CONT John Anthoney(1) of Hampstead, Middlesex, bound for Portsmouth, Rhode Island (John Anthony on certificate for the Mary & John) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Early 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Foster of Ipswich, Suffolk, bound for Boston 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Foster of Ipswich, Suffolk, bound for Ipswich 
2 CONT
2 CONT Matthew Hewlett(2) 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Latcome 
2 CONT
2 CONT Certificate of April 16, 1634, Southampton 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathaniel Davyes (listed as Nathaniel Davis in "Planters of the Commonwealth") 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Elliott (listed as William Elliot in "Planters of the Commonwealth") 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Fifeilde, bound for Hampton, New Hampshire (listed as William Fifield in "Planters of the Commonwealth") 
2 CONT
2 CONT George Kinge (listed as George King in "Planters of the Commonwealth") 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Phelps, bound for Salem 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Rider, bound for Weymouth(3) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Excerpt from letter dated May 2, 1944 from Helen Bates Parks to Mary Park Masterson:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sergt. Thomas Foster, born England about 1600, married Elizabeth about 1638.  She died 1694-5.  He came to America in the ship Hercules with his brother William in 1634.  He was a gunner at the castle.  He resided in Weymouth, Wo
2 CONC burn, Boston, Braintree and Billerica.  He was repeatedly called before the Middlesex Court for what his prosecutors called heresy.  He attended meetings of the Anabaptists.  He died Apr. 20, 1682.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was a blacksmith and a progenitor of along line of blacksmiths. He was a man of stalwart character and unflinching adherence to his convictions. In his old age, he was repeatedly called before the Middlesex county and punis
2 CONC hed for worshiping the God of his fathers after the way the judges called heresy. One record, that of 15 Jun 1675, will suffice, it reads: "Thomas Foster appeared before the court to answer to the presentment of the grand jury for
2 CONC  not attending the public worship of God on the Sabbath days, and days of humilition and thanksgiving, and by his own confession in open court being convicted of constand and ordinary frequenting the meetings of the Anti baptist`s
2 CONC  on the Lord`s Day, was sentenced to pay a fine of 5 Pounds and costs, 8 Shilling, 6 Pence. He served in King Philip`s War. His estate amounted to 98 Pounds, 17 Shillings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will Citation (dated 18th Apr 1682): "I Thomas Foster being very weak in body, yet of perfect memory, and of a disposing mind do make my last will and testament as followeth. And in the first place I Committ my soule to God that g
2 CONC ave it, and my body to the earth to a decent buriall and as for that small portion of ye good things of this life which God hath graciously continued to mee, I do thus dispose of them and first I do will that all my just debts be 
2 CONC discharged, with ye charge of my funerall, nextly I do will that my dear wife may have a comfortable and honorable maintenance out of ye remainder during ye time of her natural life. Also I do give to my son-in-law James Frost a g
2 CONC ood Cow, or four pound in case it be to be had out of my estate after ye decease of my wife; lastly I do nominate and impower my son Joseph Foster to be my sole executor, and I do put my wholl estate both personal and real in his 
2 CONC hands, after my decease, for ye accomplishment of the premises, as witness my hand and seal this eighteen day of April in ye year of our Lord, God, one thousand six hundred eighty and two."
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: http://fleurymacmillan.tribalpages.com/tribe/browse?userid=fleurymacmillan&view=0&pid=1301&rand=210660623#moreinfo_
2 CONT
