2 CONT
2 CONT REFN: 14685[919019.ged]
2 CONT
2 CONT !Hawes, Frank M. FOSTER RECORD: AN ACCOUNT OF THOMAS FOSTER OF BILLERI CA MA. Somerville MA, Fred E. Bradford Printer, 1889. p.1,2,3,4.
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation was blacksmith and the progenitor of a long line of blacksmiths . According to the fashion which then prevailed, the serivener was careful t o describe him as a sergeant, in a deed and dated Nov. 1679 wherein Thomas a n
2 CONC s wife Elizabeth convey land to Moses Haggett for L8. He was fined several times for not worshiping at the publick worship of Go d on the Sabbath day, being convicted of constant and ordinary frequenting t he meeting of the anabap
2 CONC tists on Lord's day. No record of his marriage is made but it is assumed that there was only on e Elizabeth and that she was mother of all of his children. per Hawes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Elizabeth Whitmore Married: in Norfolk Co., MA 1 3 Children
2 CONT  Hopestill Foster b: 26 MAR 1648 in Weymouth, Norfolk Co., MA
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 2 Elizabeth Children
2 CONT  Increase Foster
2 CONT  Thomas Foster b: 18 AUG 1640 in Weymouth, Norfolk, MA
2 CONT  John Foster b: 7 OCT 1642 in Weymouth, Norfolk, MA
2 CONT  Elizabeth Foster b: ABT 1646
2 CONT  Hopestill Foster b: 26 MAR 1648 in Weymouth, Norfolk Co., MA
2 CONT  Joseph Foster b: 28 MAR 1650 in Braintree, Norfolk, MA
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: Title: 1183398.ged Repository:  Media: Other Text: Date of Import: Nov 18, 2001 Title: 919019.ged Repository:  Media: Other Text: Date of Import: Nov 20, 2001 Title: ralphroberts.ged Repository:  Media: Other Text: Date o
2 CONC f Import: Dec 15, 2003
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 2.  Second Thomas Foster 3862. SERGT. THOMAS FOSTER (Thomas), b. England, abt . 16oo; m. prob. abt. 1638, Elizabeth . She d. Jan. 29. 1694 5.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Foster came to America in the ship Hercules, with his brother William in 1634. He was the gunner at the castle. In 1639. at a meeting in Boston, of Mr. Thomas Oliver, Thomas Leaverett, Mr. William Colburn, Mr. Robert Keayne
2 CONC , Capt . Edward Gibon and Jacob Elyott, there was granted to Thomas Foster, the gunner at the castle, a great lot at the Mount (Mt. Wollaston. Braintree) for six heads, upon condition expressed for "Mount Wollystone lands." This p
2 CONC roperty he conveyed in 1647 to Thomas Beamont, of London, mariner. Mt . Wollaston was incorporated as Braintree in 164o.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 164o he was residing in Weymouth and there his first child was born. He .owned land in Weymouth in 1642: "Three acres on the east field, first granted to John Allin, bounded on the east with Jeffeny Staples' land, on the west w
2 CONC ith the land ot Edward Bates." He disposed of his remaining land in Boston limits in 1647 to William Brown, of Salem. While residing in Boston, in 1642, he was admitted a Ireeman, and on his removal to Billerica, was admitted ther
2 CONC e in 1647. The following year he moved to Braintree, and before 1659 was residing in Billerica.
2 CONT
2 CONT He went from Braintree to Billerica. In June, 1659, he received a grant of an eight-acre lot. His home lot was fifty acres, his house lot being part of it, lying on the east end of Bare Hill, bounded by Shawshine road, on the west
2 CONC  ninety and four pole, by William Hamlet on the north one hundred and two poles; by ye common, .east, ninety poles; by a highway on the southwest eighty-six poles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Farmer Savage and Paige are our authorities for considering Thomas Foster, of Weymouth, Braintree and Billerica, one and the same person. From Pattee's History of Braintree. p. 47o, part of the testimony of Henry Leonard, concerni
2 CONC ng some coal at the forge, reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Sworn Oct. 27, 1655. Mr. Gifford gave me orders that if Goodman Foster, or some others of Braintree, could make any use of them (the coals) I should dispose of them. Whereupon Goodman Foster had about two half loads, and some of 
2 CONC the rest of the neighbors thereabout fetched some of them, but they were so bad they would fetch no more, and Goodman Foster took as much paynes about them as they were worth, and although they would serve his turn, they would not
2 CONC  se, ve us at the Forge."
2 CONT
2 CONT From Hazen's History of Billerica: Thomas Foster lived east of Bare Hill, the hill south of the village. "The first list of town officers, 1659-6o, names Thomas Foster as one of the selectmen. He held the same office for 166o-61-6
2 CONC 3-65-67-69. In 166o Thomas Foster was chosen 'eldest corporal of the Trayne Band.'
2 CONT
2 CONT "8: 8 mo: 1675. In pursuance of an order from Hon. Concil. sent unto them by warrant from ye worshipful Simeon Willard, Esq., Sergeant Major in reference to the gathering the inhabitants of ye town into several garrisons according
2 CONC  to their best policy.
2 CONT
2 CONT —' "For ye south end of ye town, Sergeant Foster's house is appointed and so to take his son Joseph Foster, James Frost. Joseph French. Joseph Walker, Daniel Rogers, John Kitteridge, Thomas Richardson and 2 soldiers in all, 1o sol
2 CONC diers and .6 houses.
2 CONT
2 CONT "11: 9 mo: 167o. Thomas Richardson being convicted of taking two loads of cedar from our swamp and transporting ye same out of town, contrary to our towne orders, is fined 3os, which Thomas Foster paid for him, he agreeing to buil
2 CONC d 22 poles of four raile fence for Foster." The tax list for 1679 shows that Sergeant Foster was rated for 5s. one poll. The highest rating on the list is for 13s.
2 CONT
2 CONT We now quote from Dr. Paige: (Gen. Reg. xxvi, 394.) "Thomas Foster was a blacksmith, and the progenitor of a long line of blacksmiths. According to the fashion which then prevailed, the scrivener was careful to describe him as ser
2 CONC geant in a deed dated November, 1679, wherein Thomas and wife Elizabeth convey land to Moses Haggett foi £8." 
2 CONT
2 CONT In his old age he was repeatedly called before the Middlesex County Court and punished for worshiping the God of his fathers after the way his judges called heresy. To the first accusation he offered satisfactory defense, but afte
2 CONC rward he was condemned. Thus it is recorded: "Oct. 4, 1671: Thomas Foster appearing before the Court, to answer the presentment of the grand jury for absenting himself from the public ordinances of Christ on the Lord's day, pleade
2 CONC d that the reason of his absence was the providence of God necessitating him thereto was discharged, paying costs June 18, 1672; George Farley, Thomas Foster and William Hamlet being presented for breach of the Ecclesiastical laws
2 CONC , they all confessed the presentment, were admonished and ordered to pay costs, 4s, 6d, a piece."
2 CONT
2 CONT "June 15, 1675. Thomas Foster, appearing before the court to answer the presentment of the grand jury for not attending the public worship of God on Sabbath days and days of humiliation and thanksgiving, and by his own confession,
2 CONC  in open court, being convicted of constant and ordinary frequenting the meeting of the Anabaptists on Lord's day, is sentenced to nay a fine of £5 and costs, amounting to 8s, 6d." "If Mr. Foster was heretic," savs Dr. Paige, "his
2 CONC  posterity made ample amends by service to the Orthodox church in the office of deacon."
2 CONT
2 CONT No record is found of his marriage, but in March, 1648, and in November, 1679, he had a wife Elizabeth; probably the same who is provided for by his will. She was no doubt the mother of his children. Mrs. Elizabeth Foster died Jan
2 CONC . 29, 1694-5. She had namesakes in her daughter, Mrs. Frost, and four of grandchildren.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Foster died in Billerica, April 2o, 1682. The following is his will, recorded in the Middlesex Probate Records:
2 CONT
2 CONT I Thomas Foster being very weak in body, yet of perfect memory, and of a disposing mind do make my last will and testament as followeth. And in the first place I Committ my soule to God that gave it, and my body to the earth to a 
2 CONC decent buriall and as for that small portion of ye good things of this life which God hath graciously continued to mee, I do thus dispose of them and first I do will that all my just debts be discharged, with ye charge of my funer
2 CONC all, nextly I do will that my dear wife may have a comfortable and honorable maintenance out of ye remainder during ye time of her natural life. Also I do give to my son-in-law James Frost a good Cow, or four pound in case it be t
2 CONC o be had out of my estate after ye decease of my wife; lastly I do nominate and impower my son Joseph Foster to be my sole executor, and I do put my wholl estate both personal and real in his hands, after my decease, for ye accomp
2 CONC lishment of the premises, as witness my hand and seal this eighteen day of April in ye year of our Lord, God, one thousand six hundred eighty and two. Thomas Foster.
2 CONT
2 CONT Signed and sealed in presence of us.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan Danforth sen] Jonathan Danforth jun] ""June 2o 82 Jonathan Danforth sen & jun appearing under oath in court as witnesses to ye above will
2 CONT
2 CONT Jonathan R. Cdmingbon?]  Cunningham?] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of the estate was taken June 17, 1682, by Jonathan Danforth, senior and Joseph Thompson; it amounted to ,£98, 17, o.
2 CONT
2 CONT "* Thus, in a tew pages, is set down all that is known of Sergeant Thomas Foster, whose numerous descendants are now scattered throughout our country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Though but a humble citizen, content in times of peace to labor at his anvil, yet the alarming cry of war found him ready to buckle on the sword; and conscience, too, in the wordy conflicts of his day, proved him the bold defender
2 CONC  of his convictions. ., .,
2 CONT
2 CONT His bones are dust
2 CONT
2 CONT His good sword rust
2 CONT
2 CONT His soul is with the saints we trust.
2 CONT
2 CONT "I found the transfer by John Curtis, in old Boston Court Records alludes to his estate as formerly Thomas Foster, of Braintree, and 1669 it was. Then the next year Thomas, of Roxbury, is named as on the border line of an estate. 
2 CONC The last probably Dr. Foster, who gave it up before he died, in Cambridge, in 168o. Thomas, of Billerica, only, owned the estate then in halves, so I think in his old age he bought this farm with young Joseph, and probably promise
2 CONC d him the whole when he died. All the other sons were well helped out."—Miss Abbott.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rev. B. Beadham, of Newton, North Wales, England, writes: "Thomas Foster, Boston, 1641, who came in the Hercules, 1634, and was a gunner at the Castle, is also mentioned in that valuable, but ludicrously mis (or rather non-) edite
2 CONC d book, 'Thomas Lechford's Note-Book,' p. 135, and also p. 377, of the printed edition. We learn from Lechford that the son in New England was Thomas."
2 CONT
2 CONT He d. April 2o, 1682. Res., Boston, Weymouth, Woburn, Braintree and Billerica, Mass.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3868. i. THOMAS, b. Aug. 18, 164o; m., Oct. 15, 1662, Sarah Parker, of Cambridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT _3869. ii. JOHN, b. Oct. 7, 1642; m. Mary Chillingsworth. 387a iii. INCREASE, b. abt. 1644; mentioned by Savage, but nothing further.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3871. iv. ELIZABETH, b. abt. 1646; m. Deacon James Frost, of Billerica,
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan. 22, 1666. He was son of Deacon Edmund Frost. Res., Billerica, where he d. 1711. Shed. 1726. Ch.: 1. Thomas, b. 1667. 2. John, b. 1668. 3. Samuel, b 167o. 4. Elizabeth, b. 1672; m. Peter Cornell. 5. Edmund, b. 1675. 6. Mary, b
2 CONC . 1676;
2 CONT
2 CONT m. John Walker. 7. Sarah, b. 1678; m. Howard; he d. bef.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1711. 8 Hannah, b. 168o. 9. Joseph, b. 1682. 1o. Abigail, b. 1685; m. Ephraim Kidder. 11. Benjamin, b. 1687. 12. James, b. .
2 CONT
2 CONT 3872. v. HOPESTILL, b. March 26, 1648; m., Oct. 15, 167o, Elizabeth,
2 CONT
2 CONT widow of Thomas Whittemore.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3873. vi. JOSEPH, b. March 28, 165o; m. Dec. 11, 1672, Alice Gordon, of
2 CONT
2 CONT Roxbury; m., 2d, Margaret Brown; m., 3d, Mrs. Rebecca Danforth.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT “Thomas was born about 1600 in England. He emigrated in 1634. He arrived in the "Hercules". He took the oath of freeman on 26 May 1647 in Weymouth Norfolk Co MA.  He moved from Weymouth before Mar 1648 to Braintree Norfolk Co. and
2 CONC  died 20 Apr 1682 in Billerica Middlesex Co., MA. 	He was married to Elizabeth 1638”
2 CONT
2 CONT (HISTORY: The origin of Foster Wheeler: The Foster family)  Thomas Foster was born in England about 1600, the son of  Rev Thomas and Abigail (Wimes) Foster. He immigrated to America about 1630 and initially settled near Boston.  M
2 CONC arried in the Boston area in 1638, Thomas and his 	wife, Elizabeth, had six children. Thomas was a blacksmith and an officer 	of the town board in Billerica, 20 miles northwest of Boston. The next two generations of Fosters, John 
2 CONC and Joseph, also resided in the Boston area.
2 CONT
