1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/82187565/mary-caroline-jones
2 CONT "Find A Grave Index," database, FamilySearch (https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:7JM1-MF2M : 2 June 2022), Mary Caroline Justus Jones, ; Burial, Dana, Henderson, North Carolina, United States of America, Dana United Metho
2 CONC dist Church Cemetery; citing record ID 195402304, Find a Grave, http://www.findagrave.com.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Carolina Justus Janes was married to Hiram King Jones and had 13 children by him. Hiram also was living with another woman during this time and had 3 sons by her. He also married another woman in 1885, 2 years before Mary Car
2 CONC oline's death. There were adultry charges filed in the Henderson courthouse. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the burial site of Hiram King Jones in the Jones Family Cemetery, someone has placed a marker with both their names on it. But she was NOT buried beside him.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/82187565/mary-caroline-jones Mary Caroline Justus was a daughter of John Justus Jr. and Elizabeth Thomas Justus. She married Hiram King Jones in 1839. The couple had 13 children. They lived alon
2 CONC g land bordering the Big Hungry River. The log home is still in existence. Her daughter, Emily Morrison, raised her family in it. It was later sold to the Upward Community Club and placed along U.S. 176 as a place for the club to 
2 CONC sell plants and crafts. Later it was bought by a private individual and moved to where it is located at present near the intersection of U.S. 25 South and Shepherd Street. The couple separated prior to 1870. She remained in the lo
2 CONC g cabin in Big Hungry until her youngest children were young adults. She then lived with her daughter, Nancy, and son-in-law, Jeremiah Taylor. Nancy Jones Taylor died in 1886. Her youngest child was 7 years old at the time of her 
2 CONC death. Mary Caroline Justus Jones remained in the Taylor home to care for her daughter's children. Catherine Matilda Jones and Harriet Caroline Jones, two of the daughters of Mary Caroline Justus and Hiram King Jones, also lived i
2 CONC n the Taylor home with their mother. Mary Caroline Justus Jones and her daughter, Catherine Matilda Jones, both died in 1887. Daughter Harriet then went to live with her sister, Emily Delilah Jones Morrison, at the original home p
2 CONC lace in Big Hungry. Mary Caroline Justus Jones was buried at the cemetery at Dana United Methodist Church. The Taylor family attended this church, along with Mary Caroline after she moved into the home of her daughter. In the 1980
2 CONC s, a grave marker for her could not be located. Descendants raised money to place a marker for her grave at the Dana United Methodist Church cemetery. Whoever ordered the marker, or the engraver, made an error in the spelling of h
2 CONC er maiden name (Justice instead of Justus). There is also the initial P. before her name. Her name was Mary Caroline Justus.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-40001-314953609/mary-caroline-jones-born-justus-in-familysearch-family-tree FamilySearch Family Tree] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jun 5 20
2 CONC 18, 19:58:50 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Mary Caroline Justus was a daughter of John Justus Jr. and Elizabeth Thomas Justus. She married Hiram King Jones in 1839. The couple had 13 children. They lived along land bordering the Big Hungry River. The log home is still in e
2 CONC xistence. Her daughter, Emily Morrison, raised her family in it. It was later sold to the Upward Community Club and placed along U.S. 176 as a place for the club to sell plants and crafts. Later it was bought by a private individu
2 CONC al and moved to where it is located at present near the intersection of U.S. 25 South and Shepherd Street. The couple separated prior to 1870. She remained in the log cabin in Big Hungry until her youngest children were young adul
2 CONC ts. She then lived with her daughter, Nancy, and son-in-law, Jeremiah Taylor. Nancy Jones Taylor died in 1886. Her youngest child was 7 years old at the time of her death. Mary Caroline Justus Jones remained in the Taylor home to 
2 CONC care for her daughter's children. Catherine Matilda Jones and Harriet Caroline Jones, two of the daughters of Mary Caroline Justus and Hiram King Jones, also lived in the Taylor home with their mother. Mary Caroline Justus Jones a
2 CONC nd her daughter, Catherine Matilda Jones, both died in 1887. Daughter Harriet then went to live with her sister, Emily Delilah Jones Morrison, at the original home place in Big Hungry. Mary Caroline Justus Jones was buried at the 
2 CONC cemetery at Dana United Methodist Church. The Taylor family attended this church, along with Mary Caroline after she moved into the home of her daughter. In the 1980s, a grave marker for her could not be located. Descendants raise
2 CONC d money to place a marker at her grave at the Dana United Methodist Church cemetery. Whoever ordered the marker, or the engraver, made an error in the spelling of her maiden name (Justice instead of Justus). There is also the init
2 CONC ial P. before her name. Her name was Mary Caroline Justus. It is possible that the P refers to a nickname of Polly, but no documentation shows that she ever used the nickname Polly for Mary.
