2 CONT
2 CONT Name: Cullen Thomas BRIDGES 1 Sex: M Birth: 12 AUG 1844 in Kentucky Event: Service Record Company D, 2 KY, Reg C.S.A.; Forrest's Cavalry Burial: Drewry Bridges Cemetery 1804, Trigg County, Kentucky Census: 1880 Kentucky, Trigg, Ca
2 CONC nton, District 151, age 35, married, Farmer, cannot read or write Death: 15 APR 1913
2 CONT
2 CONT Event: Service Record age 17, (1861) enlisted in Confederate Army, served under Col. Thomas Woodward, commander of a division of the 2nd Kentucky Event: Service Record age 17, (1861) enlisted in Confederate Army, served under Col.
2 CONC  Thomas Woodward, commander of a division of the 2nd Kentucky Event: Service Record Cavalry, under Gen N. B. Forrest. 
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Obituary *
2 CONT
2 CONT     1913
2 CONT     CULLEN T. BRIDGES
2 CONT     April 1913
2 CONT     C. T. Bridges Answers Last Call After Lingering Illness
2 CONT     Was Prominent Citizen, Baptist, Mason and Confederate
2 CONT     After an illness dating back for two years or more, from which he gradually grew worse, Mr. Cullen T. Bridges answered the final summons Monday night at his home at Maple Grove, Ky., about seven miles south west of here.
2 CONT     Mr. Bridges was born not far from where he had always lived and where he died, on August 12, 1844, and was the youngest son of a large family of children of William and Mary Bridges. When a young man he was united in marriage 
2 CONC to Miss Virginia Thomas, daughter of Peyton Thomas, who preceded him to the grave about two years. To this union was born ten children, seven of whom are still living as follows: G.A. Bridges, John T. Bridges, Mrs. L. Cunningham, 
2 CONC Mrs. Ben Grigsby, Mrs. Robert H. Thomas and Jessie C. Bridges, all of this county. He is also survived by one brother, Drew Bridges.
2 CONT     Mr. Bridges was a Christian gentleman, and had been a member of the Donaldson Creek Baptist church for over forty years. He had also been a member of the Canton Masonic lodge no. 242 for nearly fifty years with the exception o
2 CONC f about two years that he held his membership with the Linton lodge, when it was first organized. He had served as Master of his lodge a number of times, and was a frequent attendant upon the meetings of the Grand Lodge. He also b
2 CONC elonged to the Odd Fellows many years ago.
2 CONT     During the Civil War he served as a member of the Confederate army under Forrest's command, and ever remained loyal to the 'Lost Cause,'. In politics he had always been a staunch Democrat and had frequently served his party as
2 CONC  committeeman and in other capacities.
2 CONT     He also had the distinction of being one of the oldest postmasters in Kentucky. He had served as postmaster of the Maple Grove post office since it was first established in 1873.
2 CONT     He had always been recognized as one of the foremost citizens of the county, and was generally active in every movement for the up building and improvement of his county and community, and will be greatly missed by his relativ
2 CONC es, friends, neighbors and the citizens of the county generally.
2 CONT     His remains were laid to rest yesterday about noon in the family burying ground near his late residence with Masonic honors in the presence of a very large crowd of sorrowing friends and loved ones.
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: William BRIDGES b: 30 MAY 1800 in Bridges Plantation, Tyancoca Swamp, Edgecombe County, North Carolina Mother: Mary ''Polly'' THOMAS b: 01 JUL 1802 in Bertie County, North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage 1 Dora Virginia ''Jenny'' Ann THOMAS b: 30 SEP 1846 in Kentucky
2 CONT
2 CONT     Married: 09 NOV 1863
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
