2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Cullen was born in 1844. Cullen Bridges ... He passed away in 1913. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT "In June, 1862 at the age of 17 Cullen enlisted in the Confederate Army (Company D) under Colonel Thomas Woodward, commander of a division of the 2nd Kentucky Cavalry, under General N. B. Forrest." He "saw service at nearby Fort D
2 CONC onelson, Tennessee," along with his brother William.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Cullen was with his division until it disbanded prior to the end of the war" and was honorably discharged at Columbia, Tennessee in 1863.
2 CONT
2 CONT His brother William lost his life during his service in the Civil War due to disease.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 9, 1863 Cullen married Virginia Thomas and they had ten children: Sallie, Infant, Gent, Ora Agnes, Mark Dale, John Trice, Rosie Lee, Cleveland, Mollie and Jessie.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his early days Cullen was a teacher in the original log building at the Maple Grove, Kentucky school.
2 CONT
2 CONT He also farmed, made hand-tooled leather items including saddles and saddlebags, repaired gravestones and harvesting machines, and was Postmaster at Maple Grove, Kentucky for the last 40 years of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cullen served as a committeeman and precinct worker for the Democratic party. In 1867-1868 he served as a constable in his magisterial district.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a member of the Canton, Kentucky Masonic Lodge # 242 where he served as master and held other offices for 48 years. He also belonged to the Linton Masonic Lodge # 575 for two years after it was organized in 1874.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cullen lived his entire life at the original ancestral home place on Maple Grove RD that belonged to his grand parents, the first Bridges to settle in Trigg County, Drewry and Charity Calhoun Bridges. Eventually Cullen had the ori
2 CONC ginal log cabin on the site razed and a Folk Victorian home was erected in its place.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cullen and his son Mark Dale enjoyed attending many Civil War Veterans conventions. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Cullen was buried in the Drury Bridges Cemetery in Trigg County, Kentucky, USA.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT ↑ Entered by Nancy Stanley, Jul 2, 2011 ↑ Biographical details entered by Lois Haywood, July 13, 2014 ↑ Find a Grave memorial for Cullen T. Bridges Nancy Stanley. See the Changes page for the details of edits by Nancy and others. 
