1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The Details of John Nichols’ Will
2 CONT Heir:  Judith (Bowers Spivey) Nichols	
2 CONT Inheritance: Dower interest in the plantation John Nichols was living on (500 acres) and any profits to be gained from land called an Island on the Northwest River in Norfolk County (100 acres).	
2 CONT Notes: Dower interest is only for the remainder of the spouse’s life and does not equate ownership. Judith Nichols’ Bowers relatives owned neighboring land.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Estate 
2 CONT
2 CONT Judath Spivey in the ''Virginia, U.S., Land, Marriage, and Probate Records, 1639-1850'' < [https://www.ancestry.com/sharing/5070452?mark=7b22746f6b656e223a226f74665273674a73397444436a5655455831796a754b5a626e67336a4c57684f6c7a31633
2 CONC 1366f6c4839493d222c22746f6b656e5f76657273696f6e223a225632227d AncestrySharing] >
2 CONT
2 CONT * Will of Judith Spivey, wife of Matthew:
2 CONT * Name Judath Spivey
2 CONT * Date 26 Oct 1720
2 CONT * Location Norfolk Co., VA
2 CONT * Property Hoggs
2 CONT * Notes This probate record was extracted from microfilmed copies of the original Will Book.
2 CONT * Remarks '''Judath Nickolls''', Book O.A.W. p. 181, dated 26 Oct. 1720, proved 19 May 1721 . . . Unto ye Children of my Son Mathew Spivey dec'd all my Cattle . . . as they shall Come to age According to Law . . . Unto Tamer Spive
2 CONC y & Elizabeth Spivey my two Gold R
2 CONT * Description Heir
2 CONT * Book O.A.W.-181
2 CONT * Prove date 19 May 1721
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://descendantsofthegreatdismal.com/2017/09/23/the-christianized-nansemond-of-deep-creek/
2 CONT
2 CONT >John Nichols, who was often referred to as Major—an indication that he was a member of a standing militia in the area—married Judith Bowers Spivey (the widow of Matthew Spivey) later in life and it is possible that he had a previ
2 CONC ous marriage (through which he fathered Sarah Nichols). Prior to marrying Judith, John also appears to have fathered two illegitimate children, John and Sarah, by his slave Jean Lovina. These details can only be inferred (not veri
2 CONC fied) through the contents of his will (which proved 17 May 1697).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT From < [https://descendantsofthegreatdismal.com/2017/09/23/the-christianized-nansemond-of-deep-creek/ “ Descendants of the Great Dismal”] > Posted on September 23, 2017 By Nikki Bass
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000196507172901&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT father is John Bowers and Mother is Sarah Clark 
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT The (X) denotes that Judith signed with her mark.  She apparently could not read or write. Re:  Nathan Spivey's book.  Judith Bowers will was dated October 26, 1720 and was probated May 19, 1721.
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT THERE ARE NO FAMILY SEARCH HISTORICAL DOCUMENTS FOR THESE PERSONS. Mathew Spivey Sr. [Parents] was born in 1661 in <Nansemond County, Virginia>. He died before 1690. He married Judith (X) Bowers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith (X) Bowers [Parents] was born in 1630/1645. She died in 1720. She married Mathew Spivey Sr..
2 CONT
2 CONT Other marriages:  Nichols, John  They had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Spivey
2 CONT
