2 CONC  ---W.E. Cox) "You see Charles J. Nelson, John M. Bryan, and other names of the Bryan family who were descendants of the Johnsons, recorded as legislators of North Carolina. "John Sedrick Nelson, who was nine times a member of the
2 CONC  legislature and helped establish the State University (Battle's History of the University, Vol.I, p. 140), lived below New Bern and was of our family.  One of his daughters married a Stanly and one a Gardner. "Jordan, Caleb, Thom
2 CONC as, and Nimrod Nelson, who lived near Nelson's Crossroads, now Grimesland, and married into the Laughinghouse and Hardee families are our relatives. "My grandfathers, Edward Nelson, Sr., and Charles Roach went to the Revolutionary
2 CONC  War.  I have heard my grandfather, Edward Nelson, Sr., tell of the many exciting battles through which he passed: of Washington's Memorable Retreat; of the never to be forgotten Winter at Valley Forge, going barefooted in the sno
2 CONC w; living on three spoonsful of meal a day; the attempted Meeting; being met by Washington; was persuaded by his solemn and loving appeal to stop; threw up their hats with three cheers for Washington, and suffered on till relief c
2 CONC ame.  His cheeks would, by this time be wet with tears.  He would then finish in a joyful strain, with the capture of Cornwallis. "Edward Nelson, Sr., was six feet, eleven inches and three quarters tall.  A very large man but not 
2 CONC corpulent.  He weighed 250 pounds.  Edward Nelson, Jr., was six feet, nine inches and a quarter in height.  Was a little corpulent and weighed 225 pounds.  Both very strong men.  George Nelson, my cousin, was seven feet tall.  Cha
2 CONC rles Roach was not quite so tall, but remarkably broad shouldered, lion built, and was the strongest man in his regiment.  The Nelsons were all mechanics and blacksmiths.  The Roaches were all mechanics and machinists.  Could do a
2 CONC nything they pleased with tools.  David Roach, my mother's oldest brother and Charles Roach's oldest son, put up the first cotton mill in Georgia.  He served his trade as a machinist in Raleigh, NC  The contract to put up the Geor
2 CONC gia Mill was his first job after serving his apprenticeship.  He married and lived in Georgia and continued to put up cotton mills and all kinds of machinery.  His family are now among the wealthy people of that state.  Charles Ro
2 CONC ach, my mother's half brother, a son by the first wife, could make anything he ever tried, but to put a hornet's nest together. He could make locks and keys, ear rings, finger rings, breast pins, and silver spoons as nice as you c
2 CONC an buy.  I have seen the spoons and have one of his rings. "Charles J. Nelson was my father's oldest brother.  He was twice a member of the North Carolina legislature.  He moved to Tennessee in the thirties and was elected to the 
2 CONC legislature of that state."   The MARY NELSON SMITH FAMILY REUNIONS have been held though out the years at St. John's Episcopal Church near Grifton, NC in Pitt Co.  It is 4 miles East of Grifton, just off Highway 118 (turn North o
2 CONC n cross roads to Stokestown, the church is l mile North.  From  Southern Sidelights - A Record of Personal Experience- Illustrated, a book by the Rev. W. E. Cox, 1942, Edwards &amp; Broughton Co., Raleigh, NC p. 99   " Chapter XIV
2 CONC   AN EMERGENCY SCHOOL-TEACHER   By the side of the road on the lower or south end of Grandfather's plantation was an old schoolhouse where, prior to the Civil War, was a neighborhood school.  After the war Grandfather and his neig
2 CONC hbors were impoverished, the slaves had been freed, the white men had to cultivate the farm while women had to do the cooking and housekeeping; and the schoolhouse remained closed.  At length Grandmother, who was a resolute and re
2 CONC sourceful woman, determined that her children should not grow up in ignorance.  She began teaching them at home; and, while continuing to do her house-work, she taught them daily for a few hours each morning and afternoon.   When 
2 CONC the neighbors learned of this they asked her to take their children, and she finally moved her school to the old schoolhouse.  It was here that I got my first schooling. Some pupils, girls as well as boys, walked three miles to th
2 CONC at school every morning, and the same distance back every afternoon. "Grandmother was not at that time equipped to do much more than teach the three R's, with Grammar, Geography and United States History added, but she was thoroug
2 CONC h and she made us master the fundamentals of what she did teach.  So thoroughly did she ground us in such elemental things as the Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication, and Division Tables; also the various tables of weights and m
2 CONC easures, that to this day I can recite them as I recited them to her sixty-odd years ago.  She was equally insistent on thoroughness in spelling and writing.  We used Webster's blue-back spelling book, which started us with the le
2 CONC tters of the alphabet, then words of two letters, followed by words of three letters, going steadily on to the longest of words.* *  * A retired educator, John G. Elliot, came to live with the Smiths and to prepare Grandmother for
2 CONC  the work of teaching advanced classes. * * * Mr. Elliot graduated at the University of NC with the class of 1822.  He was nearly 80 years of age when he came to live with Grandfather and Grandmother and was 'peculiar,' with white
2 CONC  hair and beard which he kept close-cropped with blunt pointed kindergarten scissors carried in his pocket for the purpose, and was regarded as an eccentric old bachelor.* * * *   p. 104 She prepared all her unmarried daughters fo
2 CONC r college in these courses and has been called 'a Pioneer Pitt County Educator,' for the reason that she pioneered in putting in the public schools courses of high school grade.  * * * Two of those pupils later became her sons-in-
2 CONC law, and one of them who studied surveying directly under Mr. Elliot, became a practical surveyor and for many years surveyed timber lands for the Beaufort County Lumber Company in the several counties where that company operated.
2 CONC  "The increasing number of people who wanted to come from other parts of the county to study under Grandmother and Mr. Elliott, moved Grandfather and Grandmother to build a new two-story schoolhouse near the end of the lane {pictu
2 CONC re p. 104} and open their home to boarding pupils.  The school was moved to the new schoolhouse in 1882, when I was in my 12th year.  I went to school there two years, and then the call of duty at home took me away."   p.108  " Wh
2 CONC en I was about 14 {1884},* * * my father was disabled by an acute kidney disease, and responsibility for the farm and the support of the family fell on my shoulders. p. 110  "My Grandmother was a resourceful woman, and the word 'f
2 CONC ail' was not in her vocabulary.  When there was a worth while thing to be done, she found a way to do it.  Furthermore, her English ancestors were staunch Churchmen with many of them numbered among the clergy.  Grandmother, too, h
2 CONC ad a deep love for the Church, and a passion for influencing and helping men to enter the Ministry.  Her influence put one son and two grandsons in the Ministry. "In the Epistle to the Hebrews (5:4) there is a verse which says: 'A
2 CONC nd no man taketh this honour to himself, but he that is called of God, as was Aaron.'  According to Exodus 4:14-16 and 28:1-5 Aaron's call to the priesthood came 'from' God but 'through' Moses.  However slight the analogy may be, 
2 CONC those Scriptures always remind me that the call to the Ministry came to me through my Grandmother. "One summer day in my 22nd year {1892} I was surprised to see Grandmother walking slowly out into the field where I was at work, an
2 CONC d apparently in a brown study as if something weighty were on her mind.  After a casual greeting and with few preliminaries, she put to me the direct question, 'Willie, wouldn't you like to study for the Ministry?'  Surprised as I
2 CONC  was, it was like letting me peep through the crack of a door slightly ajar, to catch a glimpse of a possible new world for me.  I loved the Church, and I was spiritually inclined, but there seemed to me only one answer I could pr
2 CONC operly make; so I said 'Grandmother, I must stay here with Mother and the younger children (my father had died the year before), and furthermore I haven't the means for the necessary education.'  Instantly she replied; 'Your broth
2 CONC er is now as old as you were when you took charge of the farm, and the same Lord that has taken care of your mother and the children with you as farmer, will continue to take care of them with your brother as the farmer.  You thin
2 CONC k it over a few days, and in the meantime I will see what can be done about the necessary means.'  With that she turned and left me.  In about two weeks she informed me that she had secured for me at Trinity School, Chocowinity, a
2 CONC  not far distant military school, a scholarship provided by the Convocation of Edenton, that paid all necessary expenses except for uniform, books and incidentals; also that she had arranged with the head of the school to let me h
2 CONC ave uniform and books on credit, with the promise to give me an opportunity to work them out.* * * Thus it came about that just before my 22nd birthday, I set out from home to enter the preparatory school and study for the Ministr
2 CONC y, without resources save for good health and the driving force of a Spartan old grandmother back of me urging me on. " The head of the school gave me the job of repairing fences, out buildings, grape arbors, etc., about the schoo
2 CONC l premises.  The school was located in the country several miles from the town of Washington, NC and I soon learned that getting to town for haircuts and shoe repairs was something of a problem.  In the effort to make ends meet at
2 CONC  home, I had learned to half-sole the family shoes and cut the other childrens' hair.  I therefore sent for my scissors and shoe tools and offered to render the students the same service at half what that service would cost them i
2 CONC n town.  I must admit that my work was a bit crude, certainly at the start, but it was substantial.  The business enabled me to keep my head above water.  I paid what I owed and took care of incidental expenses through the years I
2 CONC  was there, without a dollar from home."                         This information compiled by Katharine Lassiter Butte Barbier
