2 CONT OUR FAMILY GENEALOGY , Revised Second Edition,1967  Including the Nelson, Johnson, Roach, Smith, Little, Cox, Dawson and Chapman Families, each related to the other by descent or marriage, or both.  First Edition Compiled by the R
2 CONC ev. Wm. E. Cox and Mrs. Olivia Cox McCormac 1938.  To the Memory of Mrs. Mary Nelson Smith  a rare personality with strong mind, powerful physique, resolute will, tireless energy, dauntless faith and deep devotion---one who left h
2 CONC er impress not only upon her children to the fourth and fifth generation but also upon the whole community in which she lived, this book is affectionately dedicated.  Our Family Genealogy p. l: " Autobiographical Sketch" by Mary N
2 CONC elson Smith   "Mrs. Mary Smith of Winterville, NC is the daughter of Edward Nelson, Junr., who was a descendant of the noble family of Nelsons of Kent, England.  Her mother was Sarah Roach, daughter of Charles Roach, Senr. "She wa
2 CONC s born at the old Nelson Homestead in Craven County, NC, October 2, 1825. ''On the fifteenth of March, 1846, she married William Henry Smith of Pitt Co., N. C.  She had twelve children.  Ten lived to be grown.  Her ancestors on bo
2 CONC th sides were remarkable for size and strength. Her grandfathers, Edward Nelson and Charles Roach, were soldiers in the Revolution of 1776 and were the two strongest men in their regiment.  Her grandfather Nelson was the second ta
2 CONC llest man in the old colonial courthouse, Governor Richard Dobbs Spaight being seven feet and her grandfather six feet, eleven and three-quarter inches and weighed two hundred and fifty pounds.  Her father was six feet, nine and a
2 CONC  quarter inches and weighed two hundred and thirty pounds. "Though eighty years old, she does more walking than any woman in town and can do as much house-work as most of the younger women.  She is spinning flax to make her younge
2 CONC st son, who lives in Florida, a suit of clothes.  She expects to cut and make the clothes before sending them to him  She can sew very nicely on the machine.     "The wheel on which she is spinning is the Old Abrahamic pattern.  T
2 CONC radition says it was brought from England in 1733 by the Rev. Mr. Stewart, who was the first rector of St. Thomas church, Bath, NC "Mrs. Smith bought this wheel from a widow, whose husband was a descendant of the Rev. Mr. Stewart.
2 CONC  "The wheel at her right {in the picture of her, facing p. 1} is called the Martha Washington.  This is the wheel that was used in the South in the olden days, when the women spun the clothes for their families. "It is the pattern
2 CONC  that Mrs. Martha Washington used when she wore her checked apron, knit her own stockings and superintended the running of thirteen spinning wheels. "It was used during the Civil War when all had to spin what clothes they wore, as
2 CONC  they could not buy them. "Many were the bolts of cloth manufactured every year in a family, in the good old ante bellum days of housekeeping when the negro women spun all winter (and summer too, when they were not hoeing the corn
2 CONC , cotton, and potatoes.) "Fustian, corduroy, huckaback, kersey, dimity, counterpanes, yarn and cotton with plain blankets, plaids, and stripes of all patterns and colors, were the products of the home loom.  In those days, the mon
2 CONC ey that the farmer made selling corn, pork, flour, tar, and turpentine was put in the till of the old big  chest, and was not touched till there was enough to buy a negro or a piece of land.  It took very little of his money to bu
2 CONC y what dressing his family needed.  The mistress, and housekeeper, who could not, with her chickens, eggs, and butter, pay her grocer's bill and buy a dress for herself and children occasionally, was counted a poor helpmate. "Mrs.
2 CONC  Smith, though she had no great advantage of education in her youth, has done good work as a teacher. "She taught her first year in 1845, being the year before her marriage.  After she was married she taught several years (into th
2 CONC e fifties), near her home.  She had to study her grammar as she taught it, having never studied it at school.  She mastered it and taught it successfully.  She was good in what she had studied.  Increasing family cares compelled h
2 CONC er to give up teaching. "Mr. Smith employed a teacher and kept a good school near the home until 1869.  At the close of 1868, being made poor by the war, he found he was no longer able to support a school. "There were now seven ch
2 CONC ildren at home, three having married. "Four of the younger children had never been to school. {Adelaide "Addie" 8 yrs., George 6 yrs., Mary Virginia 4 yrs. &amp; Holland "Hollon" 2 yrs.}  " In January 1870, Mrs. Smith determined t
2 CONC hey should not be without a school any longer.  She told Mr. Smith she would do the best she could, and she felt that God would help her. "She had the furniture removed from a room in the second story of her house, school desks an
2 CONC d benches put on one side, and her wheel and cards on the other.  Mr. Smith took the oldest boy to help him plow, promising that he should stay next session.  After preparing breakfast and doing the morning's work, Mrs. Smith woul
2 CONC d go up and have a three hour's school, carding or spinning when she was not hearing recitations.  Then she would go down, prepare the noon meal, do the chores, and again go up and have three hours of school in the afternoon.  Whe
2 CONC n they went down in the evening they would do the washing, or ironing or scouring, or working the garden as the turn came. "She did not go on many weeks in this way, when the neighbors found out she was teaching, and asked permiss
2 CONC ion to send.  It was not long before she had to put away the wheel and cards and give her whole attention to teaching.  She soon had the room full.  Others making application to send, she took her school down to the old schoolhous
2 CONC e to accommodate the neighbors.  The first day she had over forty pupils.  A steady increase soon made it necessary to enlarge the house. "About this time she was visited by an old gentleman who was a good Latin scholar and one of
2 CONC  the best mathematicians in the country.  He had been a prominent teacher many years.  He was now too old to manage a school, but would teach any one who wished to study.  At Mrs. Smith's request he made her house his home.  (This
2 CONC  was John G. Elliot.)  "With him, though forty-five years old, she studied Latin, algebra, geometry and surveying, especially during her vacations, having his assistance at all times. "She taught Latin and algebra and prepared her
2 CONC  children for college in these branches. "She was liberally patronized by the people of her county and surrounding counties. "She taught eighteen years without stopping.  At the time her school closed, six of her children and two 
2 CONC of her grandchildren were teachers.  One of her daughters taught twenty years. "Since then she has taught at different places in the county, making in all, over twenty-five years.  She taught her last school in 1901.  If her heari
2 CONC ng had not failed she would be teaching now. "Several of her many grandchildren are now teachers, being considered among the best in the county. "Mrs. Smith in her life's work, has truly verified the old proverb, that God helps th
2 CONC ose who help themselves. "On Sunday, October the first, 1905, she saw her oldest grandson, W. E. Cox, ordained to the priesthood.  Her son, Claudius (Claud) F. Smith, who is a minister in Washington, D. C., preached the ordination
2 CONC  sermon.  On the next day, Monday, October 2, she was present at the consecration of a church that she and her daughters have built at Winterville.  After the services, she, with about forty of her family (children, grandchildren,
2 CONC  and great-grandchildren), accompanied by the bishop and a number of friends, repaired to a grove near by, where they found a bountiful dinner spread in celebration of her eightieth birthday. " It was greatly enjoyed by all presen
2 CONC t, and was a happy time for Mrs. Smith."  *            *             *            *            *            *           *              *               * The following written by Jennette Cox St. Amand (daughter of Mary Virginia Sm
2 CONC ith who married Beriah Thaddeus Cox, M.D.) was a compiler of Our Family Genealogy: "NOTE #1:     Grandmother Smith wrote this autobiographical sketch by request after she was past eighty years of age.  Some of her children and gra
2 CONC ndchildren wanted her own story about how her little home school grew into a boarding school with advanced courses, to which came pupils from all over the county to prepare themselves for service as teachers in the public schools.
2 CONC   For reasons of her own she spoke of herself in the third person.  The sketch is presented here exactly as she wrote it.  The manuscript in her own handwriting is now in my possession. " We might add here that seven of her childr
2 CONC en, twenty-one grandchildren and fifteen great-grandchildren, in addition to her many boarding pupils, became teachers in Pitt County and elsewhere in this and other states.  Besides that, one of her sons (Claud) and two of her gr
2 CONC andsons (Wm. W. and Harvey A. Cox) became clergymen of the Episcopal Church. "The public library in Greenville has had an alcove of books given in her memory, and an oil portrait of her hangs in that library.  Furthermore, one of 
2 CONC the flax wheels shown in the picture, together with her spinning wheel (for cotton) and her loom, on which slaves formerly made cloth, have been given to East Carolina Teachers College in Greenville as an educational exhibit. "NOT
2 CONC E #2:     With the passing of the years conditions change.  * * * The old loom and the flax wheel described as being on exhibit at East Carolina College were in full use in the Department of Manual Arts during the years between th
2 CONC e two editions of this genealogy, the loom undergoing many repairs before it was finally replaced 'for lack of any of the former looms to repair.' "New Library procedures made it necessary to scatter the books given to Mary Nelson
2 CONC  Smith alcove in the Sheppard Memorial Library in Greenville among their  various departmental headings, but each contains a nameplate indicating its donor.   The oil portrait still hangs in the library." "NOTE #3:     An article 
2 CONC in the Sunday issue of the Wilmington, N. C. Star-News newspaper, January 15, 1961, describes Mr. John G. Elliott who prepared Mary Nelson Smith for teaching high school subjects in her school, as a colorful Sampson County charact
2 CONC er; in his youth so thin that he was known  as 'Ghost' Elliot.  He was a graduate of the Univ. of N. C., a classmate of James K. Polk who later became President of the United States, and a good classical scholar who insisted in 18
2 CONC 47 on conversing with President Polk at commencement time in Greek."  On page 9,   "NOTE: The {following} paper was written by my Grandmother, Mary Smith, by request, a few years before she died.  The manuscript, in her own handwr
2 CONC iting, is now in my possession.  I cannot vouch for the correctness of it in every detail, but due allowance for minor faults of memory, on Grandmother's part, I think it is an authentic and highly valuable family record.  ----Rev
2 CONC . W. E. Cox."  " MY ANCESTRY SO FAR AS I KNOW THEM" written by Mrs. Mary Smith "My father was Edward Nelson, Jr.; his father, my grandfather, was Edward Nelson, Sr., the son of William Nelson, my great-grandfather.  My great-grand
2 CONC father came from below New Bern, entered a large tract of land between Fork Swamp and Chocowinity Pocoson, and made a settlement for himself and three sons, Hardee, George and Edward. "My great-grandfather's ancestor was of the hi
2 CONC storic family of English Nelsons.  He came from England to Virginia and when the first permanent settlement was made in North Carolina he was one of the colonists. "My father, Edward Nelson, Jr., married Sallie Roach, the second d
2 CONC aughter of Charles Roach, Sr., who was the son of John Roach, who came from Ireland, and settled on Neuse River some six or seven miles below St. John's church, in the Murphy neighborhood.  He, John Roach, married Katie Johnson, s
2 CONC ister of Jacob Johnson, who lived at Vanceboro, and owned the whole village and surrounding country. "My grandfather, Charles Roach, married Polly Summers, who was of Swiss descent.  She was my grandmother.  She raised seven child
2 CONC ren: David, Hannah, Samuel, James, Sallie, Madison, and Nancy.  David built the cotton mills of Georgia and his posterity are there.  Madison and Nancy went to Tennessee, and left families.  The others all died in North Carolina. 
2 CONC  My grandfather, Edward Nelson, Sr., married Winifred Johnson, daughter of Jacob Johnson, Sr.  So my father's grandfather (Jacob Johnson) and my mother's grandmother (Katie Johnson Roach) were brother and sister. "My grandmother, 
2 CONC Winifred Nelson, had four children: Jennette, Charles, Permelia, and Edward (my father).  Jennette married Matthew Williams of New Bern.  She had one child, Edward.  Charles married Eliza Godly of Bear Creek and moved to Tennessee
2 CONC .  Permelia married a Surls on Swift Creek.  She died soon after she was married.  She was a beautiful woman.  My sister, Emily, was like her. "I do not know who my great-grandfather William Nelson married.  He had six children: H
2 CONC ardee, George, Edward, Sallie, Polly, and Esther.  Polly married an Edwards, Sally married a Clark, and Esther married a Lewis. "My great-grandfather, Jacob Johnson I, married a Pearce.  He had 13 daughters and one son, Jacob II, 
2 CONC who was cousin Fred Johnson's father, and grandfather to Allen and Edward Johnson.  He married a Coart, sister to Bryan and William Pugh's mother.  Anna Johnson married Charles Roach, Jr., the son of my grandfather Charles by his 
2 CONC first wife.  He had two other sons by his first wife, John and Ruben; one went to Florida, and the other to Georgia.  Susan Johnson married a Bull, and Bettie married a Campbell.  Rachall, Sally, and Polly, married Bryans.  This i
2 CONC s why Johnson is a favorite name with the Bryans. "One of the Johnson daughters who married a Bryan was the mother of Nancy Bryan, who was General Grimes' father's first wife.  (It was Gen. Grimes' grandfather who married a Bryan.
2 CONC  ---W.E. Cox) "You see Charles J. Nelson, John M. Bryan, and other names of the Bryan family who were descendants of the Johnsons, recorded as legislators of North Carolina. "John Sedrick Nelson, who was nine times a member of the
2 CONC  legislature and helped establish the State University (Battle's History of the University, Vol.I, p. 140), lived below New Bern and was of our family.  One of his daughters married a Stanly and one a Gardner. "Jordan, Caleb, Thom
2 CONC as, and Nimrod Nelson, who lived near Nelson's Crossroads, now Grimesland, and married into the Laughinghouse and Hardee families are our relatives. "My grandfathers, Edward Nelson, Sr., and Charles Roach went to the Revolutionary
2 CONC  War.  I have heard my grandfather, Edward Nelson, Sr., tell of the many exciting battles through which he passed: of Washington's Memorable Retreat; of the never to be forgotten Winter at Valley Forge, going barefooted in the sno
2 CONC w; living on three spoonsful of meal a day; the attempted Meeting; being met by Washington; was persuaded by his solemn and loving appeal to stop; threw up their hats with three cheers for Washington, and suffered on till relief c
2 CONC ame.  His cheeks would, by this time be wet with tears.  He would then finish in a joyful strain, with the capture of Cornwallis. "Edward Nelson, Sr., was six feet, eleven inches and three quarters tall.  A very large man but not 
2 CONC corpulent.  He weighed 250 pounds.  Edward Nelson, Jr., was six feet, nine inches and a quarter in height.  Was a little corpulent and weighed 225 pounds.  Both very strong men.  George Nelson, my cousin, was seven feet tall.  Cha
2 CONC rles Roach was not quite so tall, but remarkably broad shouldered, lion built, and was the strongest man in his regiment.  The Nelsons were all mechanics and blacksmiths.  The Roaches were all mechanics and machinists.  Could do a
2 CONC nything they pleased with tools.  David Roach, my mother's oldest brother and Charles Roach's oldest son, put up the first cotton mill in Georgia.  He served his trade as a machinist in Raleigh, NC  The contract to put up the Geor
2 CONC gia Mill was his first job after serving his apprenticeship.  He married and lived in Georgia and continued to put up cotton mills and all kinds of machinery.  His family are now among the wealthy people of that state.  Charles Ro
2 CONC ach, my mother's half brother, a son by the first wife, could make anything he ever tried, but to put a hornet's nest together. He could make locks and keys, ear rings, finger rings, breast pins, and silver spoons as nice as you c
2 CONC an buy.  I have seen the spoons and have one of his rings. "Charles J. Nelson was my father's oldest brother.  He was twice a member of the North Carolina legislature.  He moved to Tennessee in the thirties and was elected to the 
2 CONC legislature of that state."   The MARY NELSON SMITH FAMILY REUNIONS have been held though out the years at St. John's Episcopal Church near Grifton, NC in Pitt Co.  It is 4 miles East of Grifton, just off Highway 118 (turn North o
2 CONC n cross roads to Stokestown, the church is l mile North.  From  Southern Sidelights - A Record of Personal Experience- Illustrated, a book by the Rev. W. E. Cox, 1942, Edwards &amp; Broughton Co., Raleigh, NC p. 99   " Chapter XIV
2 CONC   AN EMERGENCY SCHOOL-TEACHER   By the side of the road on the lower or south end of Grandfather's plantation was an old schoolhouse where, prior to the Civil War, was a neighborhood school.  After the war Grandfather and his neig
2 CONC hbors were impoverished, the slaves had been freed, the white men had to cultivate the farm while women had to do the cooking and housekeeping; and the schoolhouse remained closed.  At length Grandmother, who was a resolute and re
2 CONC sourceful woman, determined that her children should not grow up in ignorance.  She began teaching them at home; and, while continuing to do her house-work, she taught them daily for a few hours each morning and afternoon.   When 
2 CONC the neighbors learned of this they asked her to take their children, and she finally moved her school to the old schoolhouse.  It was here that I got my first schooling. Some pupils, girls as well as boys, walked three miles to th
2 CONC at school every morning, and the same distance back every afternoon. "Grandmother was not at that time equipped to do much more than teach the three R's, with Grammar, Geography and United States History added, but she was thoroug
2 CONC h and she made us master the fundamentals of what she did teach.  So thoroughly did she ground us in such elemental things as the Addition, Subtraction, Multiplication, and Division Tables; also the various tables of weights and m
2 CONC easures, that to this day I can recite them as I recited them to her sixty-odd years ago.  She was equally insistent on thoroughness in spelling and writing.  We used Webster's blue-back spelling book, which started us with the le
2 CONC tters of the alphabet, then words of two letters, followed by words of three letters, going steadily on to the longest of words.* *  * A retired educator, John G. Elliot, came to live with the Smiths and to prepare Grandmother for
2 CONC  the work of teaching advanced classes. * * * Mr. Elliot graduated at the University of NC with the class of 1822.  He was nearly 80 years of age when he came to live with Grandfather and Grandmother and was 'peculiar,' with white
2 CONC  hair and beard which he kept close-cropped with blunt pointed kindergarten scissors carried in his pocket for the purpose, and was regarded as an eccentric old bachelor.* * * *   p. 104 She prepared all her unmarried daughters fo
2 CONC r college in these courses and has been called 'a Pioneer Pitt County Educator,' for the reason that she pioneered in putting in the public schools courses of high school grade.  * * * Two of those pupils later became her sons-in-
2 CONC law, and one of them who studied surveying directly under Mr. Elliot, became a practical surveyor and for many years surveyed timber lands for the Beaufort County Lumber Company in the several counties where that company operated.
2 CONC  "The increasing number of people who wanted to come from other parts of the county to study under Grandmother and Mr. Elliott, moved Grandfather and Grandmother to build a new two-story schoolhouse near the end of the lane {pictu
2 CONC re p. 104} and open their home to boarding pupils.  The school was moved to the new schoolhouse in 1882, when I was in my 12th year.  I went to school there two years, and then the call of duty at home took me away."   p.108  " Wh
