2 CONT 8) ELIZABETH YANCEY BN: about 1783 in Mecklenburg County, Virginia HUSBAND: JOHN GRIFFIN married 1794 in Mecklenburg Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT 9) SUSANNAH YANCEY BN: about 1785 in Mecklenburg County, Virginia HUSBAND: JOHN WILLIAMSON married 1802 in Mecklenburg Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT 10) MILLY YANCEY BN: about 1787 in Mecklenburg County, Virginia HUSBAND: Mr. GRIFFIN
2 CONT
2 CONT 11) LUCY YANCEY BN: about 1789 in Mecklenburg County, Virginia HUSBAND: STARK DANIEL married 1818 in Mecklenburg Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: There exists no evidence of this Robert Yancey ever being in either Culpeper County, Virginia nor in the state of Kentucky. This Robert Yancey died and was buried (a marker still exists) in Mecklenburg County. It is evident 
2 CONC that this was not Captain Yancey as Captain Yancey died in 1824 in Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPTAIN ROBERT YANCEY OF VIRGINIA
2 CONT
2 CONT Military records of the Revolutionary War era record one Robert Yancey of Virginia playing an active role in the militia of Virginia during the period. He first entered the military in 1776 and by 1779 had been commissioned to the
2 CONC  office of Captain. He became an original member in the Society of the Cincinnati. This patriotic society was established in 1778 by various officers of the Continental Line. It's first president was General George Washington. Mem
2 CONC bership in this society was passed on from original member to oldest son and so on down to present generations. Various persons have also gained membership in the such patriotic organizations as the D.A.R. and S.A.R. by claiming d
2 CONC escent from this Captain Robert Yancey. The identity of this Robert Yancey, however, has been a point confusion and uncertainty for many researchers over the years and various researchers at different times have confused this Robe
2 CONC rt with at least four distinct and different Robert Yanceys found living in early America. I have written this report to clear up as much as possible the confusion surrounding this subject and once and for all establish the true i
2 CONC dentity of Captain Robert Yancey.
2 CONT
2 CONT I will first list below the personal information that can be extracted from the military files of Captain Robert Yancey and can be used in ascertaining his true identity. Military records provide the following information concerni
2 CONC ng Captain Robert Yancey.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) He was from the County of Culpeper in Virginia being born about the year 1750.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) He first entered the service in 1776. He served as cornet, quartermaster, and lieutenant in the Continental Dragoons. In 1779 he was commissioned as Captain. In May of 1781 he was taken prisoner by the British and remained so u
2 CONC ntil the end of the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) He had a nephew name Philemon Yancey Jr., son of Philemon Yancey Sr., who also served during the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) He was an original member of the Society of Cincinnati.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5) He moved to Kentucky at an early date and in 1818 was living in Woodford County where he applied for his Revolutionary War Pension.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) He lived as a widower for some time before his death and lived for a period with a widowed daughter and her children.
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) He died 17 November, 1824 in Franklin County, Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8) He had a son named Robert Yancey Jr. residing in Franklin County Kentucky at the time of his death who became the administrator of his estate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Four different and distinct Robert Yanceys' have, at different times and by different people, been asserted to be the same Captain Robert Yancey who served in the militia during the Revolutionary War. Information concerning these 
2 CONC four different Robert Yanceys follows and includes personal data and information concerning their families. This has been included in an attempt to compare this information with what is known from the military files of Captain Yan
2 CONC cey and thus ascertain which, if any of the four candidates is the true Captain Robert Yancey..
2 CONT
2 CONT The four Robert Yanceys are as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Col. Robert Leighton Yancey - son of Capt. Charles & Mary (Crawford) Yancey of Louisa County
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Robert Yancey - the son of Jeremiah and Margaret (Mullins) Yancey of Albemarle County
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Robert Yancey - the son of Richard and Mary (Bolling??) Yancey of Mecklenburg County.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) Robert Yancey - the son of Lewis Davis and Winifred (Kavanaugh) Yancey of Culpeper County.
2 CONT
