2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Mr. Roy Stanley,
2 CONT
2 CONT I am pleased to be able to tell you that my research on your behalf into the Stanley and associated families has been successful and has now reached the point where I can send you a report.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Elizabeth Finn I obtained copies of her letters to you dated 23 July 1999,
2 CONT
2 CONT 9 September 1999, 7 December 1999, 5 April 2000 and 7 August 2000 containing the results of her research into the Stanley family and I used the results of her research and the information contained in your Emails as the starting p
2 CONC oint for my own research.
2 CONT
2 CONT Firstly I tried to fill in some gaps on the tree. Robert Stanley, who heads the tree, evidently died intestate so I sought the record of the grant of administration of his estate in the Probate Act Book of the local probate court,
2 CONC  the Archdeaconry Court of Canterbury. I am mailing you a print-out of the page on which this appears. The entry is in Latin and I have provided a translation on the separate sheet enclosed.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are two points of interest here. Firstly, reference to a will of Robert Stanley, which does not now seem to survive. In his probate account his widow Ruth included the cost of taking out letters of administration of his esta
2 CONC te and there is no mention of probate of any will, so perhaps there was some irregularity about it so that it could not be proved and she had to apply for letters of administration instead.
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondly, we learn the occupation - joiner - of Richard Stanley, one of the people who took the inventory of Robert Stanley's goods. This bears out references to payments to him for woodworking amongst the Tenterden churchwardens'
2 CONC  accounts which Elizabeth Finn reported in her letter of 7 August 2000. The other bondsman was John Beare, blacksmith, and it was probably he who was another appraiser of Robert Stanley's inventory - so they were both in the metal
2 CONC -working business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Next, I looked at some published material for any mention of your ancestors, the Stanleys or Eridge/Uredge, starting with the index to Hugh Roberts' book 'Tenterden: The First Thousand Years', published in 1995. There proved to be
2 CONC  no entries for the latter surnames. For Stanley only:
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 130:
2 CONT
2 CONT William Standley, late miller of the town mill of Appledore and now miller of Tenterden Mill, presented by the churchwardens of Appledore in June 1628 for grinding on the sabbath day. Said that the inhabitants asked him to as the 
2 CONC wind was good that day and they had no corn to make bread.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 141:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Stanley was paid 10s for beating the drum at two musters held in 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the sections about emigration in the book mention was made only of the Tilden and Skeets families who went to New England. I refer to the Tildens again later in this report.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also checked the personal names index to the Calendar of the White and Black Books of the Cinque Ports 1432-1955, being Minutes of meetings of the Cinque Ports Confederation - Tenterden was a limb of Rye, one of the five origina
2 CONC l Cinque Ports. I found no entries for Standley/Stanley or Eridge/Uridge in the index.
2 CONT
2 CONT The publication 'Kent at Law 1602', an indexed compilation of all legal cases involving the county in that year, included some entries for the surname Stanley and variants, but not involving the Tenterden area or any entry for a R
2 CONC obert. There are no entries for Eridge/Uridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT My own publication of 'Kent Communicants Lists 1565-1567, Part 1', includes Tenterden but it is unfortunate that only the names of those not taking communion were recorded. It was noted that in 1565 there were 612 communicants (th
2 CONC ose aged 14 and above) in Tenterden, so it was a populous place. Part 1 also covers communicants in the parishes of Woodchurch, Appledore, Brookland, Burmarsh, Dymchurch, Ebony, Fairfield, Lympne, New Romney, Orlestone, Saltwood, 
2 CONC Stone and West Hythe but no-one surnamed Stan(d)ley or Eridge/Uridge were listed.
2 CONT
2 CONT I next tried an index at Canterbury Cathedral Archives, known as the XYZ index, of people who gave depositions at local church court cases, generally between about 1560 and 1640, They were typically asked their age, where they wer
2 CONC e born, where they had lived and for how long, so these records can be very useful for tracing the origins and movements of people at a time when this can be difficult to piece together. Of course, not everyone appeared before a c
2 CONC hurch court in their lives and it turned out there was just one Stan(d)ley in the index. John Stanley, beer brewer of Canterbury, gave a deposition on 3 July 1622. He was aged 49, had been born at Ripple and had lived in Canterbur
2 CONC y for 15 years. No obvious relation of yours. There were depositions by possibly two people named Robert Eridge, both millers of Buckland (next Dover), in 1599 (born Buckland, aged 30) and in 1600 (lived in Folkestone and Buckland
2 CONC , aged 40). Again, no connection with Tenterden.
2 CONT
2 CONT I now turned to the parish registers of Tenterden to check some information you gave me in your Email of 15 August and which is on the IGI. According to the IGI, Robert Stanley was baptised at Tenterden on 9 March 1571/2 (a date s
2 CONC uspiciously similar to that of John Stanley at Ashford with whom Elizabeth started her research), married there to Ruth on 1 June 1590 and died there on 31 May 1605. The IGI also states that Ruth was born in 1574 {exact date not g
2 CONC iven) in Tenterden and was buried at Tenterden on 10 September 1617. The date of Robert's death we know to be correct from the heading to his inventory, the other dates were yet to be proved.
2 CONT
2 CONT In fact, I could find none of these entries in the original registers of Tenterden. I searched the baptisms there on 9 March 1571, also 9 March 1572 (CKS: P364/1/1) but no-one was baptised on that date in either year. There was no
2 CONC  entry for the marriage of Robert Stanley to Ruth on 1 June 1590 - I suspected this might be a fabrication anyway because Ruth's maiden surname was not given in the IGI but maiden surnames were given in the Tenterden parish regist
2 CONC er. There was no entry for the burial of Ruth on 10 September 1617. I decided to search the register of burials myself and extracted all for Stanley and Eridge/Uridge from 1605 up to 1660, with results on the sheet enclosed.
2 CONT
2 CONT It looked as though Richard Stanley, the joiner, was married to Isabell and both died before the other Stanleys emigrated. I looked for a will or admon for Richard in case there was any mention of Robert but found no will or admin
2 CONC istration in the Archdeaconry Court of Canterbury, the local probate court, and no will in the Prerogative Court of Canterbury, which had superior jurisdiction for probate purposes over the south of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT I now turned my attention to seeking the marriage of Robert Stanley to Ruth. Elizabeth's search of Tenterden parish register and the Weald of Kent Marriage Index to 1601 had drawn a blank. I tried the Sussex Marriage Index, which 
2 CONC has been published since she did her research, and there found:
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Stanle and Ruth Garland married 17 December 1588 - Heathfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heathfield is about 18 miles west of Tenterden, as the crow flies. The original, very small, village is now known as Old Heathfield to differentiate it from the modern town of Heathfield, one mile away, which grew with the railway
2 CONC . I am mailing you some information about Old Heathfield church, where the marriage took place, and the parish. Interestingly, its vicar, Robert Hunt, sailed in the spring of 1607 as chaplain to the expedition which established th
2 CONC e first permanent English settlement at Jamestown, Virginia, but he died that first winter of 1607-8.
2 CONT
2 CONT Looking back at the probate inventory for Robert Stanley, I noticed that two of the appraisers were Timothy Garland and John Garland and wondered if they might be relations of Ruth while Richard Stanley, another appraiser, was a r
2 CONC elative of Robert. A test of the relevance of this marriage would be to seek the baptisms of daughters Ruth and Patience at Heathfield, so I did so.
2 CONT
2 CONT The early parish registers of Heathfield, which start in 1581, turned out to be quite badly damaged and difficult to read, but I searched the register of baptisms for any children of Robert Stanley, with the following results:
2 CONT
2 CONT East Sussex Record Office, Lewes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Heathfield baptisms 1589-1600 for children of Robert Stanley:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1590 Nov 8 Ruth Stanle dau of Robert Stanlee.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1593 Sep 21 Freegift son of Robert Stanlee.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1595 Apr 27 Patyence dau of Robert Stanlee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finding the baptisms of Ruth and Patience proved that this was indeed your ancestral family and I had found the right marriage. Their son Freegift probably died young as there was no mention of him in Robert's probate account of 1
2 CONC 605 and I could now go on to seek his burial.
2 CONT
2 CONT The use of the name Freegift is interesting, as it was one of the names which the more extreme Puritans of the time gave to their children. Patience was another. I am mailing you copies of some pages from 'First Names First', by L
2 CONC eslie Alan Dunkling, published in 1977, which is a general history of first names. Of course, it was the Puritans who first set up colonies in America. Perhaps we now get an inkling that your emigrant ancestors held Puritan belief
2 CONC s, which may be why the three sons of Robert and Ruth were fairly early emigrants in 1633. You may remember that the 1605 inventory of Robert's goods included two Bibles.
2 CONT
2 CONT As Elizabeth mentioned in her letter of 5 April 2000, there was a flourishing iron industry in the Weald of Kent at this time and Heathfield is in that area, so it all fits.
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears that some time between April 1595 and October 1598 the Stanley family moved from Heathfield to Tenterden. I don't know yet why they would have done so but possibly Robert had business or religious interests in Tenterden
2 CONC . It may be a coincidence, but the Tildens of Tenterden were also Puritans, several of whom emigrated to New England. Brothers Thomas went in 1623 and Nathaniel in March 1635 and another brother, Joseph, moved to London, assisted 
2 CONC in fitting out the 'Mayflower' and was one of the Gentlemen Adventurers who helped in financing the Puritans in the new colony. The father of these three brothers was named Thomas Tilden and by his third wife he had a son Freegift
2 CONC , born around the same time as Freegift Stanley.
2 CONT
2 CONT I next examined a listing of memorial inscriptions inside Tenterden church and in the churchyard, which was published in 1919. However, I found no entries for any Stanley, Eridge/Uridge or Garland. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the first stages of my research I had spotted the marriage of Robert Stanley and Ruth Garland as being a possible, so while searching the register of burials at Tenterden 1605-1660 I had also searched for the surname Garland, b
2 CONC ut there were no burials in that surname. There are no entries for the surname Garland in the index to Hugh Roberts' book about Tenterden. Finally, I searched the index to Archdeaconry Court of Canterbury wills, both registered an
2 CONC d unregistered, 1449-1712, but found no entry for any Garland of Tenterden or for any Timothy or John Garland.
2 CONT
2 CONT This brings you up to date with the results of my research, but there is plenty of scope for further research. I could continue by looking at burials at Heathfield and whether there are any wills for Stanleys or Garlands amongst t
2 CONC he Sussex wills and administrations. I could also extend the search of the burials at Tenterden up to 1695 or so as it is still not firmly established whether the Ruth Stanley who married Humphrie Uredge in Tenterden in 1605 was t
2 CONC he widow of Robert or his daughter. There is also the fate of Patience, baptised 1595 and alive in 1605, to ascertain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Centre for Kentish Studies, Maidstone:
2 CONT
2 CONT P364/1/1 - Tenterden burials 1605-1660 for Stanley and variants and Eridge/Uridge and variants:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1605 Jun 1 Robte Standley.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1607 Apr 1 Richard Earidge.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1612 Sep 2 [name faded] wife to Humphrie Earidge.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1614 Jun 25 Tymothy Standley.
2 CONT
2 CONT " Feb 3 Isabell Standley wife to Richard Standley.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1616 Jul 10 Samuell Eridge: & Robert Hovenden drowned in Pysinge pond.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1625 Jun 22 Thomas Urige.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1629 May 15 Richard Standley.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1632 Dec 12 Elizabeth wife of John Stanley.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1639 Jun 29 A child of Edward Stanley unbaptized.
2 CONT
2 CONT " Jan 1 Anne wife of Willia' Stanly.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1644 Oct 26 William Standly.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1654 Feb 26 Rebeccah wife of Joseph Stand[rest of surname lost in binding].
2 CONT
2 CONT Centre for Kentish Studies, Maidstone:
2 CONT
2 CONT PRC 3/26/122.
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 June 1605.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the same day before the said substitute on the part of Ralph Bailes, notary public.
2 CONT
2 CONT On which day at the petition of Mr. Norwood issuing from the Commission of Messrs George Elye, vicar of Tenterden, William Reade, vicar of Rolvinden, and William Lawse, rector of Halden, together and separately to hear one Ruth St
2 CONC andley executrix of the will of Robert Standley late of Tenterden in the Archdiocese of Canterbury deceased in and concerning the truth and validity of the will of the deceased and to order etc. within a month by commission of the
2 CONC  aforesaid Master George Elye on the 10th day of the month of June, John Dunkin and Obadiah Elye etc. being bound with the same Richard Standley of Tenterden aforesaid, joiner, and John Beare of the same place, blacksmith, in £100
2 CONC  to produce an inventory of the aforesaid deceased to the Commission which was that same day extended, to the sum of £52 12s 1d.
2 CONT
2 CONT ______________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT Church records for Tenterden, Kent, England (a few miles southwest of Ashford), may have answered the question of where, and from whom, the "Hartford" Stanley line is descended.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robart Standley was a glazier who moved from Tonbridge to Tenterden about 1590, probably to take advantage of the flourishing iron trade there. Tenterden had a population of 357 in 1663, so it was probably about the same size, or 
2 CONC smaller, six decades earlier. Robart was a yeoman and doing rather well. When he died (31 May 1605) the inventory of his goods alone had a value of 51 Pounds. He had a servant, Thomas Glover, and small "whete" farm (which, with hi
2 CONC s work place, were not incuded in the 51 Pounds).
2 CONT
2 CONT The farm had hens and geese, swine, sheep, three kine and a calf. The shop had iron and steel, a pair of bellows and an anvil. In Tenterden he was a "whitesmith." This is defined as "a worker in white iron (tin-plate), a tinsmith,
2 CONC  or, one who polishes or finishes metal goods, as distinguished from one who forges them."
2 CONT
2 CONT Robart married Ruthe, and they had John, Thomas, Timothy (the brother's who emigrated to America, two of whom were among the founders of Hartford, CT.), Patience, Robart, William and Ruthe. On 16 September 1605, just four months a
2 CONC fter Robarts death, Ruthe married one Humphrie Uredge.
2 CONT
2 CONT John, who was just six at the time of his mothers second marriage, later apparently married Humphrie's daughter, step-daughter or other relative, Elysabeth Uredge, on 14 October 1623 at Benenden (about five miles from Tenterden), 
2 CONC (not unlike his son, John (the third "Hartford" Stanley founder), who, orphaned at ten during the voyage to America, grew up with Sara Scott next door and married her when they both came of age - proximity was apparently a door to
2 CONC  affection). The church records from Tenterden also carry a burial of Elizabeth, wife of John Stanley on 12 Dec 1632. (*)
2 CONT
2 CONT Other Standleys are cited in local records as having children in the 1564-1580 period. So far their relationship to our family is unknown, although the reoccurring names of Robart, John and Tymothy do suggest a familial tie-in.
2 CONT
