2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT JOHN STANLEY was born 9 March 1572, in England, to Peter Stanley (1539-1583) and Joane Masterson (1542-1579.) He married Ruth Garland, 17 December 1588, at Heathfield. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Heathfield is about 18 miles west of Tenterden, as the crow flies. The original, very small village is now known as Old Healthfield to differentiated it from the modern town of Heathfield, one mile away, which grew with the railwa
2 CONC y. The Parish and old Heathfield church still stands. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Stanley was a Yeoman and Whitesmith (white metal, tinplate worker.) Robert Standley was a glazier who moved from Tonbridge to Tenterden about 1590, probably to take advantage of the flourishing iron trade there. Tenterden h
2 CONC ad a population of 357 in 1663, so it was probably about the same size, or smaller, six decades earlier. Robart was a yeoman and doing rather well. 
2 CONT
2 CONT When he died (31 May 1605) the inventory of his goods alone had a value of 51 Pounds. He had a servant, Thomas Glover, and small "whete" farm (which, with his work place, were not incuded in the 51 Pounds). The farm had hens and g
2 CONC eese, swine, sheep, three kine and a calf. The shop had iron and steel, a pair of bellows and an anvil. In Tenterden he was a "whitesmith." This is defined as "a worker in white iron (tin-plate), a tinsmith, or, one who polishes o
2 CONC r finishes metal goods, as distinguished from one who forges them." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert Stanley married Ruth Garland 17d 12m 1588 at Heathfield, Tenterden Parish, Kentshire, England. They had John, Thomas, Timothy (the brother's who emigrated to America, two of whom were among the founders of Hartford, CT.), P
2 CONC atience, Robart, William, and Ruthe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 16 September 1605, just four months after Robert’s death, Ruthe married one Humphrie Uredge. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John, who was just six at the time of his mother’s second marriage, later apparently married Humphrie's daughter, step-daughter or other relative, Elysabeth Uredge, on 14 October 1623, at Benenden (about five miles from Tenterden.
2 CONC ) Not unlike his son, John the third "Hartford" Stanley founder, who, orphaned at ten during the voyage to America, grew up with Sara Scott next door and married her when they both came of age - proximity was apparently a door to 
2 CONC affection. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The church records from Tenterden also carry a burial of Elizabeth, wife of John Stanley, on 12 Dec 1632. Robert Stanley died 1 June 1605, in Tenterden, Kent, England. and was buried 1d 6m 1605 at Tenterden, Tenterden Parish, Kent
2 CONC shire, England, as a young father, age 34. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Probate Act Book of the local probate court, The Archdeaconry Court of Centerbury: Originally written in Latin. There are two points of interest here. Firstly, reference to a will of Robert Stanley, which does not seem to survive.
2 CONC  In his probate account his widow Ruth included the cost of taking out letters of administration of his estate an there is no mention of probate of any will, so perhaps there was some irregularity about it so that it could not be 
2 CONC proved and she had to apply for letters of administration instead. Secondly, we learn the occupation – joiner – of Richard Stanley, one of the people who took the inventory of Robert Stanley’s goods. This bears out references to p
2 CONC ayments to him for woodworking amongst the Tenterden churchwardens’ accounts. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Gilliam Rickard has researched the origins of the three Stanley brothers who immigrated to Connecticut: Thomas of Hartford, Timothy of Hartford, and John who died at sea in route to Connecticut. They were definitely the sons of Ro
2 CONC bert & Ruth Garland Stanley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT RUTH GARLAND was born about 1574, of Kent, England, to Timothy Garland and Unknown. Apparently she was not from Tenterden until her marriage to Robert Stanley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She married (1) *Robert Stanley, 17 December 1588, at Heathfield. Heathfield is about 18 miles west of Tenterden, as the crow flies. The original, very small village is now known as Old Healthfield to differentiated it from the mo
2 CONC dern town of Heathfield, one mile away, which grew with the railway. (2) Humphrie Uredge, 16 September 1605. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ruth Garland passed away 10 September 1617, in Tenterden, Kent, England, at age 43. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Robert Stanley and Ruth Garland: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Ruth Stanley b. 8 Nov 1590 (Heathfield) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Freegift b. 21 Sep 1593 (Heathfield) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.William Stanley b. 1593; d. 26 Oct 1644 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4.Patience Stanley b. 27 Apr 1595 (Heathfield) 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.Robert Stanley b. 8 Oct 1599; d. 23 Mar 1634 
2 CONT
2 CONT 6.John Stanley was born 28 December 1603, Ashford, Kent, England, to Robert Stanley (1572-1605) and Ruth Garland (1569-1605.) He married Elizabeth Uredge (Ingleden,) 18 May 1624, and had three children. His wife died 12 December 1
2 CONC 632, in England, and he passed away in 24 May 1634, in route to New England on the Frances, leaving three children. Two were raised by his brothers and the infant must have died upon arrival. Thomas, John’s brother, adopted John S
2 CONC tanley. Timothy, John’s brother, adopted Ruth Stanley. John Stanley died 24 May 1634, in route to New England, age 44. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 7.Thomas Stanley b. 22 Apr 1601; d. 31 Jan 1662/63 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.Timothy Stanley b. 1 Apr 1604; d. 30 Apr 1648 (Note: Gillian Richard found the baptisms of the following in Heathfield: 1.Ruth Stanle dau of Robert Stanlee. 1590 Nov 8. 2.Freegift son of Robert Stanlee. 1593 Sep 21 3.Patyence da
2 CONC u of Robert Stanlee. 1595 Apr 27 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their son, Freegift, probably died young as there was no mention of him in Robert’s probate account of 1605. The use of the name Freegift is interesting as it was one of the names which the more extreme Puritans of the time gave t
2 CONC o their children. Patience was another. Of course, it was the Puritans who first set up colonies in America. Perhaps we now get an inkling that your emigrant ancestors held Puritan beliefs, which may be why the three sons of Rober
2 CONC t and Ruth were fairly early emigrants in 1633. Note that the 160t inventory of Robert’s goods included two Bibles. It appears that some time between April 1595 and October 1598 the Stanley Family moved from Heathfield to Tenterde
2 CONC n. Tenterden burials 1605-1660: Robte Standley. 1605 June 1 Elizabeth wife of John Stanley. 1632 Dec. 12.)
