2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/15088994/thomas-stanley
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Stanley s/o John Stanley & Susan Lancocke
2 CONT
2 CONT He came from England in the ship Planter to Lynn, MA in 1635, removed to Hartford in 1636, and in 1659 removed to Hadley, MA. Freeman in MA 4 March 1635, and a member of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company in 1640. He was 
2 CONC Deputy to the General Court from Lynn in 1635 when the removal to Connecticut was decided upon. He was an original proprietor at Hartford and his home lot in 1639 was on what is now Main Street just north of Little River, extendin
2 CONC g to the present Center Church. Was constable in Hartford in 1644, 48, and 53.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas married Bennet Tritton on August 3, 1630 in Ashford, Kent, England. To this union, 5 children were born:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary
2 CONT Hannah
2 CONT Violet
2 CONT Nathaniel
2 CONT Sarah
2 CONT
2 CONT An original proprietor of Hartford, his name is on the Founders Monument. He probably came to America with his brothers, John & Timothy, he was a freeman in Mass. on March 4, 1634-5 and settled in Lynn. He was a deputy & constable
2 CONC  there, but soon removed to Hartford. His homelot in 1639 was on Main Street just north of the Little River extending to Center Church. He was also a constable in Hartford. In 1659, he removed to Hadly and was a townsman there.
2 CONT He married Benet Shepard, daughter of John and Rebecca (Waller) Shepard.
2 CONT (Families of Early Hartford, Connecticut, p. 566)
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Planters of the Commonwealth of Massachusetts 1620-1640" by Charles Edward Banks (1930) is a terrific source of info.  It is a compilation of the ships and passenger lists for that 20 year period.  There is a Thomas Stanley i
2 CONC n 1634 from Ashford, County Kent, who arrived in Boston in May with 8 other Stanleys, including his brothers John and Timothy.  The name of the ship cannot be identified with any certainty.  The ship Planter was one of 6 ships tha
2 CONC t arrived that May and of those 6 only about 20 passengers are identified, per John Winthrop's Journal.  Winthrop lists the ships but doesn't cross reference with passengers.  The ages of the passengers are not listed in this case
2 CONC .  The Thomas Stanley referenced in 1635 is given an age of 16 and listed as a servant with no mention of his point of origin and he is the only Stanley among the very large passenger list for the Planter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Information received from sassa (member #47050526) on 7 Nov. 2015:
2 CONT "1)Thomas married Bennet Tritton on August 3, 1630 in Ashford, Kent, England. To this union, 5 children were born
2 CONT 2)He married Benet Shepard, daughter of John and Rebecca (Waller) Shepard.
2 CONT (Families of Early Hartford, Connecticut, p. 566
2 CONT I believe the correct notation is Bennett Tritton, daughter of Daniel Tritton and Alice Goldhatch Dayton."
