2 CONT
2 CONT Inscription: HERE LYETH Ye BODY OF TIMOTHY STANLY WHO DEPARTED THIS LIFE April 1648 Aged 45.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: This is a small stone with a cross in front.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: Ancient Burying Ground  Hartford Hartford County Connecticut, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: Nareen, et al Record added: Jul 04, 2005  Find A Grave Memorial# 11290528
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ==Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT For the most recent research see [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/american-genealogist-the/image/?pageName=221&volumeId=13263 Leslie Mahler (2005) TAG 80:219] and [https://www.americanancestors.org/databases/great-migr
2 CONC ation-immigrants-to-new-england-1634-1635-volume-vi-r-s/image/?pageName=462&filterQuery=category:Great%20Migration%20Study%20Project Great Migration 2:6:462-66]. He was son of Robert and Ruth (Garland) Stanley. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Timothy Stanley''', bp 1 Apr 1604 in Tenterden, Kent, England came to New England in May, 1634, in the same ship with William Pantry, Samuel  Greenhill, Simon Willard, etc.; his wife, Elizabeth, and son, Timothy, came with him,
2 CONC  and perhaps one or two older children. Settled in Cambridge; freeman, March 4, 1634-5. Removed to Hartford in 1636; an original proprietor; his home lot in 1639 was on the west.side of Front St., near the road to the landing, the
2 CONC  second lot north of the present State St.; juror, 1641; townsman, 1643; he died April, 1648, age 45; inventory £332. 18. 10. His widow, Elizabeth, married, in 1661, Andrew Bacon, who removed to Hadley. She returned to Hartford af
2 CONC ter his death in 1669, and lived with her son, Caleb; died in Hartford, February 23, 1679, age about 76.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children:===
2 CONT
2 CONT i Joseph
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. Timothy, born Jan. 1633; died young,
2 CONT
2 CONT ii. Elizabeth, married Mark Sension (St John), of Norwalk.
2 CONT
2 CONT iii. Abigail, married Samuel Cowles, of Farmington.
2 CONT
2 CONT iv. Caleb, born March, 1642; lived on his father's homestead; married (1) about 1665, Hannah, daughter of John Cowles; she died Feb., 1690, and he married (2) Sarah, widow of Zachary Long, of Charlestown; shed. August 30, 1698; ma
2 CONC rried (3) 1699, Lydia Wilson, who survived him; constable, 1667, 1675; townsman, 1671, 1678,1682,1687, 1690; deputy, five or six times; Assistant, 1691-1700; in 1694, a Commissioner to Albany, with Col. Allyn, on negotiations with
2 CONC  the Indians; in 1689, Capt. Caleb Stanley, Mr. Ciprian Niccolls, Ensign Nathaniel Stanley, Capt. John Stanley, and Mr. James Treat were appointed a Council of Safety with the Governor and Assistants. He died May 6, 1718. His son,
2 CONC  Caleb, was "Mr. Secretary Stanley;" and also "the first regular surveyor with a compass that resided in any town upon the Connecticut."
2 CONT
2 CONT v. Lois, born August 23,1645; married Deacon Thomas Porter, of Farmington.
2 CONT
2 CONT vi. Isaac, born March 10, 1647-8; settled in Hadley; married Mary ____; died  (without children.) September 23, 1671. Ruth Stanley, Timothy's adopted daughter, married December 5, 1645, Isaac Moore, of Hartford, Norwalk, and Farmi
2 CONC ngton; died in Farmington, May 26, 1691.
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE:'' James Hammond Trumbull, editor, ''The memorial history of Hartford County, Connecticut, 1633-1884'', Volume 1 (Boston, Massachusetts: Edward L. Osgood, 1886), pages 260-261. Retrieved: 3 May 2011 from [http://books.go
2 CONC ogle.com/books?id=B18EAAAAYAAJ Google Books]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Everything that is recorded of him indicates that he was a man of dignity, good substance and piety, worthy of the sincere respect of his numerous descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT The arms of the Stanley family in Kent:
2 CONT Argent, on a bend azure, three bucks' heads cabossed or, a chief gules, Crest, a demi-heraldic wolf, erase, Argent, tripled, Or. (Stanley genealogy, 1887.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Timothy Stanley received 68 acres of land at Hartford as his portion, according to the sum paid by him, which was twentieth in size out of ninety-five allotments, which indicates that he was one of the wealthier men of the settlem
2 CONC ent. He subsequently purchased land at the new settlement, Farmington, but did not reside there 
2 CONT
2 CONT Probably descended from John Stanley of Wilmington, County Kent, a younger son of the family in the County of Lancaster, a kinsman of Thomas Stanley, first Earl of Derby, and his descendants to the present day
2 CONT
