1 NOTE
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY: The Descendants of Rev. Thomas Hooker, Hartford, Connecticut
2 CONT By Edward Hooker - Commander, U.S.N.
2 CONT Edited by Margaret Huntington Hooker and printed for
2 CONT Her at Rochester, N. Y. 1909  Page 123 - 124
2 CONT    Capt. John Standley, the father of Mrs. Hooker, was a man of wealth and high social position, who had won distinction as a lieutenant and captain in the Indian wars.
2 CONT    Standley. The early settlers at Hartford spelled the name with a "d," and as they were educated men of high standing, etc., it may properly supposed that they were correct in so spelling. The history of the Stanley family howev
2 CONC er assumes that they were wrong and the spelling a mistake, etc., and endeavors to trace the family to the family of Stanley of England. It has since come to light that Sir John Standley was an officer in Cromwell's army, that the
2 CONC  family was then an old one and was not known t have any connection with the famous Stanley family. Probably this Standley family was the one from which the Connecticut settler came and their spelling of the name was correct. The 
2 CONC name in America has now become irrevocably changed to Stanley.
2 CONT    Another daughter, Elizabeth Standley, married Deacon John North, and was the grand-mother of Col. Jeremiah Wadsworth the famous Commissary General of the Revolutionary War. Col. Wadsworth married Mahitable Russell of Middletown
2 CONC , Conn., a descendant from Rev. Thomas Hooker, and thus while there was no cousinship between Col. Wadsworth and his wife there was a cousinship between each of them and the descendants from Hon. John Hooker and their children bor
2 CONC e a double relationship to this branch of the Hooker family.
2 CONT    Col. Wadsworth, son of Daniel Wadsworth, founded the Wadsworth Atheneum at Hartford and presented it to the city, thus erecting a noble monument to the Wadsworth family, and one in which the Hookers of that branch have a kinshi
2 CONC p interest.
