1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Owning 1300 acres, Miles G. Dobbins was one of the original settlers of Spalding County, although at the time of purchase his property was part of Henry County. (He is listed as one of several parties located on lo
2 CONC t numbers 129 and 160  in the third district of Henry County. Spalding County was created in 1851.) Dobbins was a slaveowner, listed in the 1850 Henry County Slave Census as owning 6 slaves. Napoleon Epps Blanton, who went on to b
2 CONC ecome a prominent educator, was one of Dobbins' slaves as a very young child. He calls Dobbins' "a great and good man" in "The History of the American Negro and His Institutions" (A. B. Caldwell Publishing, Atlanta, GA, 1917). Bla
2 CONC nton returned to work for Dobbins "in the mountains of North Georgia" in order to save enough money to enter Atlanta University. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dobbins became a banker prior to the Civil War. In 1857, he was one of the incorporators of the Savings Bank of Griffin. In 1858, he became a trustee of Marshall College. Dobbins was president of the Planter's and Farmer's Bank in
2 CONC  Cartersville, the first bank in Bartow County, chartered on July 1, 1872, with capital of $100,000. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Heard County for 1850 census and Bartow County for 1880 census. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Moved to Atlanta, where he became a "one of the most prominent bankers in Atlanta," according to the Reed, author of "History of Atlanta."
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1884, Dobbins was a founding partner in Bartow Mining & Manufacturing, along with his son, Miles and John D. Cunningham, Jr.
2 CONT
2 CONT A posthumous note about a family reunion in the Courant American (a Cartersville, Georgia newspaper) on Dec. 22, 1898, refers to Dobbins as "Col. Miles G. Dobbins." The reunion was held in the home of Mrs. John D. Cunningham in At
2 CONC lanta. Those in attendance were Mrs. Mary D. Freeman, of Cartersville, Mrs. George A. Veach, of Adairsville, Mr. Miles Dobbins Jr. of Cartersville, and Mrs. Cunningham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for further research: The 1850 Heard County, Georgia, census shows the following individuals living in Miles Dobbins' home, in addition to his wife and children: W.L. Gordon, 40, b Ga.; F.E. Gordon, 14, b S.C.; A.R. Gordon, 
2 CONC b Ga.; F. Stanley, 20, b Ga; and
2 CONT
2 CONT F. Butler, 17, b Ga. "F. Butler" is probably a relative of Dobbins' wife, Susan. "F. Stanley" may belong to Dobbins' maternal line (the name "Standley" was changed to "Stanley" by family members in Georgia). The link to the Gordon
2 CONC  family is unknown, but Rebecca B. Gordon, buried in McDonough Memorial Cemetery in Henry County, Georgia, is listed as the wife of W. L. Gordon. Her birth date (5/28/1812) and death date (5/27/1846) would have left W. L. Gordon a
2 CONC  widower by the time of the census and may be why he took up residence with Dobbins. The link between the Gordon family and the Dobbins/Butler marriage is unknown; however, the deceased wife, Rebecca, may have been a Butler, due t
2 CONC o the middle initial on her tombstone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: http://files.usgwarchives.org/al/clay/cemeteries/ebeneezer.txt: http://books.google.com/books?id=IpgO3_OF724C&pg=RA5-PA17&lpg=RA5-PA17&dq=cornelia+dobbins+cunningham&source=bl&ots=xRm6g99A8x&sig=pkEn5A9_CDYD5rBwyHUwkyERg6
2 CONC s&hl=en&sa=X&oi=book_result&resnum=1&ct=result#PRA5-PA17,M1; http://www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~gaspald2/tax/1853africa.htm; http://www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~gaclayto/census/1850slaveownerscensus.htm; http://www.archive.org/stream
2 CONC /historyofamerica01cald/historyofamerica01cald_djvu.txt; http://www.gabartow.org/News/CA/newsCA.1898.1222.shtml; http://www.usgennet.org/usa/ga/county/fulton/bartowhistory/pg%20201-300red.pdf; http://www.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~gas
2 CONC pald2/history/goodrich.htm; http://searches.rootsweb.ancestry.com/usgenweb/archives/ga/henry/cemeteries/cemetery3/mcdonough.txt.
