2 CONT This is the Richard Woody who married Mary Ann Hale. There are several Richard Woodys. I'm in the process of visiting family genealogy Web sites to unravel which is which. Thanks for your patience!
2 CONT
2 CONT The Virginia Tidewater - " One of the earliest chronicles of the Woody name in Virginia can be found in court records of Lower Norfolk which was situated in the heart of the famous Tidewater region. On August 15, 1653, a Robert Wo
2 CONC oddy, age about thirty-two, testified about an incident that occurred on a ship anchored in the Elizabeth River on December 15, 1651. On April 23, 1681, a Henry Woody is mentioned in the head right claim of ship Captain Arthur Jor
2 CONC dan. To encourage settlement of America, the English government provided parcels of land to ship captains who transported immigrants from Europe. From 1682 - 1786, the Vestry Books of St. Peter's Parish and St. Paul's Parish recor
2 CONC d a succession of Woody landowners in New Kent and Hanover counties. In the records, the given names of John, Martha, Micajah, Simon, James, Anthony, Henry, Robert, and Samuel Woody appear with some frequency; however, I know of n
2 CONC o way to determine how these individuals were related. Although I have seen a lengthy undocumented pedigree published on the internet that extends the Henry Woody line back to the 1600s, I have been unable to confirm any of the re
2 CONC lationships, I welcome any evidence related to this pedigree. During this period, many of the Woodys slowly moved west. Apparently, some were converted to the Quaker faith. The 1739 - 1759 minutes of the Henrico Monthly Meeting re
2 CONC late the tribulations of the family of Micajah Woody. Also, the 1686 Register of St. Peter's Parish mentions that the name of James Woody appears frequently in Quaker records. { Source: Woody Family Roots, Hosted by Dave Woody - l
2 CONC ocated on internet at - freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~dwoody/woody/index.htm - I picked up in 2004} 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
