2 CONT
2 CONT Fanning Richardson Jones* 1866–1952
2 CONT
2 CONT James Wesley Jones* 1870–1952
2 CONT
2 CONT George Frazier Jones* 1875–1947 	
2 CONT Mitchell Weatherly Jones* 1877–1947
2 CONT
2 CONT 	 Silas Lafayette Jones* 1879–1960
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Hicks Jones* 1882–1967
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was born and grew up in the Big Hungry section in the Upward community of Henderson County. On Nov. 26, 1869, he bought property from his parents. Over the years, he bought 525 acres in the Big Hungry area and, later, 15 acres 
2 CONC along Upward Road in the Upward community. He sold land and water rights to R.M. Oates, who constructed the first power plant in Henderson County. He owned a water-powered sawmill and grist mill and furnished lumber for many homes
2 CONC  in the county. The family lived in a log cabin for several years. He later built a weather-boarded house close to the Big Hungry River near a spring. He was one of the early trustees of the Upward United Methodist Church. About 1
2 CONC 911, he sold his Big Hungry property and moved to the Upward community along Upward Road. The following are excerpts from a story published in the Times-News after his funeral: "A large and impressive gathering of friends and rela
2 CONC tives paid final tribute to the memory of George W. Jones, of Upward yesterday afternoon with funeral services in the Methodist church yard there... He was the builder of the little church at Upward, and having professed faith ear
2 CONC ly in life was one of the first members in that congregation." This refers to Upward United Methodist Church.
