2 CONT Kentucky Deaths, 1900 - 1999  MyHeritage  Death certificates represent one of the key primary sources for family information, typically being issued within days of a death and having many details about a persons' life. Frequently,
2 CONC  they contain age, birthplace, parents' names and birthplaces and the cause of death.  Collection  10046  MH:S500038 
2 CONT === GEDCOM Source ===
2 CONT https://www.myheritage.com/research/record-10046-2232332/wesley-g-thomas-in-kentucky-deaths  4  29 MAY 2017  Wesley G ThomasBirth: Circa 1860Residence: Trigg, Kentucky, USADeath: Feb 5 1946 - Trigg, Kentucky, USACertificate: 05250
2 CONC State volume: 11Officer volume: 46 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Wesley Gunn Thomas, 86, a highly respected farmer and  longtime resident of the Oak Grove community, died in Cadiz at the home of his daughter, Mrs. Peachie Terrell on Tuesday morning, February 3, 1946. He had been in poor health 
2 CONC for several years.  Known throughout Trigg County as "Cousin Wes," he was a great-grandson of James and Mary "Mollie" Standley Thomas, who migrated to Trigg County from North Carolina and settled on Donaldson Creek in about 1806.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Thomas was born April 1, 1859 and was one of eleven children born to Stanley and Emily J. Light Thomas.  On March 17, 1881, he was married to Eliza S. Henderson, daughter of John and Susan Larkins Henderson of Caldwell County,
2 CONC  with whom he had ten children. A deeply religious man, he was of the primitive Baptist faith. He and his wife raised their ten children on an 150-acre farm on Birds Creek not far from Oak Grove Baptist Church.  She passed away in
2 CONC  March, 1936.
2 CONT
2 CONT Those who knew him always mention in particular--his remarkable memory, especially about his family.  He had an ability to recall who descended from what branch of the large Thomas clan, and could even recall individual dates of b
2 CONC irth.  In 1928, when G.A. Bridges and Maston Thomas put together the first history of the Thomas and Bridges families, they relied heavily on "Cousin Wes" for assistance. Additionally, many in Oak Grove and nearby communities soug
2 CONC ht his assistance in verifying their birth when they applied for birth certificates. He was also well known for his love of  Southern Harmony hymns.  He traveled throughout Western Kentucky to "all day singings." For many years, t
2 CONC he main highlight of his year was to attend the "Big Singing" (of Southern Harmony hymns), a ritual that has been held annually at the Marshall County Courthouse in Benton since 1884.  
2 CONT
2 CONT His children were Mattie Pearl Thomas Hughes, Hazie L Thomas., Eura Wesley Thomas, Leslie Edwin Thomas, Annie Thomas Bridges, Peachie Thomas Terrell, James Stone Thomas, Lucien M. Thomas, Luther Thompson Thomas, and Berta Thomas F
2 CONC rancis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Funeral services were conducted on Wednesday, February 4th, 1946 at Dyer's Chapel Methodist Church with burial in the nearby Lawrence Cemetery.  Pallbearers included Hershel Thomas, Hugh Thomas, Elzie Light, Allen Lawrence, Claren
2 CONC ce Bruce and D. Bruce.
2 CONT
2 CONT (Biographical information from obituary in Cadiz Record February 5, 1946 and from an article "Cousin Wes" which appeared in the Thomas-Bridges Association newsletter)* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/26982533/wesle
2 CONC y_gunn-thomas Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Feb 16 2026, 23:59:41 UTC''
