1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT ===  Earliest Spiveys in England. Re:  Regis ===
2 CONT Earliest Spiveys in England. Re:  Registrum Antiquessimum:  William Spivey (Spivy) c1100. Re:  13th RefAntiquiss:  Jordan Spivi, 1316. Re:  Calendar of Inquisitions:  William Spyvay (Spivey), Yorkshire 1350. Re:  Raymond Byrd Spiv
2 CONC ey's book, "The Spiveys since 1543". The following is taken from the book "THE SPIVEYS SINCE 1543", by Raymond Byrd Spivey. The Spiveys got their start a long time ago , along with others of the central belt of Eur-Asia.  They wer
2 CONC e given, and accepted, their surname as a result of the political Treaty between French (and Roman Catholic Church) on the one hand and by England (under King Henry VIII and led by the Duke of York -- most of whose citizens were S
2 CONC axon Angles, or Western-Lowlands Lancers'), the Treaty of April 26, 1543, being held in Edinburgh, Scotland. The original Spiveys had been exiled/banished by the French-faction, because their landlords, Sir George "the Dark" (Doug
2 CONC las) of Pittendreich (Pictten-Reich-Realm of the Picts:  "Pikers, or Pike-fighters"), and his brother, the 7th Earl of Angus  -- along the north/central coast of eastern Scotland, northward from Edinburgh, Earl Archibald "the Dark
2 CONC " of Angus, had been negotiating with Henry VII concerning a possible union with England.  Margaret, sister of Henry VIII, had married James IV of Scotland, who tried to play both sides of the question.  When James IV was killed i
2 CONC n battle with the English, dying a few days after the birth of James V, Archibald of Angus became the "acting king" and married Margaret.  The French party, however, soon became stronger, and Archibald sought help from Henry VIII,
2 CONC  new King of England, spending much time in England.  Margaret divorced him, and married a French sympathizer, but soon divorced him also.  When James V was at an early age declared king by the French faction, he confiscated the l
2 CONC ands of Archibald and George, and banished both with their citizens, in 1521, putting his mother Margaret in prison for the rest of her life.  James died young , in battle, and his daughter Mary was heir-apparent.  (It was her son
2 CONC , who, a half-century later was to become James VI of Scotland, and James I of united England and Scotland.) Although these first Spiveys (French Gothic/Saxon "Speiwe" : spat-out/exiled/refugees) had been exiled in Yorkshire and N
2 CONC orthumberland (under Duke of York's "protection") they were subjects of George and Archibald, and therefore as all Scots of that time were accustomed to having only a "given" name - - with other descriptions, such as 'Big John', '
2 CONC Little Willie', 'Roderick the Smith' etc. - - when they by the Treaty of 1543 left as subjects of the Duke of York and his officials, to be shifted to the jobs their landlords desired, including the shipping companies and colonies
2 CONC  established by such companies - - some no doubt already working as such under their Scottish landlords-exile - - they were required to follow the Saxon law of registering, at birth, marriage, employment, etc., by more than one na
2 CONC me.  Since most of the 'Spiwe' would not have been able to read and write, they were given their surnames, or chose them, by their occupation, or if soldiers and non-specialized subjects, then by the name of their new landlords.  
2 CONC  By the general designation, in the Spivey the letter "W" when not beginning the word, had become 'v', the diphthong 'ei' had become simply 'i' or 'y', and a pronounced final 'e' had the 'y' added to indicate the 'e' was to be sou
2 CONC nded.  (The 'Southern 'i' sound was original.) By the Treaty of Edinburg, Archibald had his title and lands restored, though it seems his subjects in England were not returned to Angus.  His brother George was imprisoned for life 
2 CONC as a hostage, to guarantee that his older brother would not again rise up in  rebellion against the French/RC authorities in control of the government.  This 'sell-out' by Henry VIII was in recognition of the threat to England by 
2 CONC the French power.  The English added the 'Spiveys' to their labor force, still depleted fr om the black plague of a century and a half before.  The expansion in shipping and colonization, was soon to spread the Spiveys along with 
2 CONC the Smiths, Bakers, Taylors and other tradesmen and common laborers, clerks farmers (Ge-orges= earth-workers, farmers), dock workers, etc., into European ports and to the Americas.  Some no doubt were shifted with their companies 
2 CONC to Londonderry, North Ireland in the colony of Scots and English planted there by James I, to discourage the rebellious Irish, and may have later come from there to America. The names of towns and counties in northern South Caroli
2 CONC na, southern North Carolina (Durham, York, Chatham, Cumberland Richmond, Wilmington, Scotland, etc., up the Cape Fear and Pee Dee (pee-ah-tee + Choctaw/ Creek for our forest) Rivers, attest to the Yorkshire origin of many of the c
2 CONC olonists, as is true of the Virginia Colony names. The earliest recorded listing of the name "Spivey" thus far discovered in marriage, birth, and burial records has been compiled by Odis Mae Spivey Dunegin in her book "the Spivey 
2 CONC Family", indicates that the birth date of the first-known Spivey was approximately 1543.  I am indebted to Mrs. Dunegin for all the names from England.  She does not attempt a relationship sequence.  the original arrival of George
2 CONC  Spivey at the Jamestown Colony is 1643, a century to the year when the first Spivey was names, and of the settlement in Nansemond County in Virginia and into Chowan County of the North Carolina Colony.  Again she does not try to 
2 CONC connect the relationships.  She also gives the listing of Revolutionary War enlisted soldiers, and the James Spivey references in Richmond County, North Carolina.
