1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''*PARENTS OF 'CHUNKY JERRY' NOT LONG JERRY*  VERY IMPORTANT NOTE!'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "Danger Nick"
2 CONT Nickolas was called "Danger" because it was said he had an irascible temper.
2 CONT "Danger" came to Perry County in 1790's, probably at the same time as his brother John. He came through Pound Gap, from around Holston where he had been living for some time and settled on the North Fork of the Kentucky River, nea
2 CONC r what is now Combs, KY. He was one of the largest land owners in Perry Co. at that time. He received his first land grant in 1810 when this was Clay County and he had other grants on Ball Creek, Lotts Creek, Combs Mill, and Trace
2 CONC  Creek.
2 CONT He remained a Tory and tradition says that because of this he became unpopular with other Combs. "Danger" Nick died in Perry County near 1859. He is buried in the Old Combs Cemetery on Meadow Branch Near Combs Bends.
2 CONT
2 CONT www.combs-families.org Nicholas & Nancy GRIGSBY Combs I - A Documented Chronology (Based on Dickey Diary Interviews, Revolutionary War Pension Files (pertaining to Nicholas), and applicable Combs Counties of Record as noted below)
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT aft 1759 in (probably) Frederick Co, VA. Born: Nicholas, s/o John & Nancy HARDING Combs, Sr.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas was probably born in Frederick Co, VA since land records place his father in that county in 1758, and his grandfather, Mason Combs, Sr., was already there by 1751. That he was born after 1759 is documented by his 1783 She
2 CONC nandoah Co, VA sworn declaration that he was under 21 in 1781 or 1782. The 1810 Clay Co, KY Census lists him as born pre-1765, and the 1820 as born pre-1774; the 1830 and 1840 Perry Co, KY Census Enumerations list him as born 1760
2 CONC -1770, and the 1850 as b ca 1764. According to his Revolutionary War Pension File, he was born 1759-1760 (85 or 86 in Nov 1845); and/or pre-1765 ("being smartly over 90 years of age" in Dec 1855).
2 CONT
2 CONT He was apparently deceased by Jul 1859 (missing from 1860 Census and Mortality Schedule), and at the time of his death (1856-1859), was over 100 years of age according to the Dickey Diary Interviews of two of his grandchildren, Ma
2 CONC rgaret "Peggy" Combs Lewis and John S. Combs. Although both their interviews indicate that Nicholas d in 1838, it has been subsequently documented that other dates in the interview of Margaret Combs Lewis were "off" by twenty year
2 CONC s (see 1857-1858 below), which, if also true of the death date of Nicholas, would likewise move his year of birth to 1757 or 1858......
2 CONT
2 CONT ......1804 (and probably earlier) Floyd Co, KY. Nicholas had entered 400 acres, land that would become part of Clay Co, KY when it was established. This early entry also confirms Andrew Combs' Dickey Diary Interview when he stated
2 CONC : "… My Grandfather Nicholas Combs came first. He built a cabin and left his wife and went back for provisions etc… My grandfather was detained on his first trip back to Long Island of Holston and he feared his wife would starve o
2 CONC r die before he could get back but when he came up to the point of the mouth of Carr, he helloed and she answered him. His heart leaped with joy at the response. the deer were all about the cabin but she did not know how to shoot.
2 CONC  The women were not marksmen. I knew my mother to kill bear and deer. the old CombsES were property plenty. They owned slaves…" Andrew's brother, John S. Combs also confirmed Nicholas' early arrival in Kentucky, stating that Nicho
2 CONC las "… came to Kentucky early in the settlement of Kentucky. He lived and died near where L.D. Combs now lives in Perry County…" Nicholas' granddaughter, Margaret Combs Lewis added: "Nicholas Combs, one of the original eight broth
2 CONC ers who settled in Perry County from Holston River, Virginia… My father, Nicholas Combs, told me that when grandfather first came to Perry he went to Carr to get some wheat to sow from old William CORNETT…" (Kentucky land entries 
2 CONC confirm that William CORNETT had entered land in then-Floyd County in 1803. Moreover, Thomas CODY (s/o William and Sinai STACY Cody) refers in his deposition on behalf of the Rev. War Pension Application of William CORNETT to he a
2 CONC nd his father having come upon the CORNETTS during a hunting trip in 1804, and to having been at the wedding of William and Mary EVERAGE Cornett in Sullivan Co, TN in 1796. At the time (1804), the CODYS were living adjacent to Nic
2 CONC holas' paternal uncle, William, and his wife, Seth STACY (sister to Sinai STACY Cody) in Russell Co, VA on Crabtree Branch of Moccassin Creek, North Fork of the Holston River......
2 CONT
2 CONT 1857-Jun 1859 Perry Co, KY(?). Died: Nicholas Combs, Sr. Although the Dickey Diary Interviews of John S. Combs and Margaret Combs Lewis state that their grandfather d ca 1838, he is documented as having been alive at least until 1
2 CONC 856, and apparently deceased bef Jul 1859 (1860 Perry Co, KY Census and Mortality Schedule).
2 CONT **From Notes of Patricia Riley Caudill
2 CONT
2 CONT ********************** Son of John Combs & Nancy Harding; married Nancy Grigsby.
2 CONT
2 CONT See this link for detailed description of Nicholas' family: [http://www.combs-families.org/combs/families/c-n1736.htm Nicholas Combs & Nancy Grigsby]
2 CONT
2 CONT See this link for how this person relates to other Combs: [http://www.combs-families.org/combs/dna/index.html?virtual_archive/index.htm Combs Family Virtual Archives]
2 CONT
2 CONT (Seems to be a fair amount of ambiguity about this person's details at the present time...)
2 CONT
