1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR Ancestor #: A119366
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Walker_(Kentucky)
2 CONT
2 CONT George Walker (1763 – August 19, 1819) was a U.S. Senator from Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Culpeper County, Virginia, Walker attended the common schools and served in the United States Revolutionary War. He moved to Jessamine County, Kentucky in 1794 and studied law. He was admitted to the bar and commenced prac
2 CONC tice in Nicholasville, Kentucky in 1799. He served as a commissioner of the Kentucky River Company in 1801.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walker was a member of the Kentucky State Senate from 1810 to 1814. He was then appointed to the United States Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of George M. Bibb, and served from August 30, 1814 to December 16,
2 CONC  1814 when a successor was elected. He died in Nicholasville in 1819, and was interred on his estate near there.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Walker was the brother of David Walker and the great uncle of James David Walker.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT from Wikipedia George Walker (1763 – August 19, 1819)[1] was a U.S. Senator from Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Culpeper County, Virginia, Walker attended the common schools and served in the American Revolutionary War. He moved to Jessamine County, Kentucky in 1794 and studied law. He was admitted to the bar and commenced practice 
2 CONC in Nicholasville, Kentucky in 1799. He served as a commissioner of the Kentucky River Company in 1801.
2 CONT
2 CONT Walker was a member of the Kentucky State Senate from 1810 to 1814. He was then appointed to the United States Senate to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of George M. Bibb, and served from August 30, 1814 to December 16,
2 CONC  1814 when a successor was elected. He died in Nicholasville in 1819, and was interred on his estate near there.
2 CONT
2 CONT George Walker was the brother of David Walker and John Walker and the great uncle of James David Walker. He was also the uncle of two governors of Florida, Richard Keith Call and David Shelby Walker. Another nephew John George Wal
2 CONC ker served as a general in the Confederate Army during the Civil War.
