1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===John Baylor, III 
2 CONT *Gender:	Male  
2 CONT *Birth:	May 12, 1705 Walkerton, King and Queen County, Virginia  
2 CONT *Death:	April 03, 1772 (66)  Bowling Green, Caroline County, Virginia
2 CONT *Place of Burial:	Baylor Family Cemetery, Bowling Green, Caroline County, Virginia, United States 
2 CONT *Son of John Baylor, II and Lucy Mary (Todd) Baylor 
2 CONT *Husband of Frances Lucy (Walker) Baylor 
2 CONT
2 CONT Married Francis Walker of Yorktown January 2, 1744 (Baylor's History of the Baylors)
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT from http://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/baylor_john_iii_1705-1772  Encyclopedia of Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT >'''John Baylor III''' was a wealthy planter and one of the most significant importers and breeders of thoroughbred horses in pre-Revolutionary America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Baylor was born on May 12, 1705, at Smithfield, in King and Queen County, Virginia, the son of John Baylor II, a wealthy planter and merchant, and Lucy Todd O'Brien, a widow who married the elder Baylor in 1698. 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Baylor was educated in England and, upon his return to Virginia, granted land along the Mattaponi River, where he built his estate, Newmarket. He represented Caroline County in the House of Burgesses (1742–1752; 1756–1765) and on
2 CONC  the county court before falling out of political favor in a dispute over how best to oppose the Stamp Act (1765). Baylor's deepest passion was elite horseflesh and it nearly bankrupted him. By the mid-1750s, he had given up racin
2 CONC g and was instead importing, at great expense, a dozen or more of the colony's best thoroughbreds, which attracted the mares of George Washington, among others, for breeding. In 1764, he purchased the thoroughbred Fearnought for t
2 CONC he unprecedented price of a thousand guineas, and it became Virginia's premier breeding horse, whose genes were prized even into the twentieth century. Baylor, however, sank into debt and died at Newmarket in 1772 after a long ill
2 CONC ness.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 2, 1744, Baylor married Frances Lucy Walker (1728–1783), daughter of Jacob Walker (d. 1757), a Yorktown merchant. The Baylors had twelve children, eight of whom survived infancy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children listed at FindAGrave:[3]
2 CONT # Lucy Baylor
2 CONT # Walker Baylor (____ - 1826)
2 CONT # John Baylor (1750 - 1808)
2 CONT # George Baylor (1752 - 1784)
2 CONT # Robert Walker Baylor (1754 - 1838)
2 CONT
2 CONT After a long illness, Baylor died at Newmarket on April 3, 1772. Despite his substantial liabilities, he left a vast estate, including tens of thousands of acres of land, more than 120 slaves, and a stud farm that included more th
2 CONC an “fifty choice blooded horses,” according to the Virginia Gazette. Baylor’s son and principal heir, Cambridge-educated John Baylor IV (1750–1808), sought to pay off Newmarket’s debts, but his financial excesses far exceeded his 
2 CONC father’s. The younger Baylor died in debtor’s prison in Bowling Green, Virginia. Ironically, it was a prison his father helped to build while a “gentleman justice” of the Caroline County court.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT born at Walkerton and educated in England
2 CONT
2 CONT Granted a large tract of land on which he built the find old mansion Newmarket. His plantation was established the year before that part of Caroline was cut off from King and Queen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Colonel of the Caroline militia and a Burgess from 1742 to 1765
2 CONT
2 CONT County Lieutenant for Orange where he had been granted a large tract of land and where he spent his summers
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the great turfman and importer of fine horses, his stable containing a hundred thoroughbreds at the time of his death in 1772. He was one of the most noted of all importers of blooded horses in coloniel times. Among many ot
2 CONC her fine horses he imported was "Fearnaught" one of the most famous stallions in America turf history. Fearnaught cost Col. Baylor 1000 guineas and was brought over from England in 1764. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fearnaught, whose offspring included Apollo and Regulus, two of the most famous horses of their time, was responsible for a revolutionary change in American racing. Hitherto racing had been confined to short dashes, and speed was 
2 CONC emphasized rather than stamina. Fearnaught and his progeny, with the characteristic bottom of the English Thoroughbred, popularized the longer flat races in America. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Many of John's horses would race at Bowling Green. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was an intimate of Washington. Just after his marraige Washington sent an order to England for a pair of shoes to be made on "Colonel Bayor's last-but a little larger than his-and to have high heels"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===The portrait 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://colonialvirginiaportraits.org/portrait/john-baylor-iii-1705-1772/
2 CONT
2 CONT >Grandson of a planter who traded profitably in several Tidewater counties, and son of a planter and burgess from Gloucester and then King and Queen counties, John Baylor III (1705–1772) was third-generation Virginia aristocracy. 
2 CONC As heir to a dynasty, he was sent to England for early education at Putney Grammar School, followed by study at Cambridge. There, presumably at about age seventeen, he commissioned this portrait, an example of provincial English a
2 CONC rt. It is thinly painted, without a great deal of virtuosity, as distinct from the lavish London portrait of Robert Carter III—which is of the same type—as are contemporary portraits produced in Virginia. Nonetheless, the painting
2 CONC  served quite well its purpose of documenting the sitter's successful passage through English society.
2 CONT
2 CONT >Most striking about this image is the young sitter's confident, even haughty attitude, which is conveyed by his assertive deportment and costume. Still but a boy, Baylor wears an adult wig and carries a sword. To judge from this 
2 CONC image, he had been well instructed in England to take his privileged place in society. In Virginia in 1726 he patented a large tract of land in Caroline County and built a one-and-a-half-story, wooden H-plan house, now lost, a str
2 CONC ucture relatively large for its time and place. There this portrait hung. Baylor named his estate Newmarket after the famous racecourse near Cambridge in England, where he had learned about horse breeding and racing. He devoted hi
2 CONC s adult life in Virginia to the former, with more financial success than most who invested in horses.
2 CONT
2 CONT === Sources
2 CONT # [http://ead.lib.virginia.edu/vivaxtf/view?docId=uva-sc/viu00032.xml A  GUIDE TO THE BAYLOR FAMILY PAPERS] "John Baylor 1 ( 1650- 1720) resident of Gloucester County, Virginia, and later King and Queen County, Virginia, married L
2 CONC ucy Todd O'Brien (ca.1681-?) of New Kent County, Virginia, in 1698. They were believed to have had three offspring, Frances Baylor, Robert Baylor, and John Baylor 2."
2 CONT * See: Carolyn J. Weekley, Painters and Paintings in the Early American South (2013), 81; Wayne Craven, Colonial American Portraiture (1986), 201-202; Encyclopedia Virginia 
2 CONT *From http://www.vahistorical.org/dynasties/johnbaylor.htm (dead link)
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Baylor-101 cites 
2 CONT * Encyclopediaof Virginia: John Baylor III
2 CONT * Harris, Malcom. Old New Kent County [Virginia]: Some Account of the Planters ..., Volume 1. Genealogical Publishing Com, 2006. found at Google Books: Old Kent County: John Baylor
2 CONT * FIndAGrave: Col John Baylor III: Memorial ID: 104350558 Created by: MaryTatumColeman. Record added: Jan 29, 2013. Last accessed Aug 23, 2017. https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/104350558/john-baylor
2 CONT * Historical Southern Families, Vol XX
2 CONT * The Cabells and Their Kin: A Memorial Volume of History, Biography, and Genealogy Alexander Brown January 1, 1895 Houghton, Mifflin & Company: Wife, Marriage, Parents, Child page 337. PA337
2 CONT * Dorman, John Frederick. 2004. Adventurers of Purse and Person, Virginia, 1607-1624/5. Genealogical Pub. Co. 4th edition. Vol 1, Page 263 and 267, book lookup by Hill, C. [03/04/2024]
