0 @N6000000005966048232@ NOTE Wichita Eagle, The (KS) - April 24, 1993
1 CONT Deceased Name: Rainer, Bessie Lee (Williams)
1 CONT Rainer, Bessie Lee (Williams), 81, homemaker, died Wednesday, April 21, 1993. Service 1 p.m. Monday, James F. Webb Funeral Home, Newton, Miss.
1 CONT Survivors: sons, James of Longmont, Colo., Billy of Wichita; sister, Ephia Lou Nichols of Jackson, Miss.; four grandchildren; eight great- grandchildren. Memorial has been established with Alzheimer's and Related Diseases. Downing
1 CONC  & Lahey Mortuary.
1 CONT Edition: CITY
1 CONT Page: 2D
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1993 The Wichita Eagle
0 @N6000000004239754894@ NOTE @S18025@
0 @N6000000004270555272@ NOTE http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=:434296&id=I129
1 CONT
1 CONT      In this Magazine, XVII, 187, 188, was published the will of William Nelson (1670), grandfather of Thomas Nelson, the emigrant to Virginia, in which Hugh Nelson, William's father, is mentioned as living, and XIII, 402, 403, th
1 CONC e will of Hugh Nelson, father of the emigrant.
1 CONT     It appears from a deposition in York county that Thomas Nelson, the emigrant, came to Virginia in 1696 and again in 1698, probably as a merchant or supercargo. He is said to have settled at Yorktown about 1700. He was J. P. fo
1 CONC r the county of York. He married 1st Margaret Reade, granddaughter of Col. George Reade, of the Council, and 2nd Mrs. Frances Tucker, nee Houston.
1 CONT     He was buried in the churchyard at Yorktown where his tomb remains with the arms: per pale argent and sable, a chevron between three fleurs de lis countercharged; Crest: a fleur de lis per pale argent and sable, and the follow
1 CONC ing epitaph:
1 CONT     "Hie Jacet
1 CONT     Spe certa resurgendi in Christo
1 CONT     Thomas Nelson, Generous
1 CONT     Filius Hugonis et sariae Nelson
1 CONT     de Penrith in Comitatu
1 CONT     Natus 20' mo die Februarii Anno Domoni 1677
1 CONT     Vitae bene geste finem emplevit
1 CONT     7 mo die Octobris, 1745: Aetatis suae 68"
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     Isle of Weight-District of Virginia.
1 CONT     See D.A.R. Reports--Master Index.
1 CONT     At Shreve Memorial Library, Shreveport, LA.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Early Virginia Land Grants-Isle of Weigt. In the year 1704 Mr. Thomas Nelson, Esquire called "Scotch Tom" sells a parcel of Headright Land 200 Acres which he inherited from his father, Hugh Nelson, deceased husband of Sarah Ne
1 CONC lson who is also deceased. The said parcel of land being a portion or a part of such lands being set aside of and for Hugh Nelson, at his birth in the year 1636 by his father (name not readable) Nelson who had received the said He
1 CONC adright land grant in the year 1635 and being part of the original land grant of 1200 acres. Isle of Weigt District of Virginia.
1 CONT
1 CONT     From the above land sale the following part of this Genealogy chart may be ascertained.
1 CONT
1 CONT     In Combination with the D.A.R. Records (Now is Veleum Form) and Indexed at the Shreve Memorial Library, Shreveport, LA and with the following Information obtained from the Virginia Genealogist (The Virginia Section) of the Shr
1 CONC eve Memorial Library and also the Dar. D.A.R. Records of Virginia Successions.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     Berkeley Manuscripts
1 CONT     Copy of Copy of the Will of Thomas Nelson of York
1 CONT     (In Possession (1897) of Maj. William N. Berkeley, of Charlottesville, Va.)
1 CONT     In the Name of God, Amen. I, Thomas Nelson, of Yorktown, in Va., Merchant, calling to mind the uncertain Duration of human life, and desirous while I am (blessed be God) in perfect Health and of sound and disposing Memory, to 
1 CONC order my Worldly affairs, and to dispose of that estate which the Almighty hath bestowed upon me, after recommending my soul to God, in and through the Merits of my Saviour Jesus Christ, do make this my last Will and Testament in 
1 CONC manner following:
1 CONT     Imprimis: I give and bequeath unto my beloved wife the sum of Five thousand pounds sterling, to be paid her out of my personal estate by my executer, hereafter named, and I also order and oppoint that of the profits out of my 
1 CONC Lands and Negroes there by paid unto my said wife the sum of one hundred and fifty pounds sterling p. Annum during her natural life.
1 CONT     Item: I give unto my said wife all those pieces of Gold, Rings, and Jewels whereof she is now possessed. And it is also my will and desire that she have during her natural life the use of my Dwelling-House, Kitchens, Gardens, 
1 CONC on the same side of the street with the house, stable, chariot Horses, and , and of all the Furniture at my said house and Kitchens and my Town Cows; also the service of my negroes Grace, Penny, Suckey, Frank, Will, Cesar, Tryal, 
1 CONC and Little Davy during her life; and after decease I give the said furniture, chariot, horses, cows, and Negroes to my son William Nelson, which several bequests to my said wife are meant and intended her in a Lien and satisfactio
1 CONC n of the share she might be entitled to out of my personal estate and of her right of Dower in my Lands and slaves, if she pleases to accept thereof.
1 CONT     Item: I give to my Daughter, Mary Berkeley, twenty-five pounds sterling to buy her a House Wench.
1 CONT     Item: I give and bequeath unto my grandchildren, Edmund Berkeley, Nelson Berkeley, Mary Berkeley, Sarah Berkeley, and Lucy Berkeley, each the sum of Two hundred pounds sterling, to be paid them when they shall severally attain
1 CONC  the age of twenty-one years, or on the Day of Marriage, which shall first happen, and if any of my said grandchildren shall happen to die under age, not being married, the share of such grandchild so dying shall be paid unto the 
1 CONC next child my Daughter, Mary Berkeley, shall happen to have
1 CONT     Item: I give my son, Thomas Nelson, the sum of four thousand pound is sterling; but whatever sum my son William shall have paid him at or before my death is to go in discharge of and to be accounted a part of the said legacy o
1 CONC f four thousand pounds sterling; and this is all I intend my said son Thomas, having already given him the estate in King William county, which I purchased of Col. Thomas Jones; and the houses, Lots, and plantations bought for him
1 CONC  of Doctor John Dixon, etc.
1 CONT     Item: I give to my Daughter, Sarah Burwell, twenty pounds sterling, and the Negro Wench Belina, with her increase, having lately given her fifteen hundred pounds sterling as a portion upon her marriage with Mr. Robert Burwell.
1 CONT     Item: I give and bequeath to my causins, William Nelson and John Nelson, grandsons of my brother William, late of Barbados each [fifty?] pounds sterling, to be paid them when they shall severally attain the age of twenty-one y
1 CONC ears.
1 CONT     Item: My last will and Desire is that out of my personal estate my executor do cause to be provided and set up, over or near the grave of my first wife, a hansom marble stone or monument, as soon as conveniently may be.
1 CONT     Item: All my Lands, tenements, slaves, and real estate whatsoever I give and Devise to my son, William Nelson, and his heirs forever, but subject, nevertheless, to the payment of one hundred and fifty pounds sterling p. Annum,
1 CONC  herein before Devised to my wife in Lieu of her Dower, if she survives me and accepts the same.
1 CONT     Item: All the residue of my goods, chattels, and other personal estate of what kind soever it be, after my just Debts and Legacies herein before mentioned are fully satisfied and paid, I give unto my elder son, William Nelson.
1 CONT     Thomas Nelson, born February 20, 1677, at Penrith, Cumberland county, England, son of Hugh and Sarah Nelson, died at Yorktown, Va., on October 7, 1745. This will was recorded in the general court, whose records are now destroy
1 CONC ed. He married, first, Margaret Reade, daughter of Robert Reade, eldest son of Col. George Reade, formerly secretary of the colony, who owned the land on which Yorktown was established. He married, second , Frances Tucker, widow o
1 CONC f Robert Tucker, of Barbados, and mother of Col. Robert Tucker, of Norfolk. This paper explains the entry in the your records, dated 1738, wherein William Nelson, gent., of Yorktown and county March gives a negro girl to his daugh
1 CONC ter, Hephzibah Nelson, sent to him from Barbados by his father and mother, Benjamin Bessel and Hephsibah his wife. According to the will, Thomas Nelson had two grand-nephew, William and John Nelson, grandsons of his "brother Willi
1 CONC am, late of Barbados." (See Quarterly, IV., jp. 134)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     In the churchyard are the tombs of Thomas Nelson (1677-1745), a barogque sarcophagus with the Nelson arms; of William Nelson (1711-72); and of General Thomas Nelson (1738-89)---father, son, and grandson.
1 CONT     Grace Church---the Colonial York-Hampton Church---was originally T-shaped. The interior has been burned twice, once during the Revolution when the church was used by the British as a magazine, and again in 1814. When it was us
1 CONC ed for military purposes during the 1860's, a signal tower was created on its roof.
1 CONT     The Custom House, (adm. $.25), a two-story building of brick with hipped roof, turns a plain end to Main Street and faces a brick-walled enclosure. It was built in 1706 and restored in 1920. Edward Ambler, collector of revenue
1 CONC s, was among those who participated in Virginia's 'tea party' on November 3, 1774, when residents of the town boarded the Virginia and dumped overboard two half-chests of tea.
1 CONT     The Nelson House or York Hall (open April 1 to Nov. 1; adm. to $.75, to garden $.50 to both $1), in a brick-walled garden, lifts a bulky rectangle of brick two stories to a deep cornice and a broad gabled roof. It has large do
1 CONC rmers along the front, and two massive chimneys with heavily molded tops. The light stone corner quoins and unusually tall keystones in the deep segmental arches of brick over the windows add interest. There is considerable variet
1 CONC y in the size and arrangement of the fully paneled rooms. The drawing room, off the paved transverse hall, has engaged columns to frame the openings.
1 CONT     The mansion was built in 1740-41 by William Nelson (1711-72), president of the Council, who was the father of Thomas Nelson (1738-89), later a signer of the Declaration of Independence, and Governor of Virginia. While Cornwall
1 CONC is occupied the house during the siege of 1781, General Nelson directed a cannonade against his own home; a ball is still embedded in one wall.
1 CONT     The one-story Sheild house (adm. $.25), on a high basement, lifts walls of large brick to a gabled roof with clipped ends. Outside chimneys and five dormers give character to the structure, which was built by Thomas Sessions a
1 CONC bout 1699.
1 CONT     The Yorktown Monument, rising 95 feet above a green, is an elaborate and symbolically ornamented column of white marble bearing a figure of liberty with arms outstretched. Though in 1781 Congress resolved to erect a marble col
1 CONC umn here, 'adorned with emblems of the alliance between the United States and His Most Christian Majesty (France) . . . ' a century elapsed before another Congress provided the necessary money for it.
1 CONT
1 CONT     The Yorktown Nelsons
1 CONT     Thomas "Scotch Tom" Nelson (1677-1745) was the progenitor (Forefather) of Yorktown, Virginia Nelsons.He was born in Penrith, England (County Cumberland) 20 Feb 1677 to Hugh and Sarah Nelson who were cloth merchants in Penrith.
1 CONT     by John D. and Mabel Nelson: Saturday, September 04, 1999 on the MyFamily.com web site.
