0 @N6000000000557914151@ NOTE Information on Shadrack Stanley and family, source Alachua Cty., Florida 
1 CONT Added by sharonmoore489 on 6 Jan 2008 
1 CONT
1 CONT Information on Shadrack Stanley and family, source Alachua Cty., Florida 
1 CONT Added by sharonmoore489 on 25 Dec 2007 
1 CONT Standley, Shadrack (~1761 - ) - male 
1 CONT b. ABT. 1761 in Dobbs Co.N.C. (Now Craven) 
1 CONT d. in Probably Alachua Co., Fl. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [SStandley.FTW] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Shadrack Standley, having moved to Darlington, S.C. before Aug. 12, 1783,was issued the following land grants. 
1 CONT (1) Sep. 20, 1784...200 acres on Swift Creek. Bk. No. 1, page96..Surveyed Oct. 28, & recorded Nov. 22, 1784. 
1 CONT (2) Jan. 25, 1787...230 acres on Indian Branch, Bk. No. 2, page 13,Surveyed Feb. 21, & recorded May 21, 1787 
1 CONT (3) Oct. 2, 1794...110 acres on east side of Indian Creek, Surveyed Oct.3, 1794, recorded___? 
1 CONT Total grants 540 acres. 
1 CONT Shadrack Standley, oldest son of Sands Standley, served under GeneralFrancis Marion (the swamp fox) in 1782 as a private for 30 days andclaimed compensation for this service on Aug. 12, 1783. " In 1782Shadrack joined the South Car
1 CONC olina Militia and served as a private underGen. Francis Marion, as shown by the following request for pay: Voucherrecorded in Archives and History, Columbia, SC: "782 State of SouthCarolina - to Shadrack Standley, Dr Test (testifi
1 CONC ed) to 30 days duty inGen. Marion's Brigade, is (at rate of) 15 pounds (for six months period)or 2 (shillings), 10 1/4 (pence)S. Carolina, Cheraw Dist. ShadrackStandley came before me and made oath that the above 
1 CONT account is just and that he hath had no satisfaction for the same. Swornbefore me this 12th day of Aug. 1783. (signed) Thos. Powe, I. P." 
1 CONT "The State was hard pressed for cash, so the General Assembly passedan act on March 16, 1783 to issue INDENTED CERTIFICATES. On Apr. 27,1785 Shadrack was issued a copy for "Two pounds, two shillings & tenpence farthing sterling fo
1 CONC r 30 days Militia due year 1782 & accountaudited. Book O, No. 573, with interest at 2 shilling and 11 pence peryear. Issued by Peter Bosquet, Commissioner of the Treasury. 
1 CONT He would have been about 21 years of age at that time. (SandsStandley of the Pee Dee Valley, Haywood Standley) Also taken fromoriginal Rev. Audited Account of Shadrack Standley from the SC Archives..order#7384..AA 7302. 
1 CONT Shadrack Stanley is listed as a lieutenant in "Georgia's Roster ofthe Rev." (Harvey List, page 169) 
1 CONT The 1790 Census S.C. showed that he had at that time 6 boys and twogirls. (Haywood Stanley) 
1 CONT Shadrack is not listed in the 1800 SC Census. 
1 CONT From a letter written to Elaine Campbell by GraynellaMcLelland>>"Shadrack Standley joined the SC Rev. and the troops were sentto Reidsville Ga. to train with the Ga. troops. They were then sent toSt. Mary's Rev. Camp and fought th
1 CONC e British in Fl. Shadrack's bounty landwas given in Ga. from the Ga. State Prison to Lyon's Ga. and toReidsville." 
1 CONT After the close of the Rev. War, settlers began locating inTattnall Co. territory, and especially along the Altamaha and OhoopeeRivers. Most of all the early settlers were from S.C. The countygovernment was said to have been carri
1 CONC ed on in the house of Zachariah Coxuntil 1807 when a courthouse was built on the west side of the OhoopeeRiver about 300 yds. from Sheppard's bridge. One of the builders of thisfirst Tattnall Co. Courthouse is said to have been Sh
1 CONC adrack Standley. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In the last decade of the eighteenth century, during their waragainst France, Britain and her ally Spain, preyed upon US shipping --impressing seamen, capturing ships and confiscating cargo. The UnitedStates levied a claim against
1 CONC  Spain for part of the damages suffered. IfSpain were to cede the Spanish Territories of East and West Florida, thisclaim would be considered paid. If Spain did not cede the territories,and the United States were to somehow acquir
1 CONC e the Floridas, the claimwould not then be pursued. 
1 CONT In 1803, the United States purchased Louisiana from France. Notclearly defined was where the eastern boundary of Louisiana ended andwhere the western boundary of Florida began. The Spanish presence in theFloridas was limited to Mo
1 CONC bile, Pensacola, St. Augustine andFernandina. The remainder of the territory was home to roaming bands ofIndians and land-hungry citizens of the southern U.S. 
1 CONT Without the approval of Congress, U. S. President James Madison andhis Secretary of State James Monroe schemed to acquire West Florida bydisputing where the eastern boundary of Louisiana was and annexing EastFlorida by settling th
1 CONC e area and having the residents ask to become partof the United States. As an early result of this scheme hatched in early1811, on 12 March 1812, a group of armed Georgians crossed the St. MarysRiver in East Florida and marched on
1 CONC  Fernandina. The expectation wasthat Fernandina would be overrun, the United States would provideimmediate support and the Georgian "Patriots" reinforced by federaltroops would capture St. Augustine and thus be the revolutionarygo
1 CONC vernment of Florida. Other than federal gunships standing by offAmelia Island, no U.S. aid was forthcoming. Although months later, acontingent of US troops entered Florida to provide order, acceptance ofthe Patriots' declaration o
1 CONC f independence from Spain and request forannexation by the United States was accepted by Congress. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT On June 18th 1812 war with Great Britain was declared. Rumors ofoccupation of Florida by British troops offered another opportunity toinvade and annex. Congress, dominated by the northern states, chose toattempt to acquire Canada 
1 CONC instead. In January 1814, with Florida still aSpanish possession, Georgians came deep into the territory. On a prairienear King Payne's town a fort was built and The District of Elotchaway ofthe Republic of East Florida was establ
1 CONC ished. A message signed by theresidents was sent to President Madison requesting that Elotchaway beaccepted as a territory of the Union. This offer was not accepted. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT The 1790 Federal Census of South Carolina, All Saints Parish, listsa Shadrack Standley as head of household. 
1 CONT In 1795 Shadrack drew bounty land in Cheraws Dist., S.C. 
1 CONT On 29 November 1800, in Montgomery County, Georgia, ShadrackStandley and Robert Standley were witness to a deed to 200 acres of landin Liberty County, Georgia sold to Charles Flowers (husband of Shadrack'sdaughter Martha) by Willi
1 CONC am Price. 
1 CONT On 1 August 1804 in Tattnall County, Georgia, McKeen Green ofTatnall County to Shadrack Standley of Tattnall County for 800 dollarssold 575 acres of land on the Altamaha River in Tattnall County in twotracts, each of 287 1/2 acres
1 CONC : one granted to McKeen Green and the otherto Tom Brinson bounded by each other, McKeen Green, John Brinson,Altamaha River, and vacant land. Signature, witnesses and recordation notshown on photocopy. 
1 CONT The 1805 Tattnall Co. Tax Digest listed the following; JesseStanley, Sands Stanley, Shadrach Stanley, Benjamin Stanley 
1 CONT In 1806 Shadrack Standley was Justice of Peace for the 39thDistrict of Tattnall County, Georgia. 
1 CONT On 25 June 1806, Shadrack Standley was commissioned in CaptainDaniels Company. 
1 CONT On 13 October 1813 in Tattnall County, Georgia, Shadrack Standley ofTattnall County to Stephen Medlock of Tattnall County for one dollar sold332 acres of land in Tattnall County bounded by George Henderson, saidStandley and Altama
1 CONC ha River. Signed Shadrack Standley. Witnesses: JohnStandley and Jesse Standley. Recorded 15 November 1814. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT On 24 December 1815, a peace treaty was signed with Britain. Withratification on 16 February 1816, the war was officially over. No longerwas protecting Florida from foreign occupation a rationale for U.S.invasion. In 1817, Indian 
1 CONC depredations provided excuse for AndrewJackson to invade Florida. While occupying part of Florida, 1819negotiations with Spain resulted in purchase for five million dollars ofoutstanding debt. Florida became a U.S. territory in 18
1 CONC 21. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Shadrack Standley is recorded several times during this period: as aland owner along the St. Mary's River 1800-1821, as a defender ofFernandina, selling property in Tattnall County, Georgia in 1813, as asoldier on the Altamaha Riv
1 CONC er in Georgia in 1814, and as a 1814 signer atFt. Mitchell in Elotchoway. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT On 30 October 1813 in Tattnall County, Georgia, Shadrack Standley,Sr. of Tattnall County to Shadrack Standley, Jr. of Tattnall County forone dollar sold 150 acres of land in Tattnall County bounded by Brunson'sline, Big Watermelon
1 CONC  Creek, Little Watermelon Creek and ShadrackStandley, Sr. Signed: Shadrick [sic] Standley. Witnesses: Burell G.Whittington and Stephen Matlock. Recorded 11 November 1911. 
1 CONT Shadrack's son Shadrack came to Fl. with the group of 101 men at Ft.Mitchell (note it is not known for sure at this time if it was Shadrackor his son Shadrack (EC) on the lake at Gainesville, Fl. The men weresuppose to be given 50
1 CONC 0 acres of land and supplies for a year. Shadrackwas a surveyer. After three months the Indians killed their leaderHarris. Spain objected and the men had to leave. The men went back toFt. St. Mary's Ga. (McLelland) This was part o
1 CONC f the Patriot War. 
1 CONT "The Republic of East Florida was established in 1811 in the vicinityof Fernandina, despite tacit disapproval by the Spanish, and aconstitution was adopted on July 17, 1812. ... " (Roberts) 
1 CONT On Jan. 16, 1814 the Patriots stopped at Payne's old town inAlachua and two days later began the construction of a fort which theynamed in honor of former Gov. Mitchell. (McLelland) It stood app. 100miles south by southwest of Col
1 CONC eraine, Ga. Within seven miles of FortMitchell lay a large lake. The block house at Ft. Mitchell was built ona prairie which was seven or eight miles wide and twenty long. (Patrick) 
1 CONT After the Patriots had established themselves on the former Indianland, they assembled on Jan. 25, 1814, as a legislative body. BucknerHarris was confirmed as "Director", Francis R. Sanchez became presidentof the legislative counc
1 CONC il and James Dell was the "Colonel Commandant" ofthe military force. The assembled Patriots declared their community acontinuation of the 1812 Rev. force and entitled their country "TheDistrict of Eloctchaway of the Republic of Ea
1 CONC st Florida". (Patrick) 
1 CONT " ... On January 25, 1814, the Republic of East Florida was ceded tothe United States at a convention held at Fort Mitchell, situated on LakeBryant, Marion County, about 18 miles east of Ocala. The fort, named forGovernor David B.
1 CONC  Mitchell of Georgia, was a two-story blockhouseerected in January 1814 by Georgia militia Colonel Buckner Harris with150 settlers to protect an American settlement in defiance of Spanishofficialdom. ..." (Roberts) Lake Bryant is 
1 CONC in the Ocala National Forest16 miles ESE of modern-day Ocala and is crossed at the nornern end bySR40. 
1 CONT "The settlement collapsed after Harris was killed by Indians and thesettlers returned to Georgia. The fort was burned shortly afterward bypossibly the same Indians." (Roberts) 
1 CONT The Patriot's were attracted to the rich soil of Alachua Co. Theybelieved this land to be the most valuable land in Fl. Compared to thenutrient-poor soils that supported the pine and wire-grass of southernGa. the productive soil a
1 CONC ttained legendary status among the Patriotfarmers. The rich soil produced luxuriant grass for cattle, wildvegtables seemed to grow in abundance, and orange trees appeared to grow"spontaneously". The county was beautifull and water
1 CONC ed by sprinklingstreams. Climate also played a factor in the minds of the settlers asthe winters were milder than in Georgia. 
1 CONT In the War of 1812, Shadrack Stanley was a private in Captain John R.Blackmon's Company, Tenth Battalion (Tattnall County), Second Brigade(General Samuel Byrne's), First Division, Georgia Militia. The last ofhis enlistments was fo
1 CONC r one month and twenty-two days from January tenthto March tenth, 1814, under command of Lieutenant Edmond Page Wester atstationed at Fort James on Milligan's Bluff Pain's Landing , on theAltamaha river , two miles above the mouth
1 CONC  of Beard's Creek , for theprotection of the frontier against Indian attack, the "frontier" beingthe Altamaha River. As a private, Shadrack's pay was 8 dollars per month. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1815 Shadrack was given 300 acres on the St. Mary's River in Flby the King of Spain. When the English took over they tried to takeShadrack's land. He went to court and won his 300 acres, waste land andthey had to give him the 5
1 CONC 00 acres promised him at Ft. Mitchell.(McLelland) 
1 CONT East Fl. was attractive to Americans. Opportunist crossed the St.Mary's River boundary at will. Due to its proximity to Ga. and theCarolinas, the impact of settlers was felt initially in the area betweenthe St. Mary's and St. John
1 CONC s Rivers. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Shadrack Standley moved to the Territory of East Florida settlingnear the St. Mary's River. Governor Coppinger ordered G. J. F. Clarke todistribute lands to all who contributed to the defense of Fernandina in1817. Shadrack Standle
1 CONC y claimed title to 300 acres of land on the St.Mary's River bounded to the north by the river, to the east by CharlesLove, and to the south and west by vacant lands. Survey made by GeorgeClarke 10 December 1817 in conformity of th
1 CONC e royal order of 29 March1815. Shadrack Standley claimed to be in actually possession of the landand was at the time of the cession and that he was a citizen of theUnited States and a resident of East Florida. This Spanish grant w
1 CONC aslater confirmed by the board of commissioners reviewing land grants whichhad existed prior to January 1818. Confirmation was given December 20,1824. In October 1828, Shadrack Standley also claimed 640 acres which helived on and 
1 CONC cultivated before and during 1818. He claimed he hadoccupied the land since 1800. He was then 21 years of age and head of afamily with 3 or 4 negroes and a number of children. The claim wasrejected as the deadline for filing had e
1 CONC xpired. 
1 CONT On 15 August 1815 in Tattnall County, Georgia, Shadrack Standley, Sr.of East Florida to Jesse and John Standley of East Florida for one dollarsold 480 acres of land in Tattnall County bounded by Stephen Matlock, P.Standley and the
1 CONC  Altamaha River. Signed: Shadh Standley. Witnesses:Stephen Matlock and Ezekl Stafford. Recorded 9 November 1911. The deedwas entered in probate on 14 March 1829. Recorded 9 March 1911. 
1 CONT On October 10, 1820 Shadrack Standley was a signer on a petition tothe Governor from "The Inhabits of the St. Mary's-St. John's District forthe Organization of a Municipality" 
1 CONT On 15 June 1822 on the St. Johns River, Florida, Shadrack Standleysigned a Memorial to Secretary of State regarding making Jacksonville aport of entry. 
1 CONT In 1825 in Alachua County, Florida, Shaderick Stanley [sic] signed apetition against the indian agent for allowing indians to roam. 
1 CONT On 2 November 1829 (the transcript has "nine" written out, buteverywhere else dates are written as 1827) in Tattnall County, Georgia,John Stanley [sic] of McIntosh County to Jesse Stanley [sic] for onedollar sold 1/2 of the parcel
1 CONC  of land in Tattnall County bounded byStephen Medlock, John Stanley and the Altamaha River. Signed: JohnStandley. Witnesses: Joshua Fletcher and Robert Berrin. Recorded datenot given. Zilpha Standley, wife of John Standley relinqu
1 CONC ished dowerrights. Recorded 9 November 1911. 
1 CONT On 29 December 1829, in Tattnall County, Georgia Jesse Standley ofTattnall County sold to Stephen Medlock for 300 dollars two tracts ofland, one of 500 acres originally granted Shadrack Standley 13 July 1807,the other of originall
1 CONC y granted Burrell Whittington dated 27 December1815. Signed: Jesse Standley. Witnesses: John Standley and (remaindernot shown on photocopy). 
1 CONT On 10 February 1828, in Alachua County, Florida, Shadrack Standleywas an administrator of the estate of William Standley, deceased. 
1 CONT The 1830 federal census of Alachua County, Florida nearCourthouse/Santa Fe River lists Shadrack Standley as head of household ofone free white male age 20-30, one free while male 70-80 years old andone free white female between 70
1 CONC -80 years old. On 25 December 1830, inAlachua County, Florida, Shadrack Standley signed a petition to thepresident to retain Governor Duval. 
1 CONT On 30 October 1837 in Tattnall County, Georgia, John Mattox ofTattnall County to Stephen Medlock of Tattnall County for 600 dollarssold 150 acres of land originally granted to Shadrack Standley, 200 acresgranted to Richard Vince, 
1 CONC 300 acres granted to John Lee, 250 acresgranted to Sands Standley all lying on the Altamaha River. Signed: JohnMattox. Witnesses: Robert Surrency and Samuel D. Surrency, J.P. Recorded11 November 1911. 
1 CONT Secondary Sources: Masters thesis of Graynella McLelland;"Encyclopedia of Historic Forts: TheMilitary, Pioneer, and Trading Postsof the United States," by Robert B. Roberts; "Florida Fiasco," by RobertW. Patrick; 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT << 1802 (1/28) Deed, Shadrack Standley sells his 1/2 interest in thenegro man Ned to William Garner and Elizabeth Garner, executor andexcutrix of John Garner, deceased. 
1 CONT << 1804 (8/1) Shadrack buys land (575 ac.) in Tattnall from McKeenGreen.>> 
1 CONT << 1804 (7/13) 500 acres granted to Shadrack in Tattnall.>>Source:Georgia indenture from Jesse Standley to Stephen Medlock. 
1 CONT << 1807 Shadrack Standley participated in the State's second land lotteryheld in 1807 as a resident of Stafford's Militia District of TattnallCounty. He was a fortunate drawer of land lot 148, district 8 ofWilkinson, now Twiggs, C
1 CONC ounty. The 1807 Land Lottery completed thesurveying and distribution of land lots in Baldwin and Wilkinsoncounties; namely districts six through twenty in Baldwin and six throughtwenty-eight in Wilkinson. The area, ceded by the Cr
1 CONC eek Nation ofIndians to the United States, opened up for white settlers a vast ovalarea between the Oconee and Ocmulgee Rivers, moving the Indian frontierfurther west. 
1 CONT Wilkinson County, Georgia, was created May 11, 1803, and took areafrom Laurens (10 December 1807), Telfair (10 December 1807) and Baldwin(23 August 1872). Wilkinson County gave area to Laurens (10 December1807), Telfair (10 Decemb
1 CONC er 1807), and Twiggs (14 December 1809), Baldwin(4 March 1856 and 23 August 1872). 
1 CONT Georgia Militia Districts were numbered consecutively, with numberone, the oldest, being in Savannah. Each district had a captain for themilitia of that district, and for a long time it was the custom to callthe districts by the n
1 CONC ame of the district captain. Each district, whencreated was supposed to contain one hundred white men subject to militaryduty, and each district had two justices of the peace. The state reliedon the militia for its military protec
1 CONC tion and the Governor wascommander-in-chief, and could order them out at any time for purpose ofquelling riots, insurrection, or Indian troubles at any point in thestate. They could not be ordered out of the state for service. Thu
1 CONC s camethe districts to be called militia districts, and while primarily formilitia purposes they were in a sense a justice district and still inanother sense an election precinct. Georgia is the only state that nowdesignates her j
1 CONC ustice districts militia districts. 
1 CONT On certain days of the year each district had its muster day;generally all the districts in a county meeting the same day at the county 
1 CONT site. Here they went through the usual military drilling and trainingfor two or three days. Such occasions, of course drew all or nearly allof the inhabitants of the county and many athletic contests and otherattractions were prov
1 CONC ided. 
1 CONT << 1813 (10/30) Shadrack Sr. of Tattnall sells 150 ac. to Shadrack Jr. ofTattnall for $1.>> 
1 CONT << 1813 (11/23) "On 23 November 1813 in Tattnall County, Georgia, JamesStafford of Camden County to Jesse Stanley for 100 dollars sold 300 acresof land in Tattnall County originally granted John Leigh and bounded onall sides by va
1 CONC cant land. Signed: James Stafford. Witnesses: JohnStandley and E___ Stafford. The deed was entered in probate on 10 May1814. Recorded 10 November 1911." 
1 CONT << 1813 (10/13) Shadrack of Tattnall sells land to Stephen Metlock for $1. 
1 CONT (Husband of Mary Stanley.) John & Jesse witness.>> 
1 CONT <<1813 (10/22) Shadrack of East Fl. sells land by Tattnall courthouse toJames Hancock.>> 
1 CONT "On 13 October 1813 in Tattnall County, Georgia, Shadrack Standley ofFlorida, formerly of Tattnall sold town lot #1 containing 3/4 acre toJames Hancock. (Reportedly in Deed Book B, page 176 according to whatappears to be a Georgia
1 CONC  Genalogical Magazine extract)" 
1 CONT <<1816 (1/1) John of Tattnall and Zilpha of McIntosh marriage license inLiberty Co. Ga. Charles Flowers of Liberty bonded with John.>>Bond istestament allowing proceeding with marriage. A separate license isissued and executed. So
1 CONC me times marriage is days or weeks later (ormaybe never happen). 
1 CONT << 1817 Shadrack contributes to the defense of Fernadina.>> 
1 CONT <<1819 (1/23) Sands sells for $30.00 to Stephen Matlock 900 acres inTattnall Co. WHO WAS THIS SANDS? Alot of acres for $30.00>> 
1 CONT Note that there is a Sands Standley as witness on the 1/14/1820indenture Jesse Standley to Joseph McClendon. 
1 CONT <<1820 (1/14) Jesse sells land in Tattnall to Joseph McClendon.>> 
1 CONT << 1821 Shadrack's land on St. Mary confirmed.>> 
1 CONT << 1822 (6/15) Shadrack signs a petition stating he is on St. Johns's.And, in 1825 in Alachua County, Florida, Shaderick Stanley [sic] signed apetition against the indian agent for allowing indians to roam. (The 
1 CONT Territorial Papers of The United States Volume XXIII The Territory ofFlorida 1824-1828, page 406.) 
1 CONT << 1825 Shadrack Standley signs a petition against the Indians. "Memorialto Congress by Citizens of Alachua Co." (Territoral Papers XXIII) 
1 CONT <<1827 (2/14) Jesse buys land from B. Whittington (250 acres)>> 
1 CONT <<1829 (11/20) John (of McIntosh) gives Jesse (of Tattnall) for $1 his1/2 of land from Shadrack.>>I think that the date is actually 20 November1827. On 2 November 1829 (the transcript has "nine" written out, buteverywhere else dat
1 CONC es are written as 1827) in Tattnall County, Georgia,John Stanley [sic] of McIntosh County to Jesse Stanley [sic] for onedollar sold 1/2 of the parcel of land in Tattnall County bounded byStephen Medlock, John Stanley and the Altam
1 CONC aha River. Signed: JohnStandley. Witnesses: Joshua Fletcher and Robert Berrin. Recorded datenot given. Zilpha Standley, wife of John Standley relinquished dowerrights. Recorded 9 November 1911. 
1 CONT <<1829 (12/29) Jesse sells land to Stephen Medlock for $300. (over 500acres) John witness.>> 
