1 CONT On 18 September 1823, "Shadrack Standly [sic] presented his memorialto this board for three hundred acres of land lying on St. Mary's river,with a plat and certificate of survey to said Standly, dated the 10th ofDecember, 1817; wh
1 CONC ich are ordered to be filed." The board members wereAlexander Hamilton and William W. Blair members of the United StatesBoard of Commisioners for East Florida. 
1 CONT On 26 April 1824, the Land Claims Board sitting in St. Augustine,Territory of Florida, called for the case of Shadrick Stanley [sic]regarding 200 acres of land owed him. His attorney was not ready fortrial, so it was postponed. On
1 CONC  21 June 1824, the Land Claims Boardcalled for the case of Shadrick Stanley regarding 300 acres of land owedhim. George J. F. Clark was examined but not enough information fordecision was provided. On 21 December 1824, the Land Cl
1 CONC aims Boardconfirmed the claim of Shadrick Standley [sic] for 300 acres of land.(The 200 acres of the 26 April 1824 entry should probably have been 300acres. - JRH) 
1 CONT On 1 January 1825, the Land Claims Board sitting in St. Augustine,Territory of Florida, published a list of claims approved by theCommissioners for the District of East Florida. Included was ShadrackStandley's claim for Florida la
1 CONC nds owed him when the area was still underSpanish rule. Claim number 118 from claimant Shadrick Stanley [sic], wasfor 300 acres originally granted him on 20 October 1817 by (SpanishGovernor - JRH) Coppinger in accordance with auth
1 CONC ority granted in 29March 1815 to those who had participated in the defense of Fernandina .The land on the St. Mary's River had been surveyed 10 December 1817 by G.J. F. Clarke (George J. F. Clarke, Surveyor General of Spanish Flor
1 CONC ida -JRH) and occupied by Shadrack from 1815 to 1824. The land was situatedon the St. Mary's River, south of the river and bounded by Charles Loveand vacant lands. 
1 CONT Instructions provided the Surveyor General, among other things,required that grants be laid out in rectangular parrallelograms, thenarrower portion front rivers, creeks and roads, to be one-third thedepth which was to extend inlan
1 CONC d. If necessary to prevent empty spaces,he was to increase the frontage and correspondingly decrease the depth. 
1 CONT On 20 January 1829, the Commissioners of the Land Office in St.Augustine published its final report on the land claims of East Florida.Included was Shadrack Standley's claim for Florida lands owed him whenthe area was still under 
1 CONC Spanish rule. Claim number 13 of Abstractnumber 6 was Shadrack Standley's claim for 640 acres of land on the St.Mary's River. The Commissioners wrote: "This would have been a goodclaim had it been filed sooner. It was presented he
1 CONC re October 1828. Theevidence is this: That claimant occupied the land on St. Mary's river inthe year 1800, and continued there until the year 1821; that he was then21 years of age, and the head of a family consisting of a number o
1 CONC fchildren and three or four negroes. The witnesses, J. D. Hart and JohnWarren, never heard of his claiming any land under the Spanish or Britishgovernment." The time for filing claims had expired 1 November 1827.(Other entries whe
1 CONC n speaking of heads of families refer to "wife" as wellas children. I conclude that Shadrack was wifeless as some point duringthe dates mentioned in this claim and others: 1800 & 1821 and 1815 & 1824-JRH) 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT At the end of 1824, Alachua County was organized as a political unitof the new Territory of Florida. The Seminole inhabitants of the Alachuaregion had recently been ordered to a reservation, and land was availablethere for white s
1 CONC ettlers. Early in 1826, a post office was establishedin the area called "Dell's P.O." It derived its name from the Dellbrothers, who had first visited the Alachua region during the "PatriotWar" (1812-1814) and had later returned t
1 CONC o settle there. The Indians wereliving south of Alachua Co. in 1826, but there were continuingdisturbances in the area. Hunger often drove the Seminoles outside thereservation for food. In 1828, the settlement near Dell's P.O. was
1 CONC officially made the Alachua County seat and named "Newnansville" in honorof a Patriot War hero, Daniel Newnan. Newnansville became the junction ofseveral important trails through frontier Florida, including the BellamyRoad, a cros
1 CONC s-Florida route authorized by Congress in 1824 as the firstfederal road in the new territory. In 1832, when Columbia County wasformed, it included Newnansville. Newnansville was returned to AlachuaCounty when the Santa Fe River be
1 CONC came the county line in 1835. Duringthe Second Seminole War (1835-1842), hundreds of displaced refugeesettlers were settled at settled at Newnansville and also at FortGilleland, a nearby military post built in 1836. 
1 CONT After the hostilities were concluded, Newnansville prospered as acommercial center for the expanding Middle Florida frontier. The chiefproducts of the area were corn, cotton, and after the Civil War, citrus.Except for a few years 
1 CONC between 1832 and 1839, Newnansville served as theAlachua County seat until 1854. In that year, the political center ofthe county was moved to the new railroad town of Gainesville. During thenext three decades, Newnansville slowly 
1 CONC declined in population andimportance. The community was dealt a final blow in 1884, when theSavannah, Florida and Western Railroad bypassed it. A new town, Alachua,grew up near that railroad. As the years passed, the residents ofN
1 CONC ewnansville moved there or elsewhere. By the 1970's only a few tracesremained of the former community, most notable of which is theNewnansville Historic Cemetery. 
1 CONT
