0 @N6000000002932572595@ NOTE William Nelson (governor) 
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia.
1 CONT
1 CONT William Nelson (1711–November 19, 1772) was an American planter and colonial leader from Yorktown, Virginia. In the interim between the royal governors Norborne Berkeley and Lord Dunmore, he served as governor of colonial Virginia
1 CONC  in 1770 and 1771.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the early stages of the American Revolution he was an active supporter of the colonial cause, and his son Thomas Nelson, Jr. went on to sign the Declaration of Independence.
0 @N6000000004270555250@ NOTE http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=:434296&id=I101
1 CONT
1 CONT # ID: I101
1 CONT # Name: Thomas NELSON , Jr.
1 CONT # Sex: M
1 CONT # Title: Sec.
1 CONT # Birth: 1716 in Yorktown, VA
1 CONT # Death: 1782 in Yorktown, VA
1 CONT # Note:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Thomas Nelson (son of the emigrant) was born at Yorktown in 1716 and died there in 1782. His education was begun in Virginia and he then went to England where he was admitted to the Inner Temple June 1, 1733, and was called to
1 CONC  the English bar June 7, 1739 (American Members of the Inns of Court, by E. Alfred Jones). He returned to Virginia and was elected a member of the House of Burgesses for York county for the session of February 1745 (succeeding his
1 CONC  brother, William, who had been appointed to the Council) and was again a member at the sessions of July 1746, March 1747, October 1748 and April 1749; was appointed to the Council in 1749 and remained a member until 1776. He succ
1 CONC eeded William Nelson as President of the Council in 1772. He was Secretary of State 1743-1776 and from his long tenure of that office was known as "Secretary Nelson."
1 CONT     His house at Yorktown was for a time Cornwallis' headquarters; but was destroyed by the bombardment. On Oct. 8, 1781, he was sent . . .?
1 CONT
1 CONT     Secreatray Thomas Nelson, (1716-86), was a patriot and his three sons, William, Thomas and John, were officers at the siege of Yorktown, and brought their father within the lines under a flag of truce. He was born and died in 
1 CONC York. Gov. Thomas Nelson, the signer, was his nephew. 
