1 CONT Hugh Nelson of Penrith, Co. Cumberland, gent. Will 13 December, 1708. Proved 16 February, 1708-9. All land and houses in Penrith unto the Reverend Richard Holme of Lowther Co. Westmoreland and Robert Wilson, Thomas Nelson and Thom
1 CONC as Fisher all of Penrith in trust for the use of my wife Sarah Nelson for life, and then I give (the Shops and Warehouses called Redmans excepted) to the said trustees for the use of my eldest son now living Thomas Nelson he to pa
1 CONC y my sons John Nelson, Hugh Nelson, and to my daughters Dorothy wife of George Wilkinson and Bridget Nelson L300. If he make default, in such case I give the whole to said trustees (except the house and shop called Redmans) for th
1 CONC e use of my son John, he to pay the said L300 If he make default, to my son Hugh. If all fail, the estate to be divided amongst them all. To my grandson William Nelson in Barbados, L20 (having formerly laid out and paid upon the a
1 CONC ccount of his father L500, to be paid when he is 21. To my grand child Sarah Wilkinson L20. To my grand child Dorothy Wilkinson L20. To my servant Samuel Denny and Ellenor Robinson 20s. each. The house & called Redmans formerly mo
1 CONC rtgaged to William Nelson my Father, and all the rest of my goods to Sons John and Hugh and Daughters Dorothy and Bridget my joint executors.
1 CONT Overseers aforesaid trustees. Witnesses: Jos. Langhorne, John Sandson, Geo. Sandson
1 CONT Consisty Carlisle Filed Will
1 CONT [Thomas Nelson, the son who is named in the will, emigrated to Virginia and settled at Yorktown. He became a man of influence and acquired a large estate. He married, 1st Margaret Reade and, 2nd Mrs. Frances Tucker nee Houston. He
1 CONC  was the father of Wm. Nelson, member Council and acting governor, and of Thomas Nelson, Secretary of State and last President of Council under Colonial regine. Thomas, son of Wm. Nelson, was the well known signer of the Declarati
1 CONC on of Independence, Governor of Virginia, and Commander of the militia of the State at the siege of Yorktown. The tomb of the emigrant remains in the churchyard at Yorktown, and bears the following arms: per pale argent and sable,
1 CONC  a chevron between three fleus-de-lis countercharged. Crest. A fleus-de-lis per pale argent and sable and the following epitaph:
1 CONT Hie Jacet
1 CONT Spe certa resurgendi in Christo
1 CONT Thomas Nelson, Generosus
1 CONT Filius Hugonis et Sariae Nelson
1 CONT de Penrith in Comitatu Cumbriae
1 CONT Natus 20'mo die Februarii Anno Domini, 1677
1 CONT Vitre bene geske finem emplevit
1 CONT 7 mo. die Octobris, 1745. Aetatis sute 68.--Eb.]
