1 CONT ================================================================
1 CONT Regarding John "The Highwayman" and other speculative facts. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Arrived on ship Forward 
1 CONT
1 CONT John was in the militia in Prince Georges County, MD in the 1730s (1734-37) as a private serving under a CPT Beall.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Land Records of Prince Georges County, MD 1726-1733 show John owing money to William Nimmo in Queen Anns Town, MD.
1 CONT
1 CONT Based on a will I found for a John Roseman in Prince George's County, MD proved in 1789, John "The Highwayman" Roseman may have lived out his life and died in PG County, MD. IF this proves to be true, our John Roseman in Virginia 
1 CONC is probably a different John Roseman.
1 CONT ==================================================================================
1 CONT Two stories exist concerning this ancestor. William Sam Rosamond (WSR) showed his name as either John or Thomas, but it was passed down to me as Thomas. That is the name I have shown on my earlier charts. According to WSR John/Tho
1 CONC mas was probably born in County Leitrim, Ireland but he gives no date to his birth. 
1 CONT
1 CONT WSR has John/Thomas coming from Ireland to South Carolina in 1740 with his wife and 2 year old son Samuel. He places his death prior to 1790 based on the fact that John/Thomas was not listed in the first US census done in that yea
1 CONC r, while two of his sons are listed.
1 CONT
1 CONT One of our current group of researchers located information regarding a John Rosamond that was sent to the colonies as an indentured servant, arriving in Anapolis, Maryland in 1725. His indentured status was due to the fact that h
1 CONC e had committed highway robbery in England. Since the penalty for this was death, unless the perpetrator was a minor, it is believed that John/Thomas was probably in his early or mid-teens at the time of commision of his crime. Ba
1 CONC sed on this, I place his birth around the year 1710, which would make him around fifteen years old when he arrived in Maryland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Based on the ships records which brought him to the US, we now believe that his name was John, and that Thomas was really his father-in-law, Thomas Wilson. John arrived in Annapolis aboard the ship 'Forward' in December 1725. He w
1 CONC as tried in Berkshire, England, Oxford Circuit Court. The ship was owned by Johathon Forward who was a contractor transporting prisoners for England to their colonies. Ruth Menhel, another of our current researchers, shows him loc
1 CONC ated in Prince George's County Maryland as a Corporal in some sort of military organization. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Researchers assumed that John relocated to Virginia where he married Sarah Wilson. Barbara Morgan located ship records showing that Sarah arrived in Virginia with her Mother and sisters in 1740. I am unable to locate a copy of the
1 CONC se records and cannot get in touch with Barbara. This could be where WSR erroneously picked up that year for Thomas' arrival in the colonies. Thomas Wilson was Sarah's father, and had been in Virginia since 1737. John and Sarah re
1 CONC located their family to the Abbeville District of South Carolina no later than 1765.
1 CONT
1 CONT The first instance of John being in Virginia is from Chalkley's Chronicles showing hin in Augusta County in 1747. Then in 1765, there is a record in Chalkley's that says the property he and Sarah owned was sold to them by a man wh
1 CONC o didn't own the property himself. This apparently caused a problem, because the final record in Chalkley's shows them selling the land to someone else with the courts permission. The next record of him is a land grant dated 1767 
1 CONC in Abbeville District, SC. That dates their move to SC between 1765 and 1767.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was a master shoemaker in Augusta County, VA in the 1750s. This is documented in Chalkleys.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Land Records of Prince Georges County, MD 1726-1733 show John owing money to William Nimmo in Queen Anns Town, MD.
1 CONT
1 CONT Gwen shows John's middle name as Baptiste. This may come from the English records that Barbara found. I have some doubts about this due to the dates.
1 CONT
1 CONT  ca 1740 when John was 30, he married Sarah Wilson, W425, F, daughter of Thomas Willson, M & Elizabeth Dinwiddie, F, in Augusta County, Virginia. Born  ca 1726 in County Antrim, Ireland. Immigrated in 1740 to Virginia. Sarah died 
1 CONC in South Carolina  bef 1790, she was 64.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sarah Wilson arrived in the English colonies in 1740 with her sisters and her mother. Her father, Thomas Wilson, was already in Augusta County, Virginia. She married John Rosamond in Augusta County ca. 1747, and their family reloc
1 CONC ated to the Abbeville District of South Carolina sometime around 1765.
0 @N6000000003263299976@ NOTE Based on his mother not arriving in VA until 1740.
1 CONT Location near this town in Anderson District
1 CONT
1 CONT Samuel Null Rosamond, R255, M. Born  ca 1751 in Augusta County, Virginia. Samuel Null died in Anderson District, SC on 11 Aug 1814, he was 63. Buried in Williamston, SC. Occupation: Farmer, Soldier.
1 CONT
1 CONT Again there is some controversy regarding Captain Samuel Rosamond. William Sam Rosamond shows him as being born in County Leitrim, Ireland arouund 1738. This is based on the supposistion that his father John came to the US in 1740
1 CONC . If we accept that John arrived in 1725, and Samuel's mother, Sarah Wilson, didn't come to Virginia until 1740, Samuel would have to have been born after 1740. Barbara Morgan estimates that he was born sometime between 1745 and 1
1 CONC 755. If we assume that John and Sarah were married ca. 1749, 1750 would be an approximate date for his birth.
1 CONT
1 CONT After that things are a little clearer. We know that Samuel grew up in the Abbeville District, SC. He may have been married more than once as his will mentions a daughter Polly (Mary) that was not the daughter of his wife Sarah Ho
1 CONC dges. His wife Sarah was from a neighboring plantation and she was 10-15 years younger than Samuel. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Samuel enlisted in the militia around 1776-77 and served as a Lieutenant under Captain Adam Crain Jones and Colonel Robert Anderson (for whom Anderson County, SC was named.)  In 1782 he was appointed Captain and served at the Sieg
1 CONC e of Ninety-Six and the Battle of Kettle Creek in Wilkes County, GA on Feb. 14, 1779 during the Revolutionary War. This battle enabled the revolutionists to halt the British advance in Georgia after the capture of Savannah. Accord
1 CONC ing to Samuel's great-grandson James Oliver Rosamond, Samuel served as a scout and spy under the direction of Colonel Francis Mariion, the "Swamp Fox".
1 CONT
1 CONT A copy of Samuel's will is contained in another web page on this site at http://rosamond.ourfamily.com/samswill.htm.
1 CONT
1 CONT Several women have joined the DAR based on the Revolutionary War record of Capt. Samuel Rosamond: Mrs. Josie Dean Rosamond HILBUN (275253); Miss Nannie SULLIVANT;  Mrs. Molly Rosamond SULLIVANT; Mrs. Mary Rosamond RHYNE (251829); 
1 CONC Mrs. Allison Sullivant GUYTON (254893) - - - From 1965 DAR book.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Feb 1785 when Samuel Null was 34, he married Sarah (Salley) Hodges, H322, F, daughter of Richard Hodges, M & Elizabeth (Betty) Jones, F, in South Carolina. Born  ca 1765 in South Carolina. Sarah (Salley) died in SC or MS on 24 
1 CONC Apr 1844, she was 79.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ruth Menhel noted that Sarah's father also served in the Revolutionary War. It is uncertain whether Sarah died in SC, or if she traveled to Missisiippi with the family when they migrated in the early 1800s.
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT WILL OF
1 CONT SAMUEL ROSAMOND
1 CONT
1 CONT State of South Carolina, Pendleton District
1 CONT
1 CONT In the name of God, Amen.  I, Samuel Rosamond, being in health and of a disposing mind, do make this my last will and testament and desire it may be received as such by all whom it may concern.
1 CONT
1 CONT First, I do will and positively order that all my just debts be paid.
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondly, that the plantation whereon I now live consisting of three small tracts with the mill thereon be sold as soon as conveniency will admit of by my executors hereafter mentioned and titles made to the same and that a planta
1 CONC tion or tract of land at not more than One Thousand Dollars price be purchased by them for my wife and family to live on, either in Abbeville or Pendleton Districts, convenient to some place of public worship such as they make cho
1 CONC ice of , and at my wife’s death or a second marriage to fall to my male heirs.  Notwithstanding,  if my wife and family should incline to move to some other place, they are hereby empowered to sell said land and purchase other lan
1 CONC d for the same purpose in any other place in this state or in any of the United States out of the monies arising from the sale of such land and to receive titles to it for the above mentioned purchase.
1 CONT
1 CONT I will and order as soon as conveniency will allow my three Negro women, Teeney, Tonny, and Sign, to be sold and that three other young wenches be purchased in their places at not more than Twelve Hundred Dollars price, and said N
1 CONC egroes and their increase, if any, shall remain with my wife and family that remains with her and to be under her direction for her benefit and the benefit of the family that remains with her.  Notwithstanding, it is put in the po
1 CONC wer of my wife and executors, hereby, to dispose of  to my children as they marry or come of age a certain portion of the property that is with the family so that  that portion is not more than their equal divide of such property.
1 CONC
1 CONT
1 CONT As to Negro Peter, I allow to be sold or to remain with family as my wife and executors shall find to answer best.  If sold the monies arising from his sale with the other money belonging to the estate to be put to interest and to
1 CONC  continue at interest until five of my children are come of age or married and has issue.  Then the half of  said money to be divided among the five and the remainder of the money to remain at interest until the remaining children
1 CONC  shall arrive at such state as the first five when it shall be equally divided amongst the minor children.
1 CONT
1 CONT And whereas, Negro Will, by his late mistress' will, at my death has the privilege of choosing his master or mistress amongst my children.  When he has made his choice and that one child has confirmed his choice, he shall then be 
1 CONC valued by Sovorin appraisers, and that child shall take him as so much of their lawful divide, and whereas William Pyle and his wife was given their choice of the two Negro girls that they have yet the same privilege allowed them,
1 CONC  the other girl shall be accounted as part of the undivided estate.  
1 CONT
1 CONT I will and order to be sold one horse wagon and my two stills, my desk and folding table with some other small articles, my stock of cows and hogs with three horse creations, Snip Tomeny and a yard filly, to remain with the family
1 CONC  for their use, my books (Scots Family Bible excepted which is to remain with the family) to be divided into lots for my children and the eldest to have the first choice and so on to the last. The land and negroes with other thing
1 CONC s ordered to be sold upon a credit of two years paying interest for the last year.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT WILL OF SAMUEL ROSAMOND - PAGE 2 -
1 CONT
1 CONT I will that every one of my children shall have at my wife’s death their part as the law in such case has provided except the land mentioned in page first of this my will.
1 CONT
1 CONT I hereby empower my executors to make titles to Robert Young for two hundred and seventy nine acres of land sold to him in Abbeville, where I formerly lived.  Upon his paying up, the purchase money according to bargain, I give to 
1 CONC my two executors, hereafter, named upon condition of their both acting the sum of Twenty Five Dollars more than commission. 
1 CONT
1 CONT  I do hereby appoint and ordain my son-in-law, William Pyles, of Abbeville, and Robert McCan, Esquire, of Pendleton my executors in witness whereof, I hereby do hereunto set my hand and seal this Second Day of September and in the
1 CONC  Year of Our Lord One Thousand Eight Hundred and Eight.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Witness:   John WestfieldSaml Rosmamond (SEAL)
