0 @N6000000003263421046@ NOTE Sergeant James "Jacob?" Rosemond, R255, M. Born on 1 Jan 1654 in Basle, Switzerland.
1 CONT
1 CONT DISCLAIMER:
1 CONT THIS IS THE SERGEANT ROSEMOND THAT THE SOUTHERN US ROSAMONDS ARE SUPPOSED TO BE DESCENDED FROM. DESPITE FAMILY LEGEND THAT SAYS SGT. ROSEMOND WAS AN ANCESTOR, THERE IS CURRENTLY NO PROOF THAT THIS SERGEANT ROSAMOND IS THE FATHER, 
1 CONC OR EVEN RELATED TO, JOHN ROSEMAN OF VIRGINIA WHO MARRIED SARAH WILLSON IN AUGUSTA COUNTY VIRGINIA, AND IS THE EARLIEST KNOWN ANCESTOR OF THE SOUTHERN ROSAMONDS IN THE US.
1 CONT
1 CONT Despite any personal doubts about our connection to Sgt. James Rosemond, and the doubts of several of my Rosamond research cousins, I am carrying this Sergeant James Rosemond as our ancestor, if for no other reason that to preserv
1 CONC e the data about him. He is believed to have been a Huguenot soldier that left France at the time of the Edict of Nantes, and went to Holland, and then to Ireland where he finally settled in County Leitrim, the birthplace of the p
1 CONC arents of Mary Jane Loya. Mary Jane's mother is an Irish Rosamond.
1 CONT
1 CONT With regards to John and Sarah Roseman, their descendants throughout the Southern United States use the spelling Rosamond. The book from which the ancestry of Sergeant James Rosamond is taken said:
1 CONT
1 CONT "In the Southern states among those identified with our line in Ireland, the form "Rosamond" prevails as it does in England and Canada..."
1 CONT
1 CONT This would indicate that the Southern Rosamonds are from the same line, but again no documented proof has been found. One other doucment references Huguenot ancestry, and that is a newspaper article from Alabama concerning William
1 CONC  Capers Rosamond who died in 1903. The article was written during his lifetime, i.e. before 1903, and it references his Huguenot ancestry and the Edict of Nantes. It also mentions the family came from Virginia, which was not in th
1 CONC e records of William Sam Rosamond. So, the family believed in the Huguenot ancestry just 3-4 generations
1 CONT
1 CONT THE ROSAMOND BOOK  BY LELAND EUGENE ROSEMOND DOES REFERENCE BENNETT ROSAMOND WHICH TIES THE FAMILY OF MARY JANE LOYA DIRECTLY TO THIS SERGEANT ROSAMOND.
1 CONT
1 CONT I have copied this ancestry into the file showing the family tree of Mary Jane Loya. This genealogy appears to be valid for that line of the family.
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT The followng information is from Jane Chambers in England and comes from a letter she sent to her cousin Mary Jane Loya in California. The information is from a trip to Ireland Jane took in late 2007.
1 CONT
1 CONT Now to come to the family history.  I must point out some misunderstandings.
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.    James Rosamond wasn't knighted as far as I know.  He was a Sargeant at Arms.  I don't know where the heroism comes in either.  
1 CONT
1 CONT 2.    Dates also are a mystery as the Coat of Arms says Newtown Gore 1688, which was 2 years before the Battle of the Boyne and I don't think William of Orange had reached Ireland by then.  We went to Newtown Gore's small museum b
1 CONC ut there was no trace of any Rosamonds who have ever lived there.
1 CONT
1 CONT 3.    The Coat of Arms which Pop sent me is not the original but a copy which I believe was sent to him by a Mr Rosamond in Flushing, Holland.  Pop wrote to me when I was 14 that he was in touch with Mr Rosamond and could give me 
1 CONC his address.  How I wish I had followed it all up then.  I was able to piece together a lot of our family legend with the Leland Rosemond document of 1935 which Jimmy Rosamond sent me, which I am so grateful for.  Peter Rosamond o
1 CONC f Flushing was mentioned in that on pg 4.
1 CONT
1 CONT Delving into it all did bring some unsettling information.  For instance, I obtained a copy of Grandpop's birth certificate which the clerk pointed out to me was dated 19 March 1947 and was registered by his mother Elizabeth then.
1 CONC   The Clerk was curious as to why he didn't do this himself (as Elizabeth would have been 87 years old).  We realised that when emigrating to America in 1947, he would have needed his birth certificate so would have had to ask his
1 CONC  mother to obtain it and send it to England.  How sad it is that our parents did not know that they had a grandmother in Ireland who only died in 1953 - your mom would have been 28 and my dad 31.  When we saw the cottage and it's 
1 CONC surroundings, although beautiful, she would have been very isolated.  However, according to the local store, she may have had Grandpop's brother Sam living there with his wife.  They certainly lived there after she died.
1 CONT
1 CONT The other aspect is religion.  The area where the Irish Rosamonds  settled in the 17th century must have been predominantly Protestant due to William winning the war with the Catholic King James ll of England BUT when Grandpop was
1 CONC  growing up the family living in Lough Rynn, Mohil would have been very much in the minority as Protestants in a very Catholic area.  We noticed that all the Protestant Churches of Ireland were closed or derelict but the Catholic 
1 CONC churches were flourishing with big congregations.  One local said that the parish Priest, although Catholic, did know the Protestant families and would help if he could.  There is a family of Rosamonds about 3 miles from Mohil but
1 CONC  when I phoned them, they were widows of Rosamonds and said they didn't think they were related to the ones who had lived in Mohil.  I suppose they could have been perhaps second cousins of Grandpop, descending from James & Nancy 
1 CONC or Bennet & Fanny.
1 CONT
1 CONT I do wonder how Grandpop and Nanny met.   It seemed that Grandpop must have been ostracised by his family for marrying a Catholic girl.  Am I right that Nanny was born in Cork and brought up in a convent?  Dad said that his parent
1 CONC s never really spoke about their backgrounds although Dad did know he had an uncle Sam and an uncle Robert.  Does your mom know anything about Nanny's parents, was she really an orphan?  
1 CONT
1 CONT Well, I have chatted on quite a bit about the Rosamonds.
