1 CONT "Thomas Stribling Rosamond presented a copy of the last will and testament of Richard Rosamond, deceased; the original been destroyed by fire (courthouse fire) on the 28th of July, 1858."
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Probate Book, the will named Sally Rosamond, his mother, and T. S. Rosamond, his son, as executors. He named one son, James B. Rosamond to inherit certain lands in Choctaw and Attala Counties. Other children were 
1 CONC not mentioned by name. The will was written in July 1855, filed Sept. 16, 1861 and recorded Nov. 28, 1961.Ruth Menhel obtained a copy of the will and it lists Rischard's son Samuel Milton Rosamond as executor, not Sally; and the l
1 CONC ands referenced were in Calhoun and Attala Counties, nothing in Choctaw County.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard is listed in the church roll for the Shady Grove Methodist Church in Attala County, MS in 1854.
1 CONT
1 CONT On 29 Jan 1818 when Richard was 22, he married Mary H. "Polly" Stribling, S361, F, daughter of Thomas Striblinski, M. Born on 29 Jan 1799 in South Carolina. Mary H. "Polly" died on 11 Jan 1855, she was 55.
0 @N6000000003263301819@ NOTE Rosamond Family Burial Grounds
1 CONT
1 CONT Addison Rosamond Sr., R255, M. Born on 16 Jan 1803 in Abbeville County, SC. Addison died in Mississippi on 19 Jan 1873, he was 70. Buried in Winston County, MS.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ancestor of Sarah Ann Rosamond Presley, Preston, MS.
1 CONT
1 CONT From Tomb Records of Winston County, MS by Josie Worth Holman:
1 CONT "This little story was given to us by one of the descendants. Mr. Addison Rosamond requested before he died that his body be buried in his shop, which was located back of the house. This request was carried out, thus starting the 
1 CONC family graveyard. It is in a beautiful location, but as time goes on it will be neglected, as so many other private graveyards are in the country. There is no road up to it at this tine."
1 CONT
1 CONT To reach the Rosamond family burial ground, go down the DeKalb Highway about 20 miles, and turn right about a mile before reaching the county line. It is a few hundred yards off the highway. Records copied in 1963 show 16 marked g
1 CONC raves and 2 unmarked graves.
1 CONT
1 CONT Addison married Mary Elizabeth Ramsey, R520, F. Born on 12 Sep 1824 in Alabama. Mary Elizabeth died in Mississippi on 29 Jun 1893, she was 68. Buried in Winston County, MS.
0 @N6000000003263304144@ NOTE James Rosamond, R255, M. Born  ca 1807 in South Carolina. Occupation: Minister. Religion: Presbyterian.
1 CONT
1 CONT Based on an 1840 census, James and Elsa first settled in Tishomingo County, MS. They were in Walton County, Ga between 1843-1845.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is an excerpt from the "Kosciusko-Attala County History Book":
1 CONT "After the election of 1855 politics simmered down and attention was given to the opening of schools in the area. The Masonic orders had both a male and a female academy in Kosciusko as early as 1850 ...
1 CONT
1 CONT The Female College, as it was called, was not so ambitious in it tutorial offerings but rather advertised that the institution's music and ornamental departments would be thourough and scientific. This school was under the supervi
1 CONC sion of Rev. James Rosamond, assisted by two young ladies. There was an elementary department but higher courses were only offered in music and fancy needlework."
1 CONT
1 CONT William Sam Rosamond notes that Rev. James Rosamond was "President of one of the church schools many years."
1 CONT
1 CONT On 26 Nov 1839 when James was 32, he married Elsa Taylor, T460, F, daughter of John Taylor, M & Mary Knott, F, in South Carolina. Born on 24 Nov 1814.
1 CONT
1 CONT Controversy surrounding exact given name of wife of Rev. James Rosamond. Joyce Sanders lists her as "Tabitha". Josie Dean Rosamond Hilbun's DAR application papers lsits her as Elsie Taylor. Goodspeed's "Memoirs of Mississippi" lis
1 CONC ts her on page 881 as Elsa Taylor, 9th child of John and Mary (Knott) Taylor.
